window.RV_LOADED['ib-biology-sl'] = {"name":"IB Biology SL","topics":[{"name":"Unity & Diversity","subtopics":[{"name":"Cell Structure","questions":[{"ref":"BI0001","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0001\n\nWhich of the following statements about cells are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | All living cells contain cytoplasm enclosed by the plasma membrane. |\n  | II. | Microscopes have helped scientists understand cell structure. |\n  | III. | Prokaryotes do not have genetic material. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because living cells have a plasma membrane that encloses the cytoplasm and separates the cell's internal contents from its surroundings.\n\n**Statement II** is true because microscopes allow scientists to observe cells and cellular structures that are too small to distinguish with the naked eye, helping them understand cell structure.\n\n**Statement III** is false because prokaryotic cells contain DNA as their genetic material. Their chromosomal DNA is located in the nucleoid region of the cytoplasm rather than enclosed within a nucleus. Prokaryotes lack a nucleus, not genetic material.\n\n**Choice A** is therefore correct, as it includes only the true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they include one or more incorrect statements."},{"ref":"BI0002","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0002\n\n*Paramecium* produces enzymes that catalyse different chemical reactions taking place in the cytoplasm. What function of life is demonstrated in this situation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Excretion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Homeostasis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reproduction\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metabolism","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** - Excretion is the removal of metabolic wastes from the organism. Paramecia expel waste products such as carbon dioxide through the process of diffusion.\n\n**Choice B** - Homeostasis pertains to the ability of living things to maintain a stable internal environment. Paramecia maintain a stable internal condition by expelling excess water using the contractile vacuole.\n\n**Choice C** -   Reproduction is the biological process by which organisms produce offspring that are biologically similar to themselves. Paramecia can reproduce sexually through meiosis and asexually through mitosis.\n\n**Choice D** - Metabolism refers to all the chemical reactions in organisms such as anabolic and catabolic reactions. Paramecia produce enzymes that catalyse different catabolic and anabolic reactions in the cell."},{"ref":"BI0009","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0009\n\nThe photomicrograph shows *Chlorella*, the cells of which contain chlorophyll.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\nMagnification: 100 x $\\hspace{3em}$Scale bar = 10 µm\n\n[Source: Adapted from User: NEON. (2014). *Chlorella vulgaris Beijerinck Nies-2170 / Olympus IX71+DP72.* Wikimedia Common. Retrieved April 6, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chlorella_vulgaris_NIES2170.jpg. Copyright by CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\nWhich statement best explains how chlorophyll supports a function of life?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It catalyses the breakdown of glucose into ATP.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  It absorbs light energy needed to convert inorganic molecules into organic molecules.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It transports carbon dioxide into the chloroplast for glucose synthesis.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It stores the energy released during cellular respiration.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** accurately describes chlorophyll’s role in photosynthesis, a process that supports the life function of nutrition in autotrophic organisms (like plants and some unicellular organisms). Chlorophyll absorbs light energy, which is used to convert inorganic carbon dioxide and water into the organic compound glucose.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes cellular respiration, not the function of chlorophyll.\nThe breakdown of glucose into ATP happens in the mitochondria and does not use chlorophyll.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because chlorophyll does not transport carbon dioxide; CO₂ enters the leaf via stomata and diffuses into chloroplasts. Chlorophyll’s role is to absorb light, not transport gases.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because chlorophyll is involved in capturing light energy, not in storing energy from respiration. Energy from respiration is stored in ATP, not in chlorophyll."},{"ref":"BI0189","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0189\n\nWhich row correctly describes the DNA and chromosomes of Eubacteria cells?\n\n| | Shape | DNA with histones | Number of chromosomes |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Circular | Yes | More than one |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear | Yes | More than one |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Circular | No | One |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear | No | One |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEubacteria have a single chromosome composed of DNA and not associated with histones(**Choice C**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because prokaryotes' DNA is not associated with histones and they only have one chromosome. **Choice B** is incorrect because prokaryotes do not have linear DNA, their DNA is not associated with histones, and they do not have more than one chromosome. **Choice D** is incorrect because prokaryotic chromosomes are not linear."},{"ref":"BI0198","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0198\n\nWhich statement about eukaryotic cells is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic chromosomes are circular and the DNA is associated with histone proteins\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells have looped DNA molecules which are not associated with proteins in the nucleus\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells have linear DNA molecules associated with proteins\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells have linear DNA molecules that are not associated with histone proteins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe chromosomes of eukaryotes are linear, and the DNA is coiled around histone proteins (**Choice C**). **Choice A** is incorrect because eukaryotic DNA molecules are not circular. **Choice B**  is incorrect because eukaryotic cells do not have looped DNA in the nucleus. **Choice D**  is incorrect because the linear DNA molecules in eukaryotic cells are coiled around histone proteins."},{"ref":"BI0199","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0199\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nAll organisms contain genetic material. The type, quantity and location of genetic material differ between kingdoms and species.\n\n1. State if the cell shown in the image is a prokaryotic or eukaryotic cell. Give a reason for your answer. [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0199.png\" alt=\"BI0199\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Ruiz (16th January 2007). *File: Prokaryote cell diagram international.svg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 18th May 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Prokaryote_cell_diagram_international.svg. Copyright-free]\n\n2. Distinguish between prokaryotic and eukaryotic DNA. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cell **BECAUSE** chromosome is looped **OR** no nucleus is present     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    This is a **prokaryotic**($\\checkmark$) cell because the chromosome is **not within a nucleus**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is looped, **BUT** the majority of eukaryotic DNA is linear  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is not contained in a nucleus, **BUT** the majority of eukaryotic DNA is found within a nucleus     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is found in a chromosome and plasmids **WHEREAS** the majority of eukaryotic DNA is found in chromosomes and not in plasmids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the majority of eukaryotic DNA is always associated with proteins (histones) **WHILE**  prokaryotic DNA is not bound to proteins (with the exception of *Archae* whose DNA is associated with proteins)  [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nProkaryotic DNA is looped while the majority of eukaryotic **DNA is linear**($\\checkmark$). DNA can be found in **plasmids in some prokaryotes**($\\checkmark$), while the only plasmids found in eukaryotes are present in mitochondria and chloroplasts."},{"ref":"BI0390","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0390\n\nWhich row in the table correctly identifies structures which are present ($\\checkmark$) or absent ($\\times$)  in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | Structure | Prokaryotic cells | Eukaryotic cells |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| 80S ribosomes | $\\checkmark$ | $\\times$ |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plasma membrane | $\\times$ | $\\checkmark$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Nucleus | $\\checkmark$| $\\times$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mitochondria | $\\times$ | $\\checkmark$|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotic cells do not contain mitochondria but eukaryotic cells do. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as eukaryotes (as well as *Archae*) contain 80S ribosomes. **Choice B** is incorrect as both eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells contain a plasma membrane. **Choice C** is incorrect as eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus but prokaryotic cells do not."},{"ref":"BI0391","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0391\n\nThese photographs show the same section of a human retina. What is the reason for the difference between the two diagrams?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0391_1.2.png\" alt=\"BI0391_1.2\">\n[Source: Nestl, A. (2012, January 17). *File:Opthalmology AMD Super resolution Cremer.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 16, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Opthalmology_AMD_Super_Resolution_Cremer.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right was taken using an electron microscope.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right is at a higher resolution.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right is at a higher magnification.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right shows the ultrastructure of the cells.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe resolution is the ability to distinguish between two objects. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because a photograph taken using an electron microscope would allow the ultrastructure of the cells (fine detail of cells, such as organelles) to be observed. **Choice C** is incorrect because these photographs are magnified at the same power."},{"ref":"BI0392","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0392\n\nWhat type of cell is shown in the image?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0392ii.png\" alt=\"BI0392ii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg  Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A eukaryotic cell because it contains mitochondria\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A prokaryotic cell because it has a plasma membrane\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A eukaryotic cell because it does not have a nucleus\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ A prokaryotic cell because it contains ribosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEukaryotic cells contain mitochondria but prokaryotic cells do not. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as the cell is not prokaryotic; it has a nucleus. **Choice C** is incorrect because the cell has a nucleus."},{"ref":"BI0394","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0394\n\nWhich statement(s) is/are **not** true of eukaryotic or prokaryotic cells?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Prokaryotic cells have a compartmentalised structure.\n\n- II.$\\hspace{.8em}$ Only eukaryotic cells have a Golgi apparatus.\n\n- III.$\\hspace{.6em}$ Only eukaryotic cells have membrane-bound organelles.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is **NOT** true of prokaryotic cells because they do not have compartmentalised structures. Compartmentalisation is a defining feature of eukaryotic cells, allowing them to carry out more complex and specialised functions.\n\n**Statement II** is true of prokaryotic cells because they do not have a Golgi apparatus. The Golgi apparatus is a membrane-bound organelle, and only eukaryotic cells possess it.\n\n**Statement III** is true because membrane-bound organelles is a defining feature of eukaryotic cells. Prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles entirely.\n\n**Choice B** is the correct answer as it only includes **Statement I**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement II**, **Statement III** or both."},{"ref":"BI0395","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0395\n\nWhich part of the cell is labelled with the letter P?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0395%20image.png\" alt=\"BI0395 image\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Vacuole\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Lysosome\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Nucleoid\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Nucleus","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as the photo shows an animal cell, which can contain smaller, single membraned vacuoles, but the nucleus is much larger. **Choice B** is incorrect as lysosomes are smaller and have a single membrane. **Choice C** is incorrect as nucleoids are only present in prokaryotes and are not membrane bound."},{"ref":"BI0397","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0397\n\nWhat do the two labelled organelles have in common?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0397i_1.2.png\" alt=\"BI0397i_1.2\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Electron_microscope_images Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ They have a single outer membrane\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    They contain cristae\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    They are surrounded by a double membrane\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    They contain starch grains","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nX is pointing to the nucleus and Y is pointing to a mitochondrion. **Choice A** is incorrect as neither organelle has a single membrane. **Choice B** is incorrect as only mitochondria contain cristae, the nucleus does not. **Choice D** is incorrect as neither the nucleus or mitochondria contain starch grains."},{"ref":"BI0398","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0398\n\nWhich organelle is the site of protein synthesis?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_A.png\" alt=\"BI0398_A\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_B.png\" alt=\"BI0398_B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_C.png\" alt=\"BI0398_C\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_D.png\" alt=\"BI0398_D\">|\n\n[Source A: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0.\nSource B: Adapted from: Openstax. (2016, May 18). *File:0313 endoplasmic reticulum B en.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0313_Endoplasmic_Reticulum_b_en.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0. \\\nSource C: and3k, & caper437. (2009, June 25). *File: chloroplast in leaf of anemone sp Tem 85000x.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_85000x.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0 \\\nSource D: Murphy, F. (2016, August 10). *Free picture: Thin, section, ebola, virus*. PIXNIO. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://pixnio.com/science/microscopy-images/ebola-hemorrhagic-fever-virus/thin-section-containing-the-ebola-virus Copyright free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it shows the rough endoplasmic reticulum, which has bound ribosomes that synthesise proteins.\n\n**Choice A** shows the nucleus which contains the genetic information and controls the activity of the cell. **Choice C** shows a chloroplast, the site of photosynthesis. **Choice D** indicates the golgi apparatus which packages and transports proteins."},{"ref":"BI0399","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0399\n\nWhich organelle has a double membrane?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Golgi apparatus\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Nucleus\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Vacuole","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe nucleus is the only organelle among the options listed that has a double membrane. The nuclear envelope consists of two lipid bilayers: the inner membrane and the outer membrane. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **D** are incorrect as they all have a single membrane."},{"ref":"BI0400","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0400\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe photomicrograph below shows a cyanobacterium, *Synechococcus elongatus*.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/nucleoid%20region%20cyanobacter.png\" alt=\"nucleoid region cyanobacter\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gonzalez, R., & Kerfeld, C. (2022, June 13). *Synechococcus elongates*. Wikipedia. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synechococcus_elongatus Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1.\n    1. State whether the cell shown in the photomicrograph is eukaryotic or prokaryotic. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State **two** features found in **both** eukaryotic and prokaryotic cell structures. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Identify the type of microscope which was used to produce the microphotograph above. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n    4. Describe the function of X. [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure of the Golgi apparatus and rough endoplasmic reticulum. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (cell is) prokaryotic **OR** is a prokaryote [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (because it) does not have a nucleus **OR** has nucleoid **OR** no membrane-bound organelles **OR** a named organelle such as a Golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum or mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe cell is **prokaryotic** ($\\checkmark$) because it doesn’t have a **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) or **membrane bound organelles**.\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have a plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have free ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** eukaryotic plant cells and prokaryotes have cell walls [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nEukaryotic and prokaryotic cells both have **a plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$) and **free ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscope [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (because it has) higher resolution **OR** ultrastructure **OR** internal structure of the cell is visible  [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIt is an **electron microscope** ($\\checkmark$), as the image is at a **high resolution** ($\\checkmark$) where you can clearly see the **ultrastructure** of the cell.\n\n    4.    any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contains DNA **OR** the genetic information of the cell  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA controls the activities of the cell **OR** carries instructions for protein synthesis **OR** site of transcription     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ region where DNA replication occurs    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nX is the **nucleoid region** ($\\checkmark$), it **contains the cell’s DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    *One difference and one similarity are needed for full marks*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** consist of flattened membrane areas                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum has ribosomes bound to it **WHEREAS**  the Golgi apparatus doesn’t [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus has shorter cisternae **THAN** rough endoplasmic reticulum  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ part of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is continuous with the nuclear membrane **WHEREAS** the Golgi apparatus is not  [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe Golgi apparatus and rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) are both made up of **flattened membrane areas** ($\\checkmark$), however the RER has ribosomes bound to the outside of it, whereas the Golgi apparatus **does not** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the Golgi apparatus has **shorter cisternae** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0492","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0492\n\nWhich structure is found in both eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lysosome\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosome\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Golgi apparatus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleoid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUnlike eukaryotes, prokaryotes lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. However, like eukaryotes, prokaryotes contain ribosomes, a non-membrane-bound organelle required for protein synthesis. So, ribosomes are found in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes.\n\nLysosomes (**Choice A**) and the Golgi apparatus (**Choice C**) are only found in eukaryotes. The nucleoid (**Choice D**) is only found in prokaryotes."},{"ref":"BI0494","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0494\n\nThe electron micrograph below shows a cell of the bacterium *Vibrio cholerae*. Which of the labelled structures maintains the cell’s shape and protects the cell from lysing during osmosis?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0494.png\" alt=\"BI0494\">\n[Source: Blokesch, M. & Schoolnik, G.K. (2007). *Serogroup Conversion of Vibrio cholerae in Aquatic Reservoirs*. PLoS Pathog 3(6): e81. Retrieved March 15, 2022, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.ppat.0030081.Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe cell membrane of prokaryotes is surrounded by a rigid cell wall. The cell wall provides shape and structure to the organism. It also prevents the cell from bursting when the cell takes up water through osmosis.  The cell wall is located between the cell membrane and the capsule, a sticky outer layer of polysaccharide. The cell wall is shown in the image below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0494A.png\" alt=\"BI0494A\">"},{"ref":"BI0531","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0531\n\nWhich structure found in a eukaryote has a double membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Golgi apparatus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondrion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lysosome\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endoplasmic reticulum","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOrganelles of a eukaryotic cell are enclosed by a single or double membrane. Double membrane-bound organelles include the nucleus, mitochondria, and chloroplasts. So, a mitochondrion has a double membrane.\n\nThe Golgi apparatus (**Choice A**), lysosome (**Choice C**), and endoplasmic reticulum (**Choice D**) all have a single membrane."},{"ref":"BI0532","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0532\n\nThe following electron micrograph shows part of an exocrine pancreas cell. Which of the labelled structures is responsible for the production of vesicles for exocytosis?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0532.png\" alt=\"BI0532\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L.  (n.d.). *File: Pancreatic cells - TEM.jpg*. Wikipedia.  Retrieved January, 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Pancreatic_cells_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Golgi apparatus produces vesicles that contain substances destined for exocytosis from the cell. The Golgi apparatus is composed of stacks of flattened pouches called cisternae. These appear in a micrograph as the stacked, flattened discs indicated by **Choice C**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0532A.png\" alt=\"BI0532A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L.  (n.d.). *File: Pancreatic cells - TEM.jpg*. Wikipedia.  Retrieved January, 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Pancreatic_cells_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n**Choice A** is the plasma membrane, **Choice B** is a lysosome and **Choice D** is the nucleus."},{"ref":"BI0534","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0534\n\nWhich organelle is formed by a vesicle derived from the cell membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Food vacuole\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosome\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Golgi apparatus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen a cell takes in material through endocytosis, it produces a vesicle derived from the cell membrane, which is how a food vacuole is formed.\n\nRibosomes are involved in translation (**Choice B**), the golgi apparatus sorts, packages and modifies proteins (**Choice C**) and the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, among other functions, synthesizes lipids (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0560","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0560\n\nPalisade mesophyll cells produce and release large amounts of carbohydrates. Which characteristics would you expect to observe in such cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of mitochondria and a large central vacuole.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of chloroplasts and an extensive Golgi apparatus.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of chloroplasts and a large central vacuole.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of mitochondria and an extensive Golgi apparatus.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because palisade mesophyll cells are the primary site of photosynthesis. A high density of chloroplasts is required to capture light energy. The large central vacuole is essential because it pushes the chloroplasts to the edges of the cell, minimising the distance carbon dioxide must diffuse and maximising light absorption.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because while palisade cells contain mitochondria for cellular maintenance, their dominant characteristic is the machinery for production (chloroplasts), not just the breakdown of energy.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although the Golgi apparatus is involved in cell wall carbohydrate packaging, the central vacuole is a more significant and defining structural adaptation for the specific function of light-dependent carbohydrate production in the mesophyll."},{"ref":"BI0562","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0562\n\nThe electron micrograph shows a sarcomere, the smallest functional unit of striated muscle tissue. What is the actual length between the two Z lines?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0562i.png\" alt=\"BI0562i\">\n[Source: Adapted from Loison, O., Weitkunat , M., Kaya-Çopur , A., Nascimento Alves, C., Matzat , T., & Spletter , M. L. (2018). *Category: sarcomeres*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Sarcomeres Copyright under CC BY 3.0][Ruler tool, cm]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10 µm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 mm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 cm\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 µm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe length between the two Z lines is 4.5 cm, which is equivalent to 45000 µm. Actual length is calculated using the equation; image size divided by magnification. 45000 µm ÷ 45000 x = 1 µm.  **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0575","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0575\n\nMesophyll cells are found in plants. Plant cells have unique characteristics that distinguish them from any other cell type.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0575.png\" alt=\"BI0575\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Zhang, N., Zhang, L., Zhao, L., Ren, Y., Cui, D., Chen, J., ... & Chen, F. (2017). *iTRAQ and virus-induced gene silencing revealed three proteins involved in the cold response in bread wheat*. Scientific reports, 7(1), 1-16.Retrieved March 27, 2022. From https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5548720/. Copyright under CC by 4.0]\n\nIn the micrograph above, one of the cell parts is labelled X. What is the name of this structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chloroplast\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Central vacuole\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell wall\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Starch granule","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** - The cellulose cell wall is the outermost layer of plant cells just outside the plasma membrane.\n\nChloroplasts (**Choice A**) are membrane-bound oval-shaped organelles (labelled Ch in the image) that are responsible for photosynthesis.\n\nThe central vacuole (**Choice B**) is located in the centre of the cell, not towards the outside.  It is evident in the micrograph provided by the white, empty space in the middle of the cell.\n\nStarch granules (**Choice D**) are structures consisting of starch.  Their function is to store starch which is produced during photosynthesis. Starch granules are not visible in the micrgraph above."},{"ref":"BI0752","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0752\n\nWhat is the correct magnification of this image?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/bi0752.png\" alt=\"bi0752\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia (2006, March 1). *File:Electron Microscope Siemens, 1960.jpg - wikimedia commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved March 4, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscope_Siemens,_1960.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1000 x\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 100 x\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1 x\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10000 x","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMagnification is calculated using the equation image size divided by actual size. Both numbers must be the same units. The image size of the magnification bar is 5 mm, or 5000 µm, the actual size of the magnification bar is 5 µm. 5000 µm ÷ 5 µm = 1000 x. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0753","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0753\n\nThe electron micrograph shows a prokaryotic cell, *Cyclotella litoralis*. What is the actual diameter of the cell in this image?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/bi0753.png\" alt=\"bi0753\">\n[Source: Adapted from FWC Fish and Wildlife Research Institute. (2011, June 7). *Cyclotella litoralis (scanning electron micrograph)*. Flickr. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/myfwc/5808259556 Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3.5 cm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 350 µm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3500 mm\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 35 µm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diameter of the cell is the total length, along the widest part of the cell; 3.5 cm. When the scale bar, which represents 10 µm, is measured, it is shown to be 1 cm long in the image. 3.5 multiplied by 10 is 35 µm.  **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0756","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0756\n\nWhat feature distinguishes fungal hyphae and striated muscle from the two cell types shown in the images?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/bi0756.png\" alt=\"bi0756\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00.jpg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00.jpg Copyright under CC by 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are multinucleate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They only have one nucleus.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have unusually large cells that contain only one nucleus.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They contain mitochondria.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFungal hyphae and striated muscle are both exceptions to the cell theory as they are much larger than typical cells and are multinucleate. **Choice B**  is incorrect as the diagrams depict onion epidermis cells and mast cells, which each contain one nucleus. **Choice C**  is incorrect as both fungal hyphae and striated muscles contain multiple nuclei. **Choice D** is incorrect as fungal hyphae, striated muscle AND the cells in the diagram all contain mitochondria."},{"ref":"BI0798","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0798\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe micrograph shows a transverse section through a leaf.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0798A.png\" alt=\"BI0798A\">\n[Source:Adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library,2018. *Angiosperm Morphology Mesophyll Arrangement in Ligustrum (36198182664).jpg. Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angiosperm_Morphology_Mesophyll_Arrangement_in_Ligustrum_(36198182664).jpg.Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Identify an adaptation of this leaf for its function.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Identify the cell organelle that is found in particularly high numbers in the palisade mesophyll cells. [1 mark]\n\nThis micrograph shows a transverse section through a pancreatic acinar cell. Acinar cells are found in the exocrine tissue of the pancreas.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0798B.png\" alt=\"BI0798B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L., 2006. *Pancreatic acinar cells.jpg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=pancreatic+cells&title=Special:MediaSearch&go=Go&type=image&haslicense=unrestricted. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n2.\n    1. Explain how the structure of this cell is related to the function of the synthesis of proteins for release from the cell. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State why an electron microscope is needed to view the ultrastructure of the\nacinar cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large number of chloroplasts    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air spaces **OR** spongy mesophyll                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular tissues **OR** veins **OR** vascular bundles                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata **OR** guard cells                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waxy cuticle                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ columnar cells in top layer **OR** palisade mesophylll    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The leaf has a **large surface area** ($\\checkmark$) for absorption of light.\n\n1.\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The organelle in greater abundance in the palisade mesophyll cells is **the chloroplast** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus separated from cytoplasm by membrane **AND** allowing post-transcriptional modification of mRNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large numbers of membrane-bound ribosomes **OR** rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** for translation **OR** synthesis of proteins for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large amount of rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** lumen of rough endoplasmic reticulum is where polypeptides are folded and modified to form proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many vesicles **AND** used to transport proteins from rough endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi apparatus **OR** to carry proteins to plasma **OR** cell membrane for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus **AND** receives proteins from rough endoplasmic reticulum and modifies **OR** packages proteins for release from  cell [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of this cell is adapted to its function because it contains a large amount of **rough endoplasmic reticulum**. The **ribosomes** on the rough endoplasmic reticulum are the site of the **synthesis** of the **polypeptides** ($\\checkmark$) and these **polypeptides** are then **folded and modified into functional proteins** in the **lumen** of the **rough endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopes have a higher resolution than light microscopes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopes have a higher magnification than light microscopes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Electron microscopes have **higher magnification** ($\\checkmark$) and **higher resolution** ($\\checkmark$)  than light microscopes, so they allow more detail to be seen in smaller objects."},{"ref":"BI0799","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0799\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe micrograph below shows *E. coli* cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0799A.png\" alt=\"BI0799A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Highton, P., 1968. *E.coli image.jpeg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E.coli_image.jpg. Copyright under Creative CommonsCC0 1.0]\n\n1. Identify the structure labelled I in the micrograph. [1 mark]\n\n2. If the magnification of the image is x36,000 and the length of cell II in the image is 54 mm, calculate the actual length of cell II in micrometres (µm). [2 marks]\n\n3. State **one** feature, other than the structure labelled I, that can be used to identify the cells in this micrograph as prokaryotic cells. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Nucleoid **OR** region of circular DNA **OR** region of bacterial chromosome **OR** circular DNA    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Structure I is the **nucleoid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{54000 µm}}{\\text{36000}}\\right) $\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ = 1.5µm    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Magnification = x36000\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Length in image = 54mm x 1000 = 54000 µm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Actual length\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\textbf{54000 µm}}{\\textbf{36000}}\\right) $  ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.5 µm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall of peptidoglycan    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell in binary fission        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no compartments in cell **OR** no membrane bound organelles **OR** no nucleus    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n    The cell does **not have** a nucleus, or any other **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0804","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0804\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows a cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/804.png\" alt=\"804\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gonzalez, R., & Kerfeld, C. (2022, June 13). *Synechococcus elongatus*. Wikipedia. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synechococcus_elongatus Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Deduce with a reason, what type of cell this is.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the magnification of the cell.            [1 mark]\n\n    3. Outline two functions of life carried out by the cell in the diagram. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. State the type of microscope used to produce the image.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain your choice of microscope for part (i).            [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Prokaryote *(needed for mark)*\n\n    then any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has a nucleoid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no membrane bound organelles **OR** a named organelle such as a Golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum or mitochondria    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ size is typical of prokaryote (0.1μm - 5μm)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rod shaped    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cell is a **prokaryotic**  cell as it contains **no nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) or **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 110,000 x    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    magnification= image size ÷  actual size\n\n    image size = 11 mm = 11,000,000 nm\n\n    actual size = 100 nm\n\n    11,000,000nm ÷ 100 nm = **110,000** x ($\\checkmark$)\n\n1.\n    3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition: consuming food for energy **OR** metabolic processes **OR** growth **OR** reproduction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction: producing offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolism: chemical reactions that occur within the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis: maintaining a stable internal environment    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth: a permanent  increase in number **OR** size of cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion: the removal of metabolic waste    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ response: the ability to react to changes in the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ movement: change in position OR location in relation to the surroundings [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two functions of life carried out by the cell are **excretion**, which is the **removal of metabolic waste** ($\\checkmark$) and **reproduction** which is **producing offspring** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscope    **OR** transmission electron microscope        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An **electron microscope** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it allows you to see the ultrastructure of the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a high resolution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a high magnification        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An electron microscope would be used to create the image, as it allows one to see the **ultrastructure** of the cell ($\\checkmark$) due to its **high resolution**  and **magnification** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0816","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0816\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe micrograph shows an image of a plant cell taken with a light microscope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816A.png\" alt=\"BI0816A\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg . Copyright free]\n\n1. Calculate the magnification of the image, showing your work. [2 marks]\n\n2. Label, with the letter N, a nucleus in the image. [1 mark]\n\n3. The image was taken using a light microscope. State how you deduce this from the image. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw and label a diagram to show how the same type of plant cell shown in the micrograph would appear when examined with an electron microscope. [3 marks]\n\n5. State two differences that would be seen when looking at an electron micrograph of a bacterium cell compared to this plant cell. [2 marks]\n\n6. At first, the student who took this image could not see the cell when focusing the microscope. Suggest what may have caused this problem. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 4000 ÷ 50 **OR** size of image/ size of specimen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 80 x [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSA\">\n [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n Magnification = Image size ÷ Actual size\n\n Image size = 39 - 35 = 4 mm\n\n 1mm = 1000 µm\n\n 4mm = 4000 µm\n\n Actual size = 50 µm\n\n Magnification = **4000 µm ÷ 50 µm** ($\\checkmark$) = **80 x** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ labelled arrow N pointing to a nucleus [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSB\">\n [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot see the ultrastructure of the cell **OR** ribosomes **OR** mitochondria **OR** rough endoplasmic reticulum **OR** Golgi apparatus **OR** vacuoles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ low resolution [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to deduce that the image was taken with a light microscope as the **ultrastructure** of the cell is **not visible** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram must contain a large angular shape, with a labelled nucleus, cell membrane **AND** cell wall [1 mark]\n\n **Labels:**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 80S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vacuole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleolus [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816ANSCOR.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSCOR\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70s ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flagellum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleoid region [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pili [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two different structures that could be seen when looking at a bacterium as opposed to the plant cells through an electron microscope are **pili** ($\\checkmark$) and the **nucleoid region** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the light was off **OR** not bright enough **OR** diaphragm was not open enough [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the cell was not directly below the lens [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the microscope was not focused sufficiently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the slide **OR** coverslip was too dirty [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the objective lens was not clicked in correctly **OR** not appropriately housed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the lens **OR** eyepiece was dirty [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student may not have been able to see the cell as it was **not placed directly below the lens** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0833","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0833\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nBelow is a diagram of a motor neuron. Neurons are the longest cells in the human body.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0833A.png\" alt=\"BI0833A\">\n\n1. Explain the factors that limit cell size in humans. [4 marks]\n\nBelow is a diagram of a *Paramecium*, a unicellular organism.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/paramecium%20black.png\" alt=\"paramecium black\">\n\n2. Explain how life functions are performed by *Paramecium*. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area affects the rate at which materials enter and leave the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume affects the rate at which materials are used and produced by the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the surface area to volume ratio affects the rate of metabolism in a cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the bigger the cell, the smaller the SA:V ratio is [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cells having a low SA:V ratio cannot exchange materials fast enough [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some cells, **OR** motor neurons, **OR** muscle cells, **OR** other named example in humans, are long and thin, which maintains a high SA:V ratio [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n The **surface area** of a cell affects the rate at which materials **enter and leave cells** ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, the cell’s **volume** affects the rate at which materials are **used and produced** ($\\checkmark$). A small surface area to volume ratio is not ideal for human cells because it **limits the cell’s ability to export waste from the cytoplasm** rapidly enough to meet the **cytoplasm’s metabolic needs, such as the availability of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). **A large cell has a small surface area to volume ratio** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, the transport of the substances is more efficient in small cells.\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (function of life must be mentioned when explaining how *Paramecium* performs this, to award mark):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ functions of life are metabolism **AND** growth **AND** reproduction **AND** nutrition **AND** homeostasis **AND** movement **AND** excretion **AND** response to stimuli, **OR** sensitivity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement in *Paramecium* is demonstrated by cilia used to propel themselves through water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ response is through reacting to stimuli in the environment **OR** avoidance of light [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis is demonstrated by keeping the internal conditions within set limits **OR** excess water is expelled by the contractile vacuole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolism is carried out by enzymes in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ growth is an irreversible increase in size **OR** dry mass and paramecium achieve this through accumulating organic matter **OR** nutrients from food [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction function in *Paramecium* is demonstrated by asexual division through binary fission, **OR** mitosis or micronucleus and amitotic division of macronucleus **OR** sexually through conjugation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion is the removal of metabolic waste products from the cell **OR** diffusion of CO:sub[2] out of the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition is performed by ingesting smaller organisms and digesting them in vesicles **OR** through endocytosis [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n *Paramecium* acquires **nutrients** by ingesting bacteria and **digesting them in vesicles**($\\checkmark$). It exhibits **growth** when it **irreversibly increases in size** by accumulating organic matter from ingested **food** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* **responds** to different environmental stimuli. For example, it **changes** the **direction** of its movement once it **touches** a solid object ($\\checkmark$). **Excretion** in *Paramecium* occurs when waste products such as **carbon dioxide diffuse out of the cell** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* **reproduces** both asexually by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1136","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1136\n\nWhat is the actual width of the cell in this image? The scale bar represents 5μm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1135.png\" alt=\"BI1135\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia (2006, March 1). *File: Electron Microscope Siemens, 1960*.jpg - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved March 4, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscope_Siemens,_1960.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4μm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 40μm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 400μm\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4000μm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe scale bar is shown to be 5μm in width, which is represented by 0.5cm on the ruler. The ruler indicates a total cell width of 4cm. Therefore, 8 scale bar widths will fit within the cell. Actual cell width = 8 $\\times$ 5μm = 40μm.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1137","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1137\n\nWhich row in the table correctly identifies structures which are present (✓) or absent (✕)  in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | Structure | Prokaryotic cells | Eukaryotic cells |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 70S Ribosomes in Cytosol | ✓ | ✕ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell Wall | ✕ | ✓ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mitochondria | ✓ | ✕ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Naked DNA | ✕ | ✓ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotic cells contain 70S ribosomes in their cytoplasm, while eukaryotic cells have 80s cytoplasmic ribosomes, so **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as prokaryotes have a cell wall, but not all eukaryotes do. **Choice C** is incorrect as prokaryotes do not contain membrane-bound organelles such as mitochondria, but eukaryotes do. ***Choice D** is incorrect, as prokaryotic cells have naked DNA, whereas eukaryotic cells contain DNA within a nuclear membrane."},{"ref":"BI1197","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1197\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe micrograph below shows *Cyanobacteria*, which contain chlorophyll.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1197-SL.png\" alt=\"BI1197-SL\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thompson L. and Whatson N. (2007, January 1).  *Cyanobacteria*.  Wikipedia.  Retrieved on September 6, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanobacteria#. Copyright under CCO]\n\n1. Using your knowledge and the micrograph, state two features that would indicate that this is a bacterium.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the structural differences between animal and plant cells.  [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how light energy is used in photosynthesis to carry out anabolic reactions. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall of peptidoglycan            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ naked DNA **OR** DNA not associated with histones **OR** proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleoid **OR** DNA free in cytoplasm                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no membrane bound organelles                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 70s ribosomes                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell smaller than 10μm                            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two features that would indicate that this is a bacterium are it has **no membrane-bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$) and **70s ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have a cell wall, **WHEREAS** animal cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells generally have a central vacuole **WHEREAS** animal cells do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have chloroplasts **WHEREAS** animal cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells may have lysosomes **WHEREAS** plant cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells may have cilia **OR** flagella **WHEREAS** plant cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells have centrioles **WHEREAS** plant cells do not        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Plant cells have a **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), **central vacuole** ($\\checkmark$) and **chloroplasts**, whereas **animal** cells **do not** ($\\checkmark$) .\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is transferred to electrons **OR** is used to excite electrons    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy splitting water molecules **OR** photolysis releases electrons for the electron transport chain **OR** hydrogen ions for use in ATP production    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is used to synthesise ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate **OR** NADPH from electrons and one proton            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADPH **OR** ATP is used to make larger organic compounds **OR** glucose **OR** starch **OR** other named organic compound from carbon dioxide        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Light energy is **absorbed** by **chlorophyll** ($\\checkmark$) and is used to **excite electrons** ($\\checkmark$).  These electrons reduce **NADP:sup[+]** to form **NADPH**, which is an anabolic reaction ($\\checkmark$).  Energy present in **NADPH** is then used to **produce glucose** from **carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$).  This is also an anabolic reaction."},{"ref":"BI1223","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1223\n\nWhich structure in a eukaryotic cell would also be present in a prokaryotic cell?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma membrane\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 80S ribosomes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Histones","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells contain a plasma membrane; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they are present in eukaryotic cells but not prokaryotic cells."},{"ref":"BI1258","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1258\n\nBelow is a micrograph of a bacterium, *Marinomonas arctica*, which is found in Arctic sea ice.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1258.png\" alt=\"BI1258\">\n[Source: Gulf of Alaska Seamounts expedition, 2019. *Marinomonas arctica.jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved, September 25th 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Marinomonas_arctica.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\nWhich row contains features of prokaryotic cells such as *Marinomonas arctica*?\n\n| | Ribosomes | Cell wall | DNA associated with proteins\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 80S | Yes | No |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 80S | Yes | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 70S | No | No |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 70S | Yes | No |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because bacteria are prokaryotes, and prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes and a cell wall; they do not have proteins associated with the DNA. **Choice A** is incorrect because prokaryotes do not have 80S ribosomes. **Choice B** is incorrect because prokaryotes do not have 80S ribosomes or proteins associated with DNA. **Choice C** is incorrect because prokaryotes do have cell walls."},{"ref":"BI1259","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1259\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the images produced by electron microscopes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | High resolution |\n    | II. | High magnification |\n    | III. | Coloured |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nElectron micrographs have high resolution (**Statement I**) and high magnification (**Statement II**) **Choice B** is therefore correct.\n\nElectron micrographs are not coloured (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one correct statement.  **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because they include **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1263","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1263\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how some eukaryotic cells have an atypical cell structure.            [3 marks]\n\n2. Use named examples to explain how unicellular organisms carry out processes of life.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** from the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mature red blood cells do not have a nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells have many nuclei    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptate fungal hyphae **OR** fungal hyphae **OR** *Mucor* are multinucleate     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem sieve tube elements lack nuclei **OR** do not have many organelles **OR** have continuous cytoplasm flowing through adjacent cells **OR** have sieve tube elements to provide metabolic support **OR** are interconnected cells.    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Mature red blood cells **do not have a nucleus** ($\\checkmark$), mature sieve tube elements **do not have a nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and striated muscle cells and fungal hyphae have **many nuclei** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **five** of the following characteristics and a correct way the chosen unicellular organism carries out that process:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cilia allow movement in *Paramecium* **OR** flagella allow movement in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** any other suitable mode of movement relevant to a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contractile vacuole helps to regulate water potential of the cell **OR** contractile vacuole has a role in homeostasis in *Paramecium* **OR** *Chlamydomonas* **OR** any other mechanism of homeostasis that is relevant to a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme-catalysed metabolic reactions such as respiration occur in the cytoplasm and mitochondria of *Paramecium* **OR** photosynthesis occurs in the chloroplast of *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct example of metabolism for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oral groove allows nutrition in *Paramecium* **OR** nutrition is by photosynthesis in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct mode of nutrition for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Paramecium* can reproduce asexually by binary fission **OR** *Paramecium* can reproduce by conjugation **OR** *Chlorella* reproduces asexually by mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolic wastes such as CO$_2$ and ammonia diffuse out across the cell membrane in *Paramecium* **OR** metabolic wastes diffuse out across the cell membrane in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct mode of excretion for a named unicellular organism (do not accept removal of undigested food through the anal pore, as this is egestion)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of *Paramecium* occurs after cell division **OR** *Chlamydomonas* increases in size by synthesising new cell components between divisions **OR** correct mode of growth for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Paramecium* sense their environment and respond by movement due to the movement of cilia **OR** *Chlamydomonas* has an eyespot to detect light and responds by swimming towards it using its flagella (phototaxis) **OR** correct example of sensitivity for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    *Paramecia* are unicellular organisms that can carry out all functions of life. *Paramecia* have **cilia that allow movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$) happen in mitochondria and the cytoplasm. The contractile vacuole helps to **regulate the water concentration** ($\\checkmark$) of *Paramecium* by releasing excess water. The oral groove takes in food, so it is responsible for **nutrition** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* reproduces asexually by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following: \\\n*Answer **must** include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*\n\n    **Similarities:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have plasma membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have cytoplasm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have ribosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have genetic material **OR** DNA **OR** RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are not compartmentalised  **BUT** eukaryotic cells are compartmentalised    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells do not have membrane-bound organelles **BUT** eukaryotic cells do have membrane-bound organelles            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have DNA in a loop **OR** plasmid **BUT** eukaryotes have linear DNA **OR** chromosomes         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have 70S ribosomes **BUT** eukaryotic cells have 80S ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells do not have a true nucleus **OR** have a nucleoid region **BUT** eukaryotic cells do have a true nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all prokaryotic cells have a cell wall **BUT** not all eukaryotic cells have a cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is naked **OR** not associated with proteins **BUT** eukaryotic DNA is associated with histone proteins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are smaller **THAN** eukaryotic cells        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **genetic material** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **plasma membranes** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$), but **prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes, whereas eukaryotic cells have 80S ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells **do not have a true nucleus**, but eukaryotic cells do have a true nucleus ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells do not have membrane-bound organelles but **eukaryotic cells do have membrane-bound organelles** such as mitochondria ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells have **DNA in a loop** but eukaryotic cells have linear DNA ($\\checkmark$). Not all eukaryotic cells have a cell wall but **all prokaryotic cells do have a cell wall** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n*A quality mark is awarded if each part of the question (a, b, c) is answered to earn at least half of the points available, answers do not require re-reading to be understood, and there are almost no errors and little irrelevant information.* [1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI1322","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1322\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between the structure and location of chromosomes found in eukaryotes and prokaryotes.            [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe the stages of meiosis and how genetically unique, haploid gametes are created during the process.    [8 marks]\n\n3. Explain the information that can be deduced from a human karyogram.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic chromosomes are found in the nucleus **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes are found in the nucleoid region **OR** chromosomes of eukaryotes are contained within a membrane **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes are not membrane bound     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes have a circular chromosome **WHEREAS** eukaryotes have linear chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes typically have one chromosome **WHEREAS** eukaryotes typically have multiple chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic chromosomes, DNA is associated with histone proteins **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain histones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain centromeres **WHEREAS** eukaryotic chromosomes do [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain telomeres **WHEREAS** eukaryotic chromosomes do     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic chromosomes carry more genetic information **OR** DNA **OR** genes **THAN** prokaryotic chromosomes    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Eukaryotic chromosomes are found in the **nucleus** whereas prokaryotic chromosomes are found in the **nucleoid** region ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotes typically contain **one, circular** chromosome ($\\checkmark$)  whereas eukaryotes  have **multiple, linear** chromosomes ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic chromosomes **do not** contain **centromeres** and **telomeres** ($\\checkmark$), whereas eukaryotic chromosomes do ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all the DNA in a cell is replicated during interphase        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so cells are diploid **OR** contain homologous chromosomes when they enter meiosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes pair **AND** become visible **OR** condense  in prophase I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over occurs between the non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during prophase I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are moved to the equator of the cell by spindle microtubules **AND** randomly orientated OR independently assorted in metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite sides of the cell by spindle microtubules in anaphase I            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one diploid cell is split into two haploid cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromatids condense **OR** become visible during prophase II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of sister chromatids are moved to the equator of the cell by spindle microtubules during metaphase II **AND** randomly orientated **OR** independently assorted    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of sister chromatids are separated by spindle fibres in anaphase II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nuclear membrane reforms in telophase II **AND** four haploid cells are formed from the two haploid cells of     meiosis         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during prophase I **AND** independent assortment **OR** random alignment of homologous chromosomes in metaphase I **OR** metaphase II result in unique gametes    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    All of the DNA in a cell is **replicated** during **interphase** ($\\checkmark$), so cells are **diploid** when they enter meiosis ($\\checkmark$). **Homologous** chromosomes **pair up**, become **visible** ($\\checkmark$) and **crossing over** of non-sister chromatids occur during **prophase I** ($\\checkmark$). Then, homologous chromosomes are moved to the **equator** of the cell by **spindle microtubules** and **randomly orientated** during **metaphase I** ($\\checkmark$). Homologous chromosomes are pulled to the **opposite poles** of the cell by spindle fibres in **anaphase I** ($\\checkmark$) and one **diploid cell** is split into **two haploid** cells during telophase I ($\\checkmark$). The cells then enter meiosis II. The **pairs** of **sister chromatids** are **independently assorted** to form a single line along the equator of the cell, in **metaphase II** ($\\checkmark$). During **anaphase II** the centromeres split and the **sister chromatids** are pulled to **opposite poles** of the cell ($\\checkmark$). In **telophase II** the **nuclear membrane reforms** around the chromosomes, and after cytokinesis, **4 new haploid, non-identical**,  daughter cells are formed from the **2 haploid cells** that resulted from **meiosis I** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a karyogram is an image showing the positions of centromeres and banding patterns of stained **OR** dyed homologous chromosomes ordered by decreasing length        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biological sex determined by XX and XY **OR** sex chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ whether non-disjunction has occurred is determined by the presence or absence of chromosome (s)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of chromosomal abnormalities (such as breakages or translocation) determined by the banding pattern **OR** size of chromosome        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indication of chromosome disorders such as Down syndrome, which is caused by trisomy 21[1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A karyogram is an image showing the positions of **centromeres** and **banding patterns** of stained **homologous chromosomes** ordered by decreasing length ($\\checkmark$). A karyogram can be used to deduce **biological sex** ($\\checkmark$) or the presence of **chromosomal abnormalities** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1440","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1440\n\nWhich of the following statements read true for differences between prokaryotes and eukaryotes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Prokaryotes have DNA associated with histones, while eukaryotic cells have naked DNA. |\n    | II. | Eukaryotes have linear chromosomes, while prokaryotes have circular chromosomes. |\n    | III. | Most prokaryotes have a singular chromosome, whilst most eukaryotic somatic cells contain at least two chromosomes. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotes typically have DNA that is not associated with histones and is often referred to as ‘naked’. In eukaryotes, histone proteins help package the DNA into structural units called nucleosomes. This makes **Statement I** false.\n\nEukaryotic chromosomes are typically linear and located within the nucleus, while most prokaryotic organisms have a single circular chromosome found in a nucleoid region without a surrounding nuclear membrane. This makes **Statement II** true.\n\nMost prokaryotes contain a single circular chromosome, while eukaryotic somatic cells are generally diploid with paired chromosomes. Therefore **Statement III** is true.\n\nSeeming as only **Statements II** and **III** are true, this makes **Choice B** the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1471","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1471\n\nThe micrograph shows the ultrastructure of a cell. What type of cell is shown and what process is it undergoing?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1471%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1471 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Lewis, M. E., Belland, R. J., AbdelRahman, Y. M., Beatty, W. L., Aiyar, A. A., Zea, A. H., Greene, S. J., Marrero, L., Buckner, L. R., Tate, D. J., McGowin, C. L., Kozlowski, P. A., O’Brien, M., Lillis, R. A., Martin, D. H., & Quayle, A. J. (2014, May 15). Morphologic and molecular evaluation of chlamydia trachomatis growth in human endocervix reveals distinct growth patterns. Frontiers. Retrieved February 11 , 2024, from https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fcimb.2014.00071/full. Copyright free]\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$Type of cell$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Process$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| --- | :-- | :-- |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eukaryotic | Mitosis |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eukaryotic | Binary fission |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prokaryotic | Mitosis |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prokaryotic | Binary fission |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nProkaryotic cells have an approximate diameter of 0.1$-$ 5.0  µm, whereas eukaryotic cells are significantly larger with a diameter range of 10 $-$ 100  µm.  The cells in the micrograph have an approximate diameter of 0.5  µm, so are prokaryotic. Prokaryotic cells do not replicate themselves using mitosis, they use binary fission. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A**  and **B** are incorrect as the cells in the micrograph are prokaryotic, not eukaryotic. **Choice C**  is incorrect as prokaryotic cells lack a mitotic spindle, so do not carry out mitosis, they carry out binary fission."},{"ref":"BI1542","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1542\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe micrograph shows a cell of an exocrine gland from the stomach of the European beaver (*Castor fiber*).\nExocrine glands are glands that release their secretions through a duct.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1542.png\" alt=\"BI1542\">\n[Source: Adapted from Ziółkowska, N., Lewczuk, B., Petryński, W., Palkowska, K., Prusik, M., Targońska, K., Giżejewski, Z., & Przybylska-Gornowicz, B. (2014, April).\n*(PDF) light and electron microscopy of the European beaver* ... Research gate. Retrieved March 21, 2024, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261609121_Light_and_Electron_Microscopy_of_the_European_Beaver_Castor_fiber_Stomach_Reveal_Unique_Morphological_Features\n_with_Possible_General_Biological_Significance. Copyright by CC 4.0]\n\n1. Suggest one role of exocrine glands.        [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the letter X, label the organelle with the function of producing proteins that are exported outside the cell.        [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain how **two** organelles are related to the function of an exocrine cell.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Pancreatic cells are both exocrine and endocrine glands. Name one hormone produced by the pancreas and state what the stimulus is for its secretion.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ digestion **OR** production **OR** secretion of digestive enzymes **OR** saliva **OR** named digestive enzyme\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bile production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweat production **OR** secretion **OR** thermoregulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sebum production **OR** secretion **OR** lubrication of skin **OR** hair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ milk production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ seminal fluid production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tear production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Endocrine glands secrete hormones directly into the **bloodstream**, whereas exocrine glands secrete substances to the **surfaces of the body** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) should be labelled with the letter X        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1542AnsA.png\" alt=\"BI1542AnsA\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Ziółkowska, N., Lewczuk, B., Petryński, W., Palkowska, K., Prusik, M., Targońska, K., Giżejewski, Z., & Przybylska-Gornowicz, B. (2014, April). *(PDF) light and electron microscopy of the European beaver* ... Research gate. Retrieved March 21, 2024, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261609121_Light_and_Electron_Microscopy_of_the_European_Beaver_Castor_fiber_Stomach_Reveal_Unique_Morphological_Features_with_Possible_General_Biological_Significance. Copyright by CC 4.0]\n\n3. any **two** of the following (must have structure and function to gain mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extensive endoplasmic reticulum to synthesise enzymes **OR** proteins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many mitochondria to produce ATP for protein synthesis                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many secretory vesicles to secrete substances                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many Golgi apparatus to process and package proteins                [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The exocrine cell has an **extensive endoplasmic reticulum** that **synthesises enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) and **many mitochondria** that produce **ATP** for protein synthesis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin secreted in response to high **OR** increase in blood glucose **OR** sugar levels        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucagon secreted in response to low **OR** decrease in blood glucose **OR** sugar levels        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The pancreas secretes **insulin** in response to an **increase** in **blood glucose** levels  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1557","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1557\n\nThe electron micrograph shows tobacco leaf mesophyll cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1557.png\" alt=\"BI1557\">\n[Adapted from: Marys, E. (n.d.). *A Ultrathin Section of Vegetal Leaf Mesophyll Cells Showing Chloroplast Structure Stock Image - Image of discovery, botany: 211880559*. Dreamstime.com. Retrieved April 23, 2024, from https://www.dreamstime.com/ultra-thin-section-transmission-electron-microscopy-tobacco-leaf-mesophyll-cells-showing-chloroplast-structure-ultrathin-image211880559. Royalty free image.]\n\nIdentify structures X, Y, and Z in the micrograph.\n\n| | Structure X | Structure Y | Structure Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | mitochondrion | nucleus | plasma membrane |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | chloroplast | vacuole | cell wall |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | mitochondrion | vacuole | plasma membrane |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | chloroplast | nucleus | cell wall |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because structure X is a chloroplast, structure Y is the nucleus and structure Z is the cell wall.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because X is a chloroplast, not a mitochondrion. **Choice B** is incorrect because Y is the nucleus, not a vacuole. **Choice C** is incorrect because both structures X and Y are mislabelled."},{"ref":"BI2320","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2320\n\nElectron microscopes allow the ultrastructure of cells and molecules to be viewed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Edit.png\" alt=\"Edit\">\n[Source: National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID)/NIH (2019, January 4). *Electron micrograph of tau clusters (46603522841).jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved December 27, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_micrograph_of_tau_clusters_(46603522841).jpg. Public Domain]\n\nWhich of these methods is used to analyse protein structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Freeze-fracture electron microscopy\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fluorescent staining\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cryogenic electron microscopy\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Immunofluorescence","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because cryogenic electron microscopy can produce 3D images of biological molecules such as proteins, DNA, and RNA. Samples are rapidly frozen to preserve their natural state before an electron beam is passed through, allowing the structure of individual molecules to be studied.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because freeze-fracture electron microscopy is primarily used to study cellular structures, such as the internal arrangement of membranes. While it involves rapid freezing and can produce 3D images, it does not resolve molecular or atomic details of protein structures.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because fluorescent staining is used in light microscopy to identify specific molecules, such as proteins, in cells. Fluorescent dyes or antibodies emit specific wavelengths of light, highlighting target molecules, but they do not provide detailed structural information about proteins.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because immunofluorescence identifies specific proteins in cells or tissues using fluorescently tagged antibodies. While it is useful for locating proteins, it is not a technique for analysing protein structure at the molecular level."},{"ref":"BI2438","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2438\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nBelow is a diagram of *Saccharomyces cervesiae* commonly referred to as Brewer's yeast.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI2438.png\" alt=\"BI2438\">\n\n1. Distinguish between the cell wall in fungal and plant cells. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Explain the presence of chloroplast in plant cells, but not animal or fungal cells. [2 marks]\n\n 2. A chloroplast is a type of plastid. Outline the function of one other named type of plastid found in plant cells that is absent in fungal cells. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ fungal cell walls are primarily composed of chitin **WHEREAS** plant cell walls are composed of cellulose [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The major component of fungal cell walls is **chitin**, but in plants, it’s **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plants use chloroplasts for photosynthesis **AND** are autotrophs **OR** allows plants to make their own food **OR** convert light energy to chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ animals and fungi do not need chloroplast to photosynthesise because they are heterotrophs **OR** feed on other organisms **OR** obtain energy by consuming organic matter rather than producing it [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Plants are **autotrophs** and make their own food by photosynthesis. **Chloroplasts** contain chlorophyll which absorbs the light energy needed for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). Animals and fungi are **heterotrophs**, they consume their food and **do not need chloroplast** for photosynthesis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chromoplasts synthesise **OR** store pigments (other than chlorophyll) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leucoplasts are involved in storage **OR** metabolism [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Plant cells have **chromoplasts** which synthesise and **store pigments**, such as carotenoids ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2786","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2786\n\nThe table below presents observations made about four newly discovered entities (W, X, Y and Z).\n\n| Entity | Observation |\n| :-: | :-- |\n| W | Contains a cell wall, cytoplasm, and genetic material, but no internal compartments. |\n| X | Consists of a protein coat surrounding RNA. |\n| Y | Contains membrane-bound compartments, each containing DNA. |\n| Z | Exists as free-floating DNA molecules in a liquid medium. |\n\nBased on cell theory, which row of the table below correctly identifies whether each entity is likely to be classified as a living organism?\n\n| | Entity W | Entity X | Entity Y | Entity Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes  | No | Yes | No\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | Yes | No | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes | No | No | Yes |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | Yes | Yes | No |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Entity W**, with a cell wall, cytoplasm, and genetic material, strongly indicates that it is a cell, the basic unit of life. It is likely a prokaryotic cell.\\\n**Entity X** is only reported to have RNA with a protein coat surrounding it. This description best matches that of a virus. Viruses are not considered living organisms.\\\n**Entity Y** contains membrane-bound compartments, which are indicative of eukaryotic organelles and DNA. This description is likely describing a eukaryotic cell.\\\n**Entity Z** is only DNA without any barrier to the outside and does not describe a cell.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it correctly identifies **Entities W** and **Y** as likely to be classified as living, while **X** and **Z are classified** as non-living. **Choice B, Choice C**, and **Choice D** do not have this combination of entities."},{"ref":"BI2787","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2787\n\nA student, learning to use a microscope, observed a pollen grain under a microscope with a magnification of ×100. Using an eyepiece graticule, they measured the diameter of the pollen grain to be 25 divisions. At this magnification, 1 division on the eyepiece graticule equals 3 $\\mu$m. To allow the student to measure more pollen grains and to calculate a mean diameter, which of the following conclusions on the experimental work so far is/are correct?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$    Increasing the magnification of the objective lens would widen the field of view.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    The actual diameter of the pollen grain is 75 $\\mu$m.\n- III. $\\hspace{.5em}$    If the magnification were increased to ×200, each eyepiece graticule division would represent a smaller actual size.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true. Increasing the magnification will narrow the field of view. This is a common student mistake. As magnification increases, the observer is focusing on a smaller area, like zooming in with a camera.\\\n**Statement II** is true. The actual diameter of the pollen grain is calculated as 25 divisions × 3 µm/division = 75 µm.\\\n**Statement III** is true. If the magnification were increased by switching to a higher power objective, the same number of eyepiece graticule divisions would span a smaller actual distance on the specimen.\\\n**Choice C** is correct because it contains the proper combination of **Statement II** and **Statement III**. **Choice A, Choice B** and **Choice D** do not contain the proper combination of statements."},{"ref":"BI2788","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2788\n\nA researcher labelled two different proteins with molecules that emit light of distinct colours when illuminated with a specific wavelength of light. To determine if these proteins are present in the same location in the cell, which type of microscopy would be most appropriate?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bright field microscopy\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Scanning electron microscopy\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fluorescence microscopy\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transmission electron microscopy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFluorescence microscopy uses specific wavelengths of light to excite fluorescent molecules, the labels, causing them to emit light at different wavelengths. Using appropriate filters, the light emitted from each can be detected to determine their locations. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct.\n\nBright field microscopy is the term used to describe a common light microscopy technique where specimens appear dark against a bright background due to the direct transmission of light through the sample. Bright field microscopy would not be able to excite specific fluorescent molecules or distinguish between particular wavelengths of light, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nScanning electron microscopy uses a stream of electrons to produce an image of the surface of an organism and does not use or detect any wavelength of light, so **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\nTransmission electron microscopy passes a stream of electrons through a thin specimen to visualise internal structures and does not use or detect any wavelength of light, so **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2789","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2789\n\nThe plasma membrane, a structure common to all cells, is primarily composed of a phospholipid bilayer. What is the fundamental reason for this specific lipid arrangement?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To provide a flexible boundary for the cell and a source of stored energy.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To create a rigid barrier impermeable to most substances for protection.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To form a selectively permeable barrier due to the hydrophobic nature of the lipids.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To actively transport molecules using energy from the lipids.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because it accurately describes the plasma membrane as selectively permeable, allowing some things to pass and some not, and this is because the tails of the phospholipids comprising the membrane are hydrophobic.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect. While it’s true that the membrane provides a flexible boundary for the cell, it is not a source of energy. If the membrane is broken down to release energy, the boundary of the cell will be destroyed, and the cell will cease to exist.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the membrane is not rigid or impermeable. It is flexible and partially permeable.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the energy used to transport molecules across the membrane is typically provided by the hydrolysis of ATP."},{"ref":"BI2790","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2790\n\nMatch the labelled structure (Q-T from the diagram) with its primary structure.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Ed2790.png\" alt=\"Ed2790\">\n\n| | Contains genetic information | Provides support and protection | Regulates the passage of substances |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | S | Q | R |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | Q | S |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | Q | R |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | R | Q |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the diagram, T indicates naked DNA, and this is the genetic material for the cell. Q indicates the cell wall, which provides structural support and protection. R indicates the cell membrane, which regulates the passage of substances in and out of the cell, and S indicates the cytoplasm, which is the site of metabolic reactions in the cell. Therefore, **Choice C** is the correct answer because it correctly matches the functions of the structures that are indicated by the letters in the diagram.\n\n**Choice A, Choice B**, and **Choice D** do not correctly match the functions of the structures indicated by the letters in the diagram."},{"ref":"BI2791","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2791\n\nWhich of the following options correctly identifies the forms of DNA and the types of ribosomes found in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | Prokaryotic DNA | Eukaryotic DNA | Prokaryotic Ribosomes  | Eukaryotic Ribosomes |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |  :-: |  :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear | Circular | 80S | 70S |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Circular | Linear | 80S | 80S |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Circular  | Linear | 70S | 80S |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear  | Linear | 70S | 70S |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotic cells have circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, while eukaryotic cells have linear DNA and 80S ribosomes. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct. **Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because they do not have the correct combinations."},{"ref":"BI2792","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2792\n\nThe diagram depicts a single-celled organism living in freshwater that faces water constantly moving into its cell due to osmosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI2792.png\" alt=\"BI2792\">\n[Source: Adapted from *File: Paramecium caudatum.svg.* (2024, March 12). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 04:34, April 23, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Paramecium_caudatum.svg&oldid=859786930.CC Public Domain]\n\nTo prevent bursting, the cell actively pumps out excess water using specialised structures. This process is an example of:\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Obtaining nutrients\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eliminating waste products\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Responding to external stimuli\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Maintaining homeostasis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHomeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment. The organism pumping out of excess water is a mechanism to keep its osmolarity stable and different from the outside of the cell. Therefore, **Choice D** is correct.\\\nThe organism is not taking in or making any substance for energy and growth, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nExcess water may be considered a waste product. However, the primary purpose of the pump is to regulate osmolarity and prevent lysis. Waste products typically refer to metabolic wastes like carbon dioxide or nitrogenous compounds. Therefore, **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\nThe active pumping out of water is the organism's way of maintaining internal conditions rather than just a direct response to the stimulus, and so **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2793","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2793\n\nWhich of the following statements is true of fungal cell walls?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are composed of cellulose.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fungal cells do not have cell walls.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are composed of chitin.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are composed of peptidoglycan.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because fungal cell walls are primarily composed of chitin. **Choice A** is incorrect because cellulose is the main carbohydrate that makes up plant cell walls. Peptidoglycan is found in bacterial cell walls, so **Choice D** is incorrect. Fungal cells do have cell walls, so **Choice B** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2794","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2794\n\nWhich of the following rows correctly pairs the cell type with its nuclear characteristic?\n\n| | Cell type | Nuclear state |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Phloem sieve tube elements | No nucleus |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Skeletal muscle cells | Single nucleus |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Red blood cells | Single nucleus |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Aseptate fungal hyphae | No nucleus |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because phloem sieve tube elements lose their nucleus as they mature to facilitate the transport of sugars. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because skeletal muscle cells are multinucleated to accommodate their length. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because mature red blood cells lack a nucleus. They jettison the nucleus when mature to increase the haemoglobin capacity of oxygen transport. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because aseptate fungal hyphae have a continuous cytoplasm containing many nuclei."},{"ref":"BI2795","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2795\n\nWhich of the following correctly pairs the structure with the type of cell in which it is found?\n\n| | Structure | Type of cell |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Nucleoid region | Animal |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell wall | Prokaryotic  |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chloroplast | Fungal cell |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sap vacuole  | Prokaryotic  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because nearly all prokaryotic cells possess a cell wall that provides structure, support, and protection. **Choice A** is incorrect because a nucleoid region is characteristic of a prokaryotic cell, not an animal cell. **Choice C** is incorrect because chloroplasts are found in plants and some algae cells, but not fungi. **Choice D** is incorrect because sap vacuoles are characteristic of plant cells, not prokaryotic cells."},{"ref":"BI2796","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2796\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe light micrographs below show examples of skeletal muscle, phloem sieve tube elements, aseptate fungal hyphae, and red blood cells. These types of cells are sometimes used as atypical examples of cells.\n\n| Skeletal Muscle | Phloem Sieve Tube Elements |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Aii.png\" alt=\"Aii\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BIII.png\" alt=\"BIII\"> |\n| [*File: Skeletal Muscle Tissue.jpg*. (2024, June 13). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:00, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Skeletal_Muscle_Tissue.jpg&oldid=883658940.]| [*File:Herbaceous Dicot Stem Tangential Section Phloem Parenchyma in Cucurbita (35207518570).jpg*. (2020, September 16). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:05, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w /index.php?title=File: Herbaceous_Dicot_Stem.]|\n\n| Aseptate Fungal Hyphae | Red Blood Cells |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/ci.png\" alt=\"ci\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/DII.png\" alt=\"DII\"> |\n| [*File: Yeast cells and short hyphae in Gram stain.jpg*. (2024, September 17). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:10, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia .org/w/index.php?title=File:Yeast_cells_and_short_hyphae_in_Gram_stain.jpg&oldid=925217916.]|  [*File: Blood cells 090304-F-5951M-108.jpg*. (2024, October 5). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:14, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Blood_cells_090304-F-5951M-108.jpg&oldid=933116797.] |\n\n1. Explain how the number of nuclei in each cell type in the micrograph of each cell relates to the cell’s function.            [4 marks]\n\n2. Contrast the cell wall composition of the aseptate hyphae and phloem sieve tube elements.                [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. to award a mark, the response must include the number of nuclei **AND** a reason for this\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skeletal muscle cells have multiple nuclei, providing multiple copies of genes to produce the large numbers of proteins needed for muscle contraction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem sieve tube elements lack a nucleus (at maturity), which creates space for the efficient transport of sugars and other organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptate fungal hyphae have multiple nuclei to allow for rapid growth **OR** distribution of nutrients **OR** cytoplasmic streaming throughout the extensive network **OR** multinucleation occurs due to nuclear divisions without cytoplasmic division    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ red blood cells lack a nucleus, which increases space for haemoglobin, maximising oxygen transport     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Skeletal muscle cells** have **many nuclei** because they must make many **copies of proteins** for contractions along the entire length of the cell, which may be **very long** ($\\checkmark$). **Phloem** sieve tube elements have **no nucleus** to allow for more space for transporting **sugars** more efficiently ($\\checkmark$). **Aseptate hyphae** have **many nuclei** throughout the cell to help it grow quickly and **distribute nutrients** ($\\checkmark$). **Red blood cells** have **no nuclei** at maturity for greater volume to **carry haemoglobin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    ***Note a table showing the comparisons would also be acceptable:***\n\n    | Cell | No. of nuclei | Relation to cell function | |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | Skeletal muscle | **many** | **for many copies of protein synthesis** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Phloem sieve tube elements | **none** | **to allow room for translocation** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Aseptate fungal hyphae | **many** | **to allow rapid transfer of nutrients** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Red blood cell | **none** | **to create more room for haemoglobin** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ fungal cell walls are composed of chitin, **WHEREAS** plant cell walls are composed of cellulose [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Fungal cell walls** are composed of **chitin**, while **plant cell walls** are composed of **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2852","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2852\n\nThe diagram shows three dividing eukaryotic cells (not drawn to scale).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI2852%20A2.2%20core%20MCQ.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2852 A2.2 core MCQ.jpeg\">\n\nWhich row correctly pairs each cell with its type?\n\n| | Cell X | Cell Y | Cell Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Palisade mesophyll | Yeast | Liver |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Liver | Palisade mesophyll | Yeast |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yeast | Liver | Palisade mesophyll |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Palisade mesophyll | Liver | Yeast |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Cell X** is undergoing budding, a process characteristic of unicellular fungi like yeast. The cell also has a cell wall, a large vacuole and no chloroplasts, reinforcing that this cell is indeed a fungal cell, such as yeast.\n\n**Cell Y** has no cell wall, no visible vacuoles and there are centrioles in the dividing cells, indicating that this is a dividing animal cell, such as a liver cell.\n\n**Cell Z** has is dividing into daughter cells, each of which has a cell wall, vacuole, and chloroplasts. The cell is also dividing evenly by laying down a cell plate between the cells, which will develop into the cell walls of the new cells. These are all features of plant cells, such as palisade mesophyll cells.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore the correct answer to this question."}]},{"name":"Conservation of Biodiversity","questions":[{"ref":"BI0901","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0901\n\nWhich definitions are matched to the correct form of diversity?\n\n| | Ecosystem diversity | Genetic diversity | Species diversity\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Different habitats, communities and ecological processes | The type of alleles in a population | Genetic variation within a species |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | The number of species and their abundance in an area | Phenotypic variation in a community | Genetic variability within a species |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Variety and abundance of different ecosystems in a geographical area| Genetic variability within a species or population | The number of species and their abundance in an area |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Different habitats, communities and ecological processes | The total number and type of epigenetic markers in a population | The variety of all life on Earth|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEcosystem diversity is defined as the variety and abundance of different ecosystems present in a geographical area. Genetic diversity is the genetic variability within a species or population. Species diversity is the number and relative abundance of species found in a specific area. Therefore **Choice C** is correct.\n :br**Choice A** is incorrect because genetic diversity does not only refer to the type of alleles in a population but the number of different alleles in a population. **Choice B**  is incorrect because phenotypic variation refers to differences in observable characteristics, not genetic diversity, and the definition of ecosystem diversity shown in row B is actually the definition for species diversity. **Choice D**  is incorrect as genetic diversity does not relate to the control of the expression of genes (epigenetics) within a population and the variety of all life on Earth is the definition for biodiversity."},{"ref":"BI0902","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0902\n\nWhich of the following provides evidence for a higher level of biodiversity now compared to any time in the past?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Changing levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The fossil record\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A decrease in the rate of evolution\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased levels of speciation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of organisms found via the fossil record for any era is much lower compared to the number of organisms found on Earth now and those that continue to be found, so **Choice B**  is correct. **Choice A**  is incorrect as changing levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide provide evidence for humans' impact on carbon dioxide levels. **Choices C** and **D**  are incorrect as evidence suggests that the rate of evolution and speciation are increasing, which provides evidence for increasing biodiversity."},{"ref":"BI0903","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0903\n\nWhich is **not** a cause of anthropogenic species extinction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overhunting\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deforestation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Introduction of new diseases\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Volcanic eruptions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnthropogenic species extinction is extinction caused by human activities. Volcanic eruptions are natural events and are not caused by human activities. However, they can still contribute to species extinction by releasing ash, gases, and lava that disrupt ecosystems and reduce biodiversity. **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\nWhen humans overhunt (**Choice A**) it can lead to a decline in populations and, in some cases, extinction. Deforestation (**Choice B**) is another human activity. The clearing of forests for purposes such as agriculture, logging or urban development, can destroy habitats and disrupt ecosystems, leading to extinction of species that rely on those habitats. When humans introduce new diseases to ecosystems (**Choice C**), either accidentally or deliberately, it can have devastating effects on native species and bring about extinction.  **Choices A**, **B**  and **C**  are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0904","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0904\n\nThe image shows changes in coverage to mixed dipterocarp forests in Southeast Asia over the last 100 years.\nWhich is not a factor that contributed to these changes?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0904_A4.2.png\" alt=\"BI0904_A4.2\">\n[Source: Adapted from San Jose. (2006, February 8). File: map of east asia.png - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 10, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Map_of_East_Asia.png. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building of new roads to mining sites\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Poaching of endangered species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Planting of rubber plantations\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building of hydroelectric dams","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe image shows the loss of forests in Southeast Asia over 100 years. Poaching of endangered species does not result in deforestation; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nBuilding new roads to mining sites involves clearing land to mine sites, so **Choice A**  is incorrect. Planting rubber plantations requires clearing forests to plant rubber trees, so **Choice C**  is incorrect.  Building hydroelectric dams can lead to flooding of forests and irreparable damage to trees, so **Choice D** is also incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0905","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0905\n\nWhich statement(s) are possible solutions to the biodiversity crisis?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Promoting rewilding projects |\n    | II. | Recycling and reusing products |\n    | III. | Increase meat consumption |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nRewilding projects attempt to restore and rehabilitate ecosystems by reintroducing native species and allowing natural processes to become re-established.  **Statement I** is therefore correct. Recycling and reusing products means fewer resources will be extracted from the Earth which is a process that can destroy natural habitats and lead to excessive landfill use, so **Statement II** is correct. **Choice C**  is therefore correct (Statement I and II).\n\nIncreasing meat consumption (**Statement III**) results in more land being cleared for animals such as cattle to be bred, decreasing biodiversity. **Choice D** is therefore incorrect as it includes this statement. **Choices A** and **B** are missing one of the possible solutions to the crisis and are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0906","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0906\n\nWhich is **not** a cause of the current biodiversity crisis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased urbanisation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The widespread use of human vaccines\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased air travel\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased transmission of diseases","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUrbanisation is not decreasing, it is increasing all over the world, which is contributing to the current biodiversity crisis. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nThe widespread use of vaccines has led to fewer people dying of diseases before reproductive age, contributing to worldwide population growth, which contributes to the current biodiversity crisis, so **Choice B** is incorrect. Increased air travel results in more pollution and makes it easier for diseases to spread worldwide which sometimes negatively impacts species, contributing to the biodiversity crisis, so **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0907","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0907\n\nHow does introducing new invasive species, such as Japanese knotweed (*Reynoutria japonica*), to ecosystems affect biodiversity?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0907.png\" alt=\"BI0907\">\n[Source: Adapted from Carter, W. (2019, September 11). File: reynoutria japonica in Brastad 1.jpg - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 10, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Reynoutria_japonica_in_Brastad_1.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases biodiversity because there are more species in the ecosystem\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreases biodiversity as the invasive species outcompetes native species for resources\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreases biodiversity as new plants encourage new insects and animals to immigrate to the ecosystem\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases biodiversity as the invasive species brings new pests into the ecosystem","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nInvasive species are species that arrive in an ecosystem to which they are not endemic to and they often lack natural population controls such as herbivores or predators.  They therefore sometimes outcompete native species for resources which can decrease biodiversity.  **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as introducing an invasive species usually reduces biodiversity. There are many reasons for this, including competition with native species, predation, herbivory, habitat alteration and the introduction of disease. **Choice C** is incorrect as although new plants may encourage new insects and plants to immigrate into an area, this would increase biodiversity, not decrease it.  **Choice D** is incorrect because although invasive species can bring new pests, these pests often reduce biodiversity by decimating native populations."},{"ref":"BI0908","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0908\n\nThe table shows the number of tree species in two similarly sized forests. Which statement about the two communities is correct?\n\n| | Tree species | Forest 1 | Forest 2\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Quercus alba* | 30 | 25 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Acer rubrum* | 12 | 25 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Fagus sylvatica*| 50 | 25 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Betula occidentalis* | 8 | 25|\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | **Total number of trees** | 100 | 100|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 2 has the same species richness as Forest 1\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 1 has a higher species evenness than Forest 2\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 1 has a higher species richness than Forest 2\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 2 has an equal species evenness to Forest 1","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSpecies richness is the number of different species present in an area. There are 4 species present in both forests so **Choice A** is correct.\n\nSpecies evenness is the relative abundance of different species in an area. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as Forest 2 has a higher species evenness than Forest 1 as there is an equal number of each tree species. **Choice C** is incorrect as species richness is the same in both forests."},{"ref":"BI0909","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0909\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows the changes to mixed dipterocarp forests in Southeast Asia over 100 years. Researchers have concluded that the changes are mainly due to anthropogenic factors.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0904bi.png\" alt=\"BI0904bi\">\n[Source: Adapted from San Jose. (2006, February 8). File: map of east asia.png - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 10, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Map_of_East_Asia.png. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Distinguish between the forest coverage in 1900 and 2000  [1 mark]\n\n2. State the definition for ‘anthropogenic’.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain two possible anthropogenic reasons for differences in forest coverage between 1900 and 2000.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest what evidence scientists would need to collect from the forests to provide evidence for a biodiversity crisis in South East Asia.    [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest why it might be important to increase biodiversity in the area.    [1 mark]\n\n6. Conservationists in the area have started a reforestation project in the area, in an attempt to restore the forest to 1900 coverage levels. Discuss what effects this would have on biodiversity.    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is less forest coverage in the year 2000 **COMPARED TO** the year 1900, **OR** there is more forest coverage in the year 1900 to the year 2000     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThere is **less forest** coverage in the year 2000 compared to the year 1900 ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental **OR** ecological change caused by human activity     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe term anthropogenic refers to an **environmental change** caused, directly or indirectly, by **human activity** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation for agriculture OR urbanisation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollution from factories OR vehicles weakening trees     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human population growth resulting in more trees being cut to build infrastructure **OR** provide fuels **OR** make resources such as paper     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spread of pests **OR** diseases **OR** introduction of invasive species causes damage to trees     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forest fires caused by human activities     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mining **OR** oil drilling **OR** other resource extraction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specific examples related to climate change such as changing rainfall patterns **OR** increased frequency of extreme weather *(do **NOT** allow climate change without a specific example)*     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTwo anthropogenic reasons for the loss of forests between 1900 and 2000 are **deforestation** to grow **crops**  ($\\checkmark$) and **human population growth** resulting in more trees being cut down for the **building of infrastructure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surveys of plant and animal species in forested **AND** deforested areas to compare species richness **OR** abundance **OR** diversity over time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analysis of species population trends     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amassing data from citizen scientists on species richness **OR** abundance, **OR** diversity     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTo provide evidence for the biodiversity crisis in South East Asia, scientists would need to conduct surveys on **species diversity in the area over time**. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain food chains **OR** webs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ trees carry out carbon capture **OR** release oxygen into the atmosphere **OR** climate change mitigation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevent extinction of organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promote tourism in the area **OR** aesthetic reasons     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ slow down the spread of zoonotic diseases (as loss of habitat means that animals will live closer together and closer to humans)     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potential medical resources **OR** webs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ preservation of cultural heritage of indigenous communities     [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ethical responsibility to preserve biodiversity     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIt is important to increase biodiversity in the area, as **trees** carry out **carbon capture** ($\\checkmark$) which contributes to mitigating climate change and promotes **tourism** in the area, which would **boost the economy** of South East Asia ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **three** of the following (**must** state what effect it has on biodiversity for mark):\n\n    ***Increase biodiversity because***...\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides more habitats     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides places for organisms to hide from predators **OR** hunters         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents floods **OR** landslides due to increased transpiration     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides more food for organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ encourages the establishment of complex ecological interactions **OR** an example such as an increase in number of pollinators for plant reproduction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases species richness     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic diversity **OR** gene flow     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Decrease biodiversity because***...\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ invasive species are introduced     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ native species are displaced during deforestation         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is competition between newly planted trees and existing vegetation         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species competition is influenced by edge effects     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soil disturbance during planting impacts other species negatively     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Have little effect on biodiversity because***...\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more than one approach is needed to change biodiversity in the area     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ limited species of trees **OR** monoculture are planted         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tree species are not selected for their suitability to the area     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reforested areas are isolated **OR** lack connections to the existing forest     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they are not monitored **OR** managed properly     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reforested areas are too close to human settlements, potentially leading to conflict     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ trees are planted but animals are not reintroduced    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nReforesting may increase biodiversity in the area as it will provide more habitats for organisms ($\\checkmark$) and provide organisms with a place to hide from hunters or predators ($\\checkmark$). However, it may decrease biodiversity if only limited species of trees are replanted in the area ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0910","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0910\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows the extinct North Island giant moa (*Dinornis novaezealandiae*), a flightless bird that was native to New Zealand. The Moa became extinct in the last 600 years, during the current sixth mass extinction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0910.png\" alt=\"BI0910\">\n[Source: Adapted from Michael, B. H. (2012). File: Euryapteryx.jpg - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 21, 2023 from URL https://\ncommons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Euryapteryx.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. State the key difference between the current sixth mass extinction and the previous mass extinctions.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. State one possible cause of the extinction of the Moa.  [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Outline the physical evidence scientists have collected indicating that the Moa existed.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. In the past, scientists thought that the kiwi (*Apteryx haastii*), a flightless bird based in New Zealand, was the Moa’s closest living relative.  However, this has been disproven. State what evidence scientists may have found to disprove this relationship.  [1 mark]\n\n    3. The Moa’s closest living relatives are the South American Tinamou (*Tinamus major*), which have the ability to fly. Explain why these birds are less likely to go extinct than the Moa.  [1 mark]\n\n3. Evidence suggests that the Tinamou and Moa shared a common ancestor, but they evolved independently from each other when they were geographically isolated millions of years ago. Explain the evolution of the Tinamou with reference to natural selection.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Another animal at risk of extinction is the Javan rhinoceros (*Rhinoceros sondaicus*), shown in the image below. There are thought to be fewer than 80 in the world. Suggest two methods scientists could use to prevent its extinction, with a reason for each.    [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0910b.png\" alt=\"BI0910b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Nelson, S. (2012, July 20). Category: Rhinoceros Sondaicus. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 21, 2023 from URL https://\ncommons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category: Rhinoceros_sondaicus. Copyright under CC BY 3.0\n]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sixth mass extinction is mostly anthropogenic **OR** mainly caused by human activity **WHEREAS** the previous ones were (primarily) caused by natural events     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sixth mass extinction event is **FASTER** than the previous ones\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe current sixth mass extinction is mostly **anthropogenic**, whereas the **previous** ones were primarily caused by **natural events** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hunting    for food         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fragility of eggs means that they were prone to damage\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ introduction of predators **OR** Polynesian dogs **OR** invasive **OR** non-native species\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitat due to human activity\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nOne possible cause of the extinction of the Moa is them being **hunted** ($\\checkmark$) for food by humans.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rock art **OR** artefacts depicting Moa\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA analysis from Moa remains         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ isotopic analysis from Moa remains\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n **Fossils** ($\\checkmark$) provide physical evidence for the existence of the Moa.\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dissimilar DNA **OR** amino acid sequences     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detailed morphological comparisons show notable differences in skeletal structures **OR** body size, **OR** other anatomical features\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ phylogenetic analysis placed the Kiwi and Moa on separate branches of the phylogenetic tree         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossil evidence **OR** fossil dating has shown different ancestral lineages\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ another species discovered that appears more closely related to the Moa\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nScientists may have found that the Kiwi had **dissimilar DNA sequences** ($\\checkmark$) to the Moa, and more similar DNA sequences to another organism.\n\n    3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Tinamous can fly so can escape predators **OR** hunters more easily    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Tinamous can build their nests high up in trees so that their young are less vulnerable\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTinamous are less likely to go extinct than the Moa, as they can **build their nests high** up in trees so that their **young are less vulnerable** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic isolation of the Tinamou **OR** Moa lineage meant that different populations were subjected to varying environmental conditions         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutations lead to genetic  variation\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic variation may have resulted in various physical and behavioural traits\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ certain traits among Tinamous **OR** Moas would have been more advantageous than others\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ for example, camouflage **OR** flight ability **OR** specific feeding adaptations **OR** any other suitable example of advantageous trait          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ therefore greater chance of survival **OR** more organisms with these advantageous traits survived to reproductive age\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduced and passed on alleles\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ repeated for many generations\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe **geographic isolation** of the Tinamou lineage meant that different populations were subjected to **different environmental conditions** ($\\checkmark$).  **Random mutations** in the populations resulted in **genetic variation** ($\\checkmark$), which may have resulted in **different physical and behavioural traits** ($\\checkmark$). Some traits among Tinamous, such as camouflage or flight abilities, would have been more **advantageous** than others ($\\checkmark$), resulting in **higher survival rates**($\\checkmark$). Therefore, these organisms with advantageous traits were able to **pass on their alleles** to **successive generations** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ breeding programmes **OR** carefully selecting individuals for breeding, **REASON** to increase numbers of rhino **OR** population size **OR** genetic diversity **OR** increase chance of survival of offspring **OR** produce offspring that can later be released into the wild; **OR** freeze eggs and sperm **OR** gametes **REASON** to preserve genetic diversity **OR** to use IVF and a closely related rhino species to produce more Javan rhinos         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat protection **OR** restoration projects, **REASON** to provide safe spaces for rhino **OR** to provide enough resources for rhino in natural area\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place rhinos into zoos **OR** nature reserves, **REASON** to protect from poaching, **OR** hunting, **OR** habitat loss, **OR** for research **OR** for monitoring **OR** public awareness **OR** education **OR** funding **OR** to have animals that can be released into the wild after catastrophic events, **OR** severe disease, in natural habitat\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ change laws to protect rhinos, **REASON** to reduce hunting **OR** poaching **OR** export of rhino horn **OR** use of rhino horn\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence DNA of as many Javan rhinos as possible **REASON** to preserve genetic diversity **OR** different alleles          [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nScientists could prevent the extinction of the Javan rhinoceros by introducing **breeding programmes** to **increase** the rhino **population** ($\\checkmark$) or start **habitat restoration projects** to provide enough area with enough **resources to support** the rhino ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0911","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0911\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline why scientists must have several approaches to conserve biodiversity.   [4 marks]\n\n2. There are approximately 80 Javan Rhinoceros (*Rhinoceros sondaicus*) in the world. Discuss the environmental and economic implications the Indonesian government must consider before volunteering the Javan Rhinoceros to the EDGE programme.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain why rapid human population growth is linked to the current biodiversity crisis.   [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no single approach is sufficient **OR** establishing conservation areas is often effective, but without reintroduction of lost species it will have limited success **OR** other suitable example showing that when one approach is used, another is required for effective conservation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different species **OR** ecosystems require different measures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ not all methods are affordable for different countries **OR** organisations, **OR** governments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity encompasses a vast range of organisms, habitats, and ecosystems with different interdependencies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ socio-economic factors need to be considered to ensure that conservation efforts take into account local communities' needs and interests   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ongoing changes in biodiversity may mean that strategies need to be adapted accordingly      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple approaches can create a more resilient and effective conservation strategy   [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Scientists must have multiple approaches to conserve biodiversity as **no single approach** is sufficient ($\\checkmark$), different species require **different methods** ($\\checkmark$) and not all methods are **affordable for different governments** ($\\checkmark$).  In addition, biodiversity encompasses a vast range of organisms and habitats with **different interdependencies** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*implications must be stated*):\n\n    ***Environmental negative considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruption of current food chains **OR** webs   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Environmental positive considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ restoration of food webs **OR** chains  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitats **OR** forests will have to be restored     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Javan Rhinoceros is a keystone species, so conserving this species would indirectly protect the ecosystem      [1 mark]\n\n    ***Economic negative considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be expensive to put rhinos in breeding programmes **OR** zoos, **OR** EDGE program would require financial investments in research **OR** monitoring, **OR** conservation initiatives            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ land that has been used for urbanisation **OR** businesses **OR** agriculture  will have to be reforested            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ local communities might rely on the same resources as the rhinoceros, **OR** local communities' livelihoods might be impacted by conservation measures       [1 mark]\n\n    ***Economic positive considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase tourism in the area      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may improve international cooperation **OR** trade agreements, **OR** foreign investments      [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Before volunteering the Javan rhinoceros to the EDGE programme, economic considerations must be taken into account.  For example, protecting the Javan rhinoceros would likely **increase tourism** in the area ($\\checkmark$) as people will travel to the country to see the rhinos. However, it can be **expensive** to put rhinos into **breeding programmes** ($\\checkmark$) or **reforest** land that has been **urbanised** ($\\checkmark$), and this may be **unaffordable** for the Indonesian government. A positive environmental consideration is that **reforestation will increase biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$) in the area. However, it may also result in **disruption of current, stable food chains** ($\\checkmark$)in the area.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more hunting **OR** poaching **OR** for food **OR** resources, which endangers animals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitats due to urbanisation **OR** agriculture [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased agriculture can lead to a monoculture **OR** a loss in diversity  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation for building infrastructure **OR** resources such as paper **OR** agriculture   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased pollution due to increased vehicles **OR** global transport **OR** industry **OR** factories can cause damage to organisms **OR** contribute to global warming   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased use of fertilisers leading to eutrophication      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of diseases to plants **OR** animals due to increased global transport [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased risk of spread of invasive species due to increased global transport     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of pests due to increased air travel damaging agriculture **OR** forest ecosystems     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Rapid human population growth is linked to the current biodiversity crisis because as the global population grows, there is an increased need for resources which can result in **loss of habitat** due to **increased agriculture** ($\\checkmark$), an increase in the use of **fertilisers** which can result in **eutrophication** ($\\checkmark$) and more **hunting** of animals ($\\checkmark$) which lowers animal populations.  An increase in agriculture which utilises **monocultures** lowers species diversity in the area ($\\checkmark$).  An increase in the human population is also correlated with **increased global transport**, which can result in increased **pollution**, which contributes to **global warming** ($\\checkmark$), which can have a detrimental effect on ecosystems and also can result in an increased **spread of invasive species** ($\\checkmark$) and **pests** ($\\checkmark$), which lowers biodiversity within ecosystems."},{"ref":"BI1458","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1458\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nMountain gorillas (*Gorilla beringei beringei*)  were reclassified from \"Critically Endangered\" to \"Endangered\" by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) in November 2018. This change in status was a significant conservation success story, reflecting the positive impact of concerted efforts by governments, conservation organisations, and local communities to protect and increase the population of this subspecies of the eastern gorilla. \n\nThe table below shows the changes in the number of mountain gorillas from 2010 to 2018.\n\n| Year | Number of gorillas |\n\n| :-: | :-: |\n\n| 2010 | 480 |\n\n| 2016 | 604 |\n\n| 2018 | 1004 |\n\n1.  Calculate the percentage increase in the number of gorillas from 2010 to 2018.  [1 mark]\n\n2.  Although the mountain gorilla is rarely seen in zoos, another gorilla subspecies, the western lowland gorilla (*Gorilla gorilla gorilla*) is. Explain how zoos play a critical role in the conservation of endangered species. [3 marks]\n\nStudies have shown that the invasive plant species *Sericostachys scandens* affects forest composition and may affect the Grauer’s gorilla (*Gorilla beringei graueri*) recovery in Congo. \n\n3.  Outline two precautions that should be taken before considering biological control of invasive plants such as these.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 109.16% **OR** 109.2%    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    $\\dfrac{(1004 - 480)}{480} \\times 100\\%$                                                 \n\n    = :answer[109.16%] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.  Any **three** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal husbandry facilities to help prevent death from disease or injury    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain detailed records **OR** pedigrees **OR** known studbooks to track genetic lineage used to choose breeding pairs to maintain genetic diversity **OR** minimise inbreeding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exchange animals between different institutions to introduce new genetic diversity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ captive breeding **OR** artificial insemination **OR** in vitro fertilisation increases chance of successful breeding **OR** means they do not have to find a mate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  reintroduction programs **OR** release animals into wild    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scientists **OR** researchers can study the species up close **OR** in detail for greater understanding of how to protect it *in-situ*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ educational programs to educate the public about the importance of animal conservation    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Zoos facilitate the **exchange** of **animals** between institutions ($\\checkmark$) and maintain **detailed records** to track **genetic lineage** used to choose **breeding pairs** to introduce **new genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$).  **Artificial insemination** also increases the **success rate** of breeding ($\\checkmark$) endangered species. Moreover, zoos are instrumental in **reintroducing programs** that release **animals** back into the **wild**, directly contributing to restoring **natural populations** ($\\checkmark$) and ecosystems.\n\n3.  Any **two** of the following \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ research to make sure control organism specifically targets invasive species **OR** does not affect native species **OR** does not damage ecosystem **OR** habitats **OR** food chains    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conditions in the target environment should support the survival of the control species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control species should have a natural control **OR** predator    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control species should not spread outside the targeted area **OR** should not become an invasive species themselves    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long-term monitoring plans to observe the effects of the biocontrol species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ possible socio-economic impacts **OR** consider ethical **OR** cultural **OR** social implications of control species should be assessed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plan should be developed in case species need to be controlled **OR** eradicated [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Before using biological control, it is crucial to conduct **thorough research** to ensure that the control organism **specifically targets** the **invasive** species **without impacting native** species or the **ecosystem** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **conditions** in the target environment must **support** the **survival** of the **control** species ($\\checkmark$)\n\n, ensuring it can effectively reduce the invasive population **without becoming invasive** itself($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1599","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1599\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nConservationists must consider the size and arrangement of in situ nature parks when creating and managing them. The diagram below shows the theoretical design of three sets of nature reserves.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/nature%20reserve%20design%20BI1599ii.png\" alt=\"nature reserve design BI1599ii\">\n\nFor each pair of designs labelled A and B, compare the two and determine which design is better suited for the long-term preservation and management of animal populations. For each comparison, provide a reason to justify your choice. [3 marks]","markScheme":"**AI vs BI**\n\nany **one** of the following:\n\n*AI is better than BI sincee a larger reserve*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ supports more species **OR** a wider variety of species [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ensures a more stable food chain **OR** provides more resources [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reduction in the chance of inbreeding **OR** extinction [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ can better buffer species against environmental changes because it includes a variety of microhabitats **OR** climates [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ more likely to support keystone species, critical for structure of community [1 mark]\n\n**AII vs BII**\n\nany **one** of the following:\n\n*AII is better than BII since a less fragmented reserve*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ has better connectivity for breeding **OR** gene flow [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ has reduced edge effect so fewer disturbances **OR** predators **OR** invasive species **OR** reduces habitat degradation preserving the core habitat for sensitive species [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ provides enough resources to sustain viable populations in the long term [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ might reduce human-wildlife conflict, as animals less likely to stray into human-populated areas [1 mark]\n\n**AIII vs BIII**\n\nany **one** of the following:\n\n*AIII is better than BIII since corridors*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ facilitate movement of animals **OR** migration which is crucial for breeding **OR** finding food **OR** escaping predators **OR** for maintaining genetic diversity [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ can make the reserve more accessible for maintenance and management purposes [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**AI is better** than BI because the **larger reserve** area in AI can **support more species**, as it provides **more resources** ($\\checkmark$).\n**AII is better** than BII because it is **less fragmented**. This **minimises** the **edge effect**, which **reduces exposure** to threats such as **poaching**, **predation**, and **invasive species**. ($\\checkmark$).\n**AIII is better** than BIII because the **corridors** in AIII **facilitate animal movement**, which is essential for **maintaining genetic diversity**, accessing **resources**, and **escaping predators**. ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1608","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1608\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOrganisms that are better adapted to their environment are more likely to survive and thrive.\n\n1.  Describe how the skin and mucous membranes provide both physical and chemical defence against pathogens.    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Explain the importance of concentration gradients and the mechanisms that regulate them in the human ventilation system.    [5 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss to what extent the fossil record provides evidence for evolution.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **four**  of the following (*answer must include **one** point from physical defence and **one** point from chemical defence to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Physical defence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skin provides a physical barrier **OR** is composed of multiple tissue layers preventing pathogens from entering the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skin continuously sheds dead cells which removes any pathogens on the surface    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in skin are held together by tight junctions reinforcing the barrier function **OR** preventing pathogens from passing between cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus produced by mucous membranes can trap pathogens     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus containing trapped pathogens is either expelled by the body or swallowed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cilia on mucous membrane surfaces move pathogens **OR** foreign particles out of the body    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Chemical defence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sebaceous glands in the skin secrete sebum, which lowers the pH of the skin and inhibits the growth of bacteria and fungi    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus contains lysozyme **OR** antimicrobial enzymes which break down bacterial cell walls    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweat contains antimicrobials **OR** acids which inhibit growth of pathogens     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The **skin** provides **physical defence** by forming a **barrier** that **prevents pathogens** from **entering** the **body** ($\\checkmark$). It also provides **chemical defence** against pathogens by having **sebaceous glands** that **secrete sebum**, which **lowers** the **pH** of the skin and so **inhibits** the **growth** of **bacteria** and **fungi** on the skin ($\\checkmark$). Mucous membranes line the internal surfaces of some organs and body cavities and secrete mucus, which can **trap pathogens** ($\\checkmark$). The mucus is then **expelled** by the body or **swallowed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steeper the concentration gradient, the greater the rate of diffusion, allowing for faster movement of molecules from areas of high concentration to areas of low concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inhaled air in alveoli has a higher concentration of oxygen than oxygen-depleted blood in the capillaries surrounding the alveoli **OR** blood has a higher concentration of carbon dioxide than the air in the alveoli. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration gradients facilitate diffusion of oxygen from alveoli into blood **OR** carbon dioxide from blood into alveoli, which provides cells with oxygen for respiration and removes waste carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large network of capillaries surrounding alveoli rapidly transport oxygen away from the lungs **OR** rapidly transport carbon dioxide from the body to the lungs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the processes of inhalation and exhalation ensure that there is a constant flow of air in and out of lungs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors in carotid artery **OR** aorta **OR** medulla monitor blood pH levels **OR** blood carbon dioxide concentrations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors send signals to respiratory centre in medulla oblongata OR brain stem     [1 mark]\n\n    respiratory centre **OR** medulla oblongata controls breathing rate **OR** depth of breathing to maintain concentration gradient of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    Efficient gas exchange in the lungs relies on maintaining concentration gradients, where **inhaled air** in the **alveoli** has a **higher concentration** of **oxygen** than **oxygen-depleted blood** and a **lower concentration** of **carbon dioxide than that in** the surrounding **capillaries** ($\\checkmark$). This gradient allows **oxygen** to **diffuse from** the **alveoli** into the **blood** and **carbon dioxide from** the **blood into** the **alveoli** ($\\checkmark$). An extensive **network of capillaries** surrounding the alveoli rapidly **transports oxygen away** from the **lungs** and **carbon dioxide to** the lungs ($\\checkmark$). The processes of **inhalation** and **exhalation** ensure a **constant flow** of **air** in and out of the lungs, **maintaining** these **gradients** ($\\checkmark$). **Chemoreceptors** in the **carotid artery** and **aorta** monitor **blood pH** ($\\checkmark$), sending **signals to** the **respiratory centre** to **adjust breathing rate** and **depth** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** answer from ‘provides evidence’ and **one** answer from ‘doesn’t provide’ evidence to gain full marks*):\n\n    \n\n    ***Provides evidence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some organisms have very good fossil records, which allow their evolution to be tracked easily, e.g. horses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sequence of fossils that appear in layers of rock matches the sequence in which they would be expected to evolve     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ older organisms appear in deeper rock layers **OR** strata, and more recently evolved organisms appear in the upper layers    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are fossils of extinct organisms with transitional forms **OR** fossils of extinct organisms that have features that link different stages of evolution, e.g. Archaeopteryx, bird-like dinosaurs that support the hypothesis that birds evolved from reptiles        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record shows evidence of mass extinction events **OR** there are many more extinct species than living ones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils show evidence of adaptive radiation **OR** divergence of different organisms from a common ancestor **OR** speciation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record shows evidence of vestigial structures that are no longer present in current organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is fossil evidence for the existence of the last universal common ancestor (LUCA) near hydrothermal vents        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils provide chronological evidence that supports the theory of gradualism in evolution, where small changes accumulate over long periods    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossilised remains of early hominids show a clear progression in brain size **OR** bipedalism, supporting human evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Doesn’t provide evidence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossil record is incomplete, with many gaps **OR** some fossil species appear abruptly in the fossil record without any precursors    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some species in the fossil record appear not to have changed much over long periods of time        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution **OR** fossils of unrelated organisms with similar features makes it difficult to track evolution accurately    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soft-bodied organisms are rarely fossilised, leading to an incomplete record that skews our understanding of evolutionary history    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ process of fossilisation is rare and biased towards certain environments, meaning that the fossil record may not accurately represent all ecosystems and organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The fossil record supports evolution because some organisms have **well-documented fossil records** that allow their evolutionary changes to be **tracked easily** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **sequence** in which **fossils appear in rock layers** matches the sequence in which they would be **expected to evolve** ($\\checkmark$). For example, **older** organisms are found in **deeper layers** of rocks, while more **recently evolved organisms** are found in the **upper layers** ($\\checkmark$). There are also fossils of extinct organisms with **transitional features**, such as Archaeopteryx, a bird-like dinosaur, which supports the hypothesis that birds evolved from reptiles and provides **evolutionary links** between present and extinct species ($\\checkmark$). However, there are **gaps** in the fossil record ($\\checkmark$), some species appear not to have **changed over long periods of time** ($\\checkmark$), and **convergent evolution** can make it difficult to track evolution accurately ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1684","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1684\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe Eurasian beaver (*Castor fiber*) has been described as a species that promotes biodiversity. Beavers are herbivores that feed on freshwater plants and tree bark. They use their sharp front teeth to fell trees and use the logs and branches to build dams that create pools in which they can build their lodge. A beaver lodge is a construction in which they nest and raise their young, providing protection from predators.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI1684%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1684 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Jerzy Strzelecki, retrieved July 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Castor_fiber_07(js),_Narew_River,\n\n_Poland.jpg, copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest ways in which the reintroduction of beavers can increase biodiversity. [4 marks]\n\nThe effect of introducing beavers on the species diversity of birds and bats was studied in four areas: on river banks, one without any beaver activity and one where beavers are present, and then ponds, again, one without beaver activity and one where beavers were present. The graph below shows the calculated Simpson’s reciprocal index of Diversity for each area.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI1684%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1684 fig2\">\n\n2. State two necessary measurements to calculate Simpson’s reciprocal index of diversity. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in the species diversity of bats in the pond after the introduction of the beaver. [2 marks]\n\nBeavers became extinct in the UK over 400 years ago. Reintroduction programmes are now being carried out in various regions of the UK.\n\n4. Suggest **three** anthropogenic activities that could have led to the extinction of the beavers in the UK. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ many different habitats **OR** niches are created by beaver activity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dams create pools that slow the run-off of rivers, benefiting plant rooting [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dams generate wetlands that harbour insect **OR** invertebrate species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this promotes a food chain with consumers such as birds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pools are habitats for aquatic plants **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ felling trees allows sunlight onto the forest floor, benefiting ground-level plants and shrubs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fallen trees are a source of food for microorganisms **OR** detritivores [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The activity of beavers **creates many habitats** for other organisms ($\\checkmark$), with an overall increase in biodiversity. For example, the felling of trees opens up patches of ground to sunlight, **promoting ground-level vegetation** ($\\checkmark$). The pools created by beaver dams **attract insects** ($\\checkmark$) and the consumers (such as birds) that **feed on those insects** ($\\checkmark$). Dams slow the run-off of rainwater which has a **beneficial effect on plant roots** which are not washed away by fast-flowing water ($\\checkmark$). The pools also attract **fish species and aquatic plants** that can oxygenate the pond water ($\\checkmark$); beavers do not eat fish, so there is no harm to the fish from them.\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ total number of different species in the area **OR** species richness **AND** total number of organisms of each species **OR** species evenness [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Two measurements needed to calculate Simpson’s Diversity Index are the **total number of species in the area** and the **total number of organisms of each species**($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage increase = {(final volume - initial volume)/initial volume} $\\times$ 100\\% [1 mark]\n\n **OR** {(0.81-0.51) / 0.51} $\\times$ 100\\%\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 58.8\\% (allow numbers between 52 and 64%) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Percentage increase = **{(0.81$-$0.51) / 0.51} x 100\\%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **58.8** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hunting for fur **OR** castoreum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat destruction **OR** the draining of wetlands **OR** other hydrological changes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation **OR** logging **OR** increase in agricultural land [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water pollution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fragmentation of landscape [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ urbanisation **OR** industrial development **OR** road construction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ introduction of alien species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ trade **OR** travel leading to introduction of disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use of pesticides **OR** herbicides **OR** agricultural chemicals [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The extinction of beavers in the UK may have been caused by **overhunting** for their valuable **fur** and **castoreum** ($\\checkmark$), combined with **extensive habitat destruction** due to the **draining of wetlands** ($\\checkmark$) and **deforestation** for **agricultural expansion** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **introduction of non-native species** ($\\checkmark$) and **diseases**, through international **travel**, ($\\checkmark$) further contributed to their decline."},{"ref":"BI2323","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2323\n\nWhich species are prioritised for conservation in the EDGE of Existence programme?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are genetically distinct from other living species, and are in danger of extinction.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are evolutionarily distinct, and are found in vulnerable ecosystems.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are closely related to keystone species, and are in danger of extinction.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are keystone species, and are found in vulnerable ecosystems.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the EDGE of Existence program highlights and focuses on species that are evolutionarily distinct with few living relatives and, therefore, represent a disproportionately high level of genetic diversity. Additionally, the EDGE of Existence program only prioritises species on the endangered and vulnerable IUCN lists, which are, therefore, in danger of extinction.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the EDGE of Existence program does not use vulnerable ecosystems as a criterion. It focuses solely on species that are evolutionarily distinct and threatened with extinction.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because while keystone species play a crucial role in maintaining ecosystem structure and function, they are not necessarily evolutionarily distinct. The EDGE programme does not prioritise species based on their ecological role.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because neither keystone species nor vulnerable ecosystems are criteria for the EDGE of Existence program."},{"ref":"BI2378","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2378\n\nThe graph shows the global human population since 1700 CE, with a projection made to 2086.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/QuesEdit2.png\" alt=\"QuesEdit2\">\n\nAccording to the projection, what will be the percentage population increase between 2000 and 2050 CE?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 27%\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 38%\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 62%\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 88%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the global human population in 2000 was 6.0 billion, projected to increase to 9.7 billion by 2050. \\\nThe increase is:\n9.7 billion − 6.0 billion = 3.7 billion\n\nTo calculate the percentage increase:\\\nPercentage increase = ($\\dfrac{\\ce{increase}}{\\ce{population in 2000}}) \\times 100$%\n\n= ($\\dfrac{\\ce{3.7 billion}}{\\ce{6.0 billion}}) \\times 100$% = 61.67%\n\nAll the choices are stated to 2 significant figures, so rounding this answer to two significant figures gives **62%**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/AnsEdit2.png\" alt=\"AnsEdit2\">\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because they all contain calculation errors."},{"ref":"BI2714","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2714\n\nBelow is a list of conservation strategies:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Establishing a zoo to breed endangered species in controlled environments |\n    | II. | National parks and protected areas where species live in their natural habitat |\n    | III. | Seed banks that store genetic material from endangered plant species |\n\nWhich of the statements describes an example of in-situ conservation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because a zoo is an example of ex-situ conservation (when species are protected outside of their natural habitat). \\\n**Statement II** is true because a National park is an example of in-situ conservation, focusing on protecting species in their natural habitats.\\\n**Statement III** is false because a seed bank is an example of ex-situ conservation.\n\n**Choice B** is therefore correct as it only gives the true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2715","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2715\n\nThe following image shows the restoration of an old quarry site into a rehabilitated park.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2715.png\" alt=\"BI2715\">\n[Source: Tzeferis, P. (2016, March 11). *Qyarry Rehabillitation. Wikipedia*. Retrieved April 30, 2025, from: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Quarry_Rehabillitation.JPG. CC BY-SA 4.0\n]\n\nWhat term describes this process?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rewilding\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reclamation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sustainable development\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ In-situ conservation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because reclamation is the process of restoring disturbed land, such as an old quarry, to a functional ecosystem or other land use (e.g., a park). This often involves soil restoration, planting vegetation, and reconstructing habitats.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because rewilding refers to the restoration of ecosystems by reintroducing native species and minimising human intervention. While rewilding can occur in some restoration projects, quarry rehabilitation typically involves more active human efforts.  \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because sustainable development refers to using resources in a way that meets present needs without compromising the needs of future generations. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because in-situ conservation focuses on protecting species in their natural habitats. This term does not describe the process of rehabilitating a disturbed land area, such as a quarry."},{"ref":"BI2716","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2716\n\nWhich of the following has/have directly contributed to biodiversity loss?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The overhunting of African elephants for their ivory |\n    | II. | The expansion of cities like São Paulo, Brazil |\n    | III. | The spread of the invasive zebra mussels in North America through ballast water from ships |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because overhunting is one cause of the biodiversity crisis. \\\n**Statement II** is true because urbanisation is a cause of the biodiversity crisis. \\\n**Statement III** is true because the introduction/spread of pests, diseases and alien species due to global transport are causes of the biodiversity crisis.\n\nTherefore, **Choice D** is the correct answer as **Statements I, II** and **III** are true."},{"ref":"BI2808","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2808\n\nThe electric blue tarantula (*Chilobrachys natanicharum*, pictured below) is a newly-listed (2023) species found in the mangrove swamps of south-western Thailand. The vivid blue colour is created by nanostructures on the spider’s hairs that produce an iridescent effect.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2808.png\" alt=\"BI2808\">\n\nThe electric blue tarantula *Chilobrachys natanicharum*\n\n[Source: Chomphuphuang, Narin *et al* (2024 March, 28), File: *Chilobrachys natanicharum2* [image]. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 2025 April, 30 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chilobrachys_natanicharum2.jpg. CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhich statement about the electric blue tarantula is most likely to be correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula would be able to mate successfully with another tarantula found in Thailand, *Chilobrachys huahini*, and produce fertile offspring.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula has recently evolved from darker-coloured species of tarantula.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula was previously classified in the class Insecta (insects).\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula has existed for a long time, but was not discovered until recently.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because, like the vast majority of animal species that live on Earth today, this species had been unknown to humans. It has existed in remote areas away from human habitation, in a niche that makes it a rare global species. As such, it was not discovered until 2023, when human intervention to prevent habitat loss to palm oil farming identified the species. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because *Chilobrachys huahini* is a distinct species; only members of the same species can reproduce successfully and produce fertile offspring. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because there is no evidence of the recent emergence of this species; it is more likely to have been undiscovered for a long time. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because all spiders, including the tarantula, belong to the class *Arachnida* (arachnids). The key difference is the number of pairs of legs; insects have 3 pairs, whereas arachnids have 4 pairs."},{"ref":"BI2809","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2809\n\nThe image shows Aotearoa’s (New Zealand) North Island giant moa (*Dinornis novaezealandiae*), which became extinct in the period 1300 - 1500 A.D.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2809.png\" alt=\"BI2809\">\n[Source: Adapted from Frederick William Frohawk (16 July 1861 - 10 December 1946). File: *Dinornis novaezealandiae* [image]. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 2025 April 30 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinornis_novaezealandiae.png. Public domain]\n\nWhich of the following factors was **not** a contributing factor to the extinction of the giant moa during that period?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Habitat loss caused by human encroachment\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anthropogenic climate change\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hunting\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Predation by non-human species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct (not a factor in the moa’s extinction) because this is an example of anthropogenic extinction, but not a direct result of climate change. In the period 1300-1500 A.D., human-caused climate change had not yet occurred; such climate changes began to occur in the mid-20th century and have continued to the present. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect (a factor in the moa’s extinction) because human forest clearance would have taken place to create living and farming space for humans, their crops and livestock. This will have reduced the moa’s habitat area, impacting its population.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect (a factor in the moa’s extinction) hunting by humans was the single largest factor impacting the moa’s population. They were hunted mainly as a food source for the early settlers of Aotearoa. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect (a factor in the moa’s extinction) because the moa was not an apex predator; the Haast’s eagle (*Hieraaetus moorei*) was a natural predator of the moa, so it played a role in reducing the moa’s population size."},{"ref":"BI2810","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2810\n\nWhat is the main cause of Southeast Asia's loss of dipterocarp forest ecosystems?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plantations of palm oil (*Elaeis guineensis*)\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fungal disease affecting trees\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nutrient depletion in the soil\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anthropogenic climate change","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because palm oil plantations now cover large areas previously inhabited by dipterocarp species. Palm oil is used widely in the food and cosmetics industries.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, whilst fungal disease does occur in all natural rainforests, it has not caused widespread loss of dipterocarp species. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because soil nutrient depletion will have occurred, but as a result of biodiversity loss, not a cause of it. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, whilst anthropogenic (human-caused) climate change is a cause of ecosystem loss, it is not the main cause in this case."},{"ref":"BI2811","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2811\n\nWhich is the most significant factor in the loss of global biodiversity being experienced in the 21st century?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Industrialisation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Human population growth\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Invasive plant species\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deforestation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the rapid growth in human population (which has included a doubling in the period 1960-2020) has put pressure on all the Earth’s resources that humans rely upon. In doing so, habitat loss has had a major negative effect on thousands of species across all five kingdoms. \\\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect; whilst they are all factors contributing to global biodiversity loss, they are not the overarching cause. The question asks for the ‘most significant factor’, which is human population growth."},{"ref":"BI2812","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2812\n\nWhich approach, carried out by conservationists in zoos and botanical gardens, defines the transition between *ex-situ* conservation and *in-situ* conservation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building more capacity in zoos and botanical gardens.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carrying out artificial selection within zoos and botanical gardens.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building the population of captive-bred species across zoos and botanical gardens in many countries.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Release of captive-bred species from zoos and botanical gardens into nature reserves.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the transfer of ex-situ to in-situ means the return of species from captivity to areas that could become their natural habitats again. Nature reserves increase the chances of successful re-introduction, although many other measures would have to be taken to ensure success. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because building more captive breeding areas will not increase the chances of being able to reintroduce species back into the wild. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, whilst artificial selection would allow desirable traits to be selected for in captivity, this would not necessarily guarantee that species would thrive or even survive in a wild habitat again. Artificial selection would also take a long time (measured in years and decades for some species in particular), as successive generations are bred. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because having more animals in captivity and plants in botanical gardens would do little to increase the chances of those species being successfully reintroduced to the wild. A large captive population serves no natural purpose to conservation unless a species is released into the wild at some stage. Keeping species in captive breeding might be called ‘preservation’, but it is not ‘conservation’ in its truest sense."},{"ref":"BI2813","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2813\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph below shows Biodiversity Intactness, a long-term indicator that measures how much original biodiversity remains within terrestrial communities on the continents, based on levels from around 1500 A.D., when records began.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2808%20-%20Bi2813%20A4.2%20PQ%20MCQ%20and%20SAQ%20PhilCh.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2808 - Bi2813 A4.2 PQ MCQ and SAQ PhilCh.jpeg\">\n[Source: adapted from WWF (2024) *Living Planet Report 2024 – A System in\nPeril*. WWF, Gland, Switzerland http://bit.ly/4jzPxrE. Retrieved 30 April 2025, Public Domain]\n\n1.\n    1. Using data from the graph, compare and contrast biodiversity in Asia and Europe.  [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest reasons for the difference(s) you highlighted in part (a) (i)   [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline **two** reasons why the graph might give an inexact picture of global biodiversity loss.  [2 marks]\n\n3. Certain Asian species, including the Chinese Crocodile Lizard (*Shinisaurus crocodilurus*), have been selected for the EDGE of Existence programme in Asia, whereas another endangered species, the Asian elephant (*Elephas maximus*), is not on the EDGE of Existence list. Suggest why.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following (*must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*)\n\n        ***Similarity:***\\\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** showed a high level (close to 1.0) in 1800    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** declined from 1800 to 1950  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** showed an overall decline 1800 - 2024 **OR** in the period of study    [1 mark]\n\n        ***Difference:***\\\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ Asia fell steeply from 1950-2000 **WHEREAS** Europe recovered in that period  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ Asia continues to decline (in 2024) **WHEREAS** Europe is stable **OR** increasing   [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        A similarity is that **both** continents’ biodiversity **fell from 1800 to 1950** ($\\checkmark$). By contrast, Europe’s biodiversity **increased** from 1950 onwards, whereas Asia’s **continues to decline** in the present day ‘any two of the following (must include one similarity and one difference to gain full marks) ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **three** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ better awareness **OR** education in Europe   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ weaker environmental protection law in Asia [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ less poverty **OR** more access to money to fund biodiversity work in Europe   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more reliance on subsistence farming in Asia   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ less human population growth in Europe since 1950 **OR** high population density in Asia   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more deforestation **OR** urbanisation in Asia since 1950    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ European **OR** American companies operating in Asia; accept palm oil plantation [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ theft **OR** trafficking **OR** poaching **OR** illegal wildlife trading **OR** of Asian native species   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ conflict **OR** political instability in some Asian regions limits long-term biodiversity policy implementation   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ higher rate of industrialisation **OR** infrastructure development in Asia since 1950  [1 mark]\n\n        *accept converse arguments*\n\n        [3 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Asia’s rapid **economic growth** and **population growth** have put pressure on natural resources ($\\checkmark$). An example could be the draining of wetlands and deforestation to create city space for housing ($\\checkmark$). **Weaker laws and law enforcement** in Asia ($\\checkmark$) have led to a rise in **poaching and trafficking** of endangered species, and this can lead to eventual extinction. Non-Asian companies have made **large agricultural investments** in Asia such as large-scale palm oil plantations, which damage the ecosystem in native tropical forest areas ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only terrestrial ecosystems **OR** land are covered in the data **OR** there is no information on aquatic **OR** seas **OR** ocean ecosystems   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no distinction between subcontinents such as North and South America    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no distinction between kingdoms **OR** between plants and animals **OR** microorganisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ data collection in 1800 will have been less sophisticated **OR** accurate than 2024    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity in 1500 **OR** baseline data was already influenced by human activity   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indicator may rely on modelled **OR** estimated data rather than direct field measurements   [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The data **only** covers **terrestrial ecosystems**, so takes no account of aquatic organisms ($\\checkmark$). Also, **methods** of measuring biodiversity in 2024 will be a lot **more accurate** than those used in previous centuries ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the elephant has close genetic relatives **OR** more close relatives (than the crocodile)   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lizard is more evolutionarily unique (than the elephant)   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conservation data on the elephant is insufficient **OR** not up-to-date (to qualify for EDGE)      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the elephant may not be globally endangered **OR** it may be endangered in one country only **OR** have a healthy population in other countries   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is better ex-situ conservation **OR** better global zoo **OR** captive populations of the elephant than the  lizard    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lizard may occupy a more restricted **OR** fragmented habitat, increasing extinction risk   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Chinese crocodile lizard may be **very distinct genetically from its nearest relative**, which gives it priority for selection into the EDGE programme, whereas the elephant has more close relatives ($\\checkmark$). The elephant is a popular species for zoos and conservation projects, so there will be **higher populations in captivity** ($\\checkmark$), making its extinction risk lower. Also, the elephant may be endangered in one country but may **exist in large populations elsewhere**, lowering its risk of global extinction danger ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Diversity of Organisms","questions":[{"ref":"BI0081","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0081\n\nThis table shows the estimated number of genes in *E. coli* bacteria, fruit flies, dogs, humans, and rice.\n\n| Species | Estimated number of genes |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| *E.coli* (bacteria species) | 4,000 |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly) | 13,000 |\n| *Canis lupis familiaris* (dog) | 20,000 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (humans) | 30,000 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 40,000 |\n\nWhat can you conclude from the above information?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species in the same kingdom of life have a similar number of genes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Different species have different numbers of genes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ More complex species have more genes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of genes is not a characteristic of a species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is the correct answer.  This is a small sample of species, but the process of elimination means **Choice B** is the only possible correct answer. Fruit flies, humans and dogs are all in the animal kingdom but have different numbers of genes (**Choice A**).  This information shows that the number of genes does not determine the complexity, as rice has more genes than humans (**Choice C**). It is not possible to conclude that the number of genes is not a characteristic of a species from this small sample of data (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0101","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0101\n\nThe okapi looks like a small-sized giraffe and has stripes that resemble the zebra. It lives in the Ituri Forest in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Okapis eat the leaves and buds of trees they can reach. They also graze on grass and ferns and eat fruit. Okapis tend to be solitary animals and the mother gives birth to one offspring which continues to be dependent on the mother’s milk for six months. When they are four to five years old, they are mature and can breed, but only with other okapis.\n\nWhich of the following statements is applicable to the okapi?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Okapi is a hybrid of a giraffe and a zebra |\n  | II. | Okapi is an herbivore |\n  | III. | Okapi is its own species |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOkapi is a herbivore **(II)** because it only eats plants. Okapi is a species **(III)** since it can interbreed and produce fertile offspring.\n\nThey are not a hybrid of a giraffe and a zebra (**I**), even though they look like one."},{"ref":"BI0102","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0102\n\nWhat is the best definition for a species?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals living in a defined area.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals of the same species living and interacting with one another in a given area.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals living in a given area and interacting within the environment.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA group of individuals living in a defined area is not a biological definition (**Choice A**).  A group of individuals of the same species living in a given area at the same time who are interacting with each other is a population (**Choice B**) and the environment in which they live is a habitat (**Choice C**). It is only when they can interbreed to produce fertile offspring that they can be considered a species. (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0190","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0190\n\nWhat type of human cell does the following karyogram represent?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI0190.png\" alt=\"BI0190\">\n[Source: Adapted from Abedi M., Salmaninejad A. and Sakhinia E. (13th November 2017). *File: Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 15th May 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png. Copyright under Creative Commons 4.0 International ]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sperm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Egg\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Brain cell\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Skin cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because there are 23 chromosomes, so it is a haploid cell, and one of the sex chromosomes is the ‘Y’ chromosome.  Egg cells do not have the Y chromosome. **Choice B** is incorrect because all egg cells have one ‘X’ sex chromosome. **Choice C** and **choice D** are incorrect because skin and brain cells are diploid, so they have two of each chromosome: 46 in total."},{"ref":"BI0200","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0200\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe table below shows the genome size and diploid chromosome numbers of five different species.\n\n| Organism | Chromosome number | Genome size (millions of base pairs)\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| T4 phage (virus) | Single piece of DNA | 0.17 |\n| *Escherichia coli* (bacteria) | 1 | 5 |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly) | 8 | 140|\n| *Homo sapiens* (human)| 46 | 3,000 |\n| *Paris japonica* (woodland plant)| 40 | 150,000 |\n\n1. Evaluate the evidence from the table in relation to the statement that “organisms with a higher number of chromosomes do not always have more base pairs”. [2 marks]\n\n2. Discuss the data as evidence for the notion that increased genome size means that the organism is more complex. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (comparison of) *Paris japonica* **AND** *Homo sapiens* data support the statement    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *P. japonica* has fewer chromosomes than *H. sapiens* but has more base pairs **OR** *P. japonica* has 40 chromosomes with 150,000 million base pairs while *H. sapiens* has 46 chromosomes with 3,000 million base pairs.[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (comparison of) *E.coli*, *D. melanogaster* and *P. japonica* **OR** *H. sapiens* data does not support the statement                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (because) as the number of chromosomes increases, so does the genome size, **OR** the number of base pairs [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The data shows that for all organisms apart from *Paris japonica* that higher chromosome number has a larger genome (greater number of base pairs). *Paris japonica* has **fewer chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$) than *Homo sapiens* but has a **larger genome** ($\\checkmark$). ***P. japonica*** ($\\checkmark$) has 40 chromosomes which are less than ***Homo sapiens*** ($\\checkmark$) but has 150,000 million base pairs compared to 3,000 million base pairs in *Homo sapiens*.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Supporting the statement**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Escherichia coli* is the simplest one-cell organism and has the smallest genome size **OR** has 5 million base pairs compared to more complex multicellular organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ T4 phage is not a living organism and has a smaller genome than any of the living organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fruit flies are simpler organisms than humans and have a smaller genome [1 mark]\n\n    **Not supporting the statement**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Homo sapiens* is considered the most complex organism in the table but does not have the largest genome, **OR** *H. sapiens* has 3,000 million base pairs compared to 150,000 in *P. japonica*. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a larger genome does not necessarily mean that an organism has more genes  [1 mark]\n\n    *If only one side of the argument is mentioned (but not both), the maximum mark is one.*\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThe data **does not fully support** the statement that more complex organisms have a larger genome. \\\nFor example, ***Homo sapiens*** is the most complex organism in the table but does not have the largest genome ($\\checkmark$). ***Paris japonica*** , a woodland plant, has 150,000 million base pairs compared to 3,000 in *Homo sapiens*, which does not support the statement that more complex organisms have larger genomes because plants are simpler organisms than humans and achieve bigger genomes mostly due to polyploidy. ($\\checkmark$) \\\nOn the other hand, ***Escherichia coli*** is the simplest living organism in the table and has the smallest genome size, 5 million base pairs. The other organisms are more complex and have larger genomes, e.g. *Homo sapiens* has 3,000 million base pairs.  ($\\checkmark$)  T4 page is not considered to be a living organism. The above statements **support** the notion that the increased genome size means that the organism is more complex."},{"ref":"BI0250","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0250\n\nBiologists use the binomial naming system to name every organism on the planet. The binomial name for the platypus is *Ornithorhynchus anatinus*. Which statement(s) below are true for the binomial naming system and the platypus?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$    The genus for the platypus is *Ornithorhynchus*.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    The binomial naming system is based on an international agreement\nbetween scientists.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$     The name, *Ornithorhynchus anatinus* is unique to platypus species.\n\n-  A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    I and II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll three statements are correct. The first part of the binomial name is the genus, the second part is the species which is unique to platypus species. The binomial system and binomial names are agreed between biologists at international congresses."},{"ref":"BI0712","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0712\n\nThe table shows factors that can result in speciation. Which word is matched to the correct definition?\n\n| | Word | Definition |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polyploidy | Having two complete sets of chromosomes |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sexual reproduction | The combining of genetic information from two individuals |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reproductive isolation | Species breeding to produce infertile offspring |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mutations | Random changes to the amino acid sequence |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSexual reproduction involves the fusion of gametes from two individuals, resulting in offspring with a combination of genetic material. This process increases genetic variation, which is essential for evolution and natural selection. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nPolyploidy occurs when an organism has more than two complete sets of chromosomes (e.g., triploid 3n, tetraploid 4n). The definition given in the table describes diploidy (2n), which is the standard chromosome number in most sexually reproducing organisms; **Choice A** incorrect.\n\nInterbreeding between different species can sometimes produce infertile offspring, such as a horse × donkey = mule. This is an example of reproductive isolation because the hybrid offspring is sterile, preventing gene flow between the two species. However, because horses and donkeys are already separate species, this does not result in speciation—it simply reinforces their reproductive barrier. For reproductive isolation to lead to speciation, it must occur within a single species, preventing interbreeding between different populations.  **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nMutations are random changes in DNA sequences, not in the amino acid sequence; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0840","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0840\n\nThe table shows the nucleotide base sequence and genome size (number of base pairs, bp) for different species.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI0840.png\" alt=\"BI0840\">\n\nWhich conclusion can be drawn, based on the table and your own knowledge?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genome size is proportional to the size of the species.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The more closely related two species are, the more similar their base sequences.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Humans have more genes than chimpanzees, mice and horses.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The base sequence in the table for humans and chimpanzees is the same, therefore they have an identical genetic code.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSpecies that share a recent common ancestor, such as humans and chimpanzees, are more likely to have similar base sequences. Over time, as species diverge and evolve, mutations accumulate in their base sequences. The longer two species have been separated from their common ancestor, the more mutations will have accumulated in their genomes. Therefore, species that are closely related have fewer accumulated mutations and more similar base sequences (**Choice B**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because genome size is not necessarily proportional to the size of the species. The genome size can vary significantly even among organisms of similar size, and organisms with similar-sized genomes, such as the horse and mouse vary greatly in size. **Choice C** is incorrect because it is possible to have a larger genome size with a comparable, or even smaller, number of genes, depending on the amount of non-coding DNA and repetitive sequences present in the genome. Humans have a larger genome but a similar number of genes to the chimpanzee, mouse and horse. **Choice D** is incorrect because there is a difference of one nucleotide base in the table between humans and chimpanzees. The genome of humans and chimpanzees is similar but not the same."},{"ref":"BI1021","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1021\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n*Ammophila arenaria* (Marram grass), pictured below, is plant species that is often found on sand dunes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1021A.png\" alt=\"BI1021A\">\n[Source: Agnieszka K., Nova. (2020). *File: Ammophila arenaria Piaskownica zwyczajna* 2020-06-29 04.jpg\n.Wikimediacommons. Retrieved July 29, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Ammophila_arenaria_Piaskownica_zwyczajna_2020-06-29_04.jpg.  Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the genus to which *Ammophila arenaria* belongs. [1 mark]\n\n    2. State the naming system used to name *Ammophila arenaria*. [1 mark]\n\n    3. State the term used to describe the differences between organisms, which is used as the basis for the naming system in part (ii).  [1 mark]\n\n    4. Describe how the genome within a species could vary.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the morphological and biological species concepts.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Ammophila*                                                [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    ***Ammophila*** ($\\checkmark$) is the genus to which Marram grass belongs.\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial naming system    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **binomial naming system** ($\\checkmark$) is used to name organisms.\n\n1.\n    3.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variation **OR** biodiversity **OR** characteristics **OR** phylogeny **OR** morphology        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Variation** ($\\checkmark$) refers to the differences between organisms, which is the basis for the binomial naming system.\n\n1.\n    4. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the genome can vary in size **OR** base sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** is a result of mutations **OR** single-nucleotide polymorphisms **OR** insertions **OR** deletions **OR** tandem repeats **OR** other named mutations        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Single-nucleotide polymorphisms** ($\\checkmark$) could result in changes to the **base sequence** ($\\checkmark$) of DNA between organisms of the same species.\n\n2. **BOTH** of the following (answer must include at least one similarity and one difference to achieve full marks):\n\n    **Similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** seek to define a species     [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept refers to species being groups of organisms with shared physical traits, **WHEREAS** the biological species concept refers to species being groups of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Linnaeus proposed the morphological species concept **WHEREAS** the biological species concept was proposed by Mayr     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept is applicable to sexual and asexual organisms **HOWEVER**, the biological species concept is only applicable to sexually reproducing organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept was proposed before the biological species concept [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ The biological species concept reflects the evolutionary relationship, **WHEREAS** morphological species concept may not represent genetic relatedness or evolutionary relationships     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** the morphological and biological species concepts **aim to define** what constitutes a **species** ($\\checkmark$) in the field of biology. However, the **morphological** species concept defines species based on their **shared physical characteristics**, whereas the **biological** species concept defines species based on the ability of individuals to **interbreed and produce fertile offspring** under natural conditions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1022","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1022\n\nWhich of the following is the correct format for species names according to the binomial naming system?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genus + Species\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genus + species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Genus* + *species*\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Genus* + *Species*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because only the genus name should be capitalised, it should come before the species name, and both should be in italics. **Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because only the genus name should be capitalised. **Choice B** is incorrect, as the genus and species name should be in italics."},{"ref":"BI1023","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1023\n\nWhich of the following is true for the biological species concept?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms within the same species can interbreed to produce fertile offspring\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms within the same species can interbreed to produce infertile offspring\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species are groups of organisms with shared traits\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species are groups of organisms without shared traits","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAccording to the biological species concept, a species is a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring. **Choice A** is correct. **Choice B** is incorrect as it refers to infertile offspring. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because they refer to the morphological species concept."},{"ref":"BI1024","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1024\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) is/are true for the genome?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The genome is all the genetic information of an organism.  |\n    | II. | Genomes within a species are identical in size. |\n    | III. | Genomes within a species vary in base sequence. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe genome is all the genetic information in an organism (**Statement I**). The base sequence can vary within a species due to single-nucleotide polymorphism (**Statement III**). Therefore, **Choice C** is correct as it contains both true statements.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one true statement.\nThe size of the genome within a species can vary, this can be due to mutations, such as insertions and deletions, so **Statement II** is not true. **Choices B** and **D** are, therefore, incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1026","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1026\n\nWhich row in the table is correct for either the biological or morphological species concept?\n\n| | Concept | Species Definition | Proposed by… |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Biological | A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring | Linnaeus |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Biological | A group of organisms with shared traits | Mayr |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Morphological | A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring | Mayr |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Morphological | A group of organisms with shared traits | Linnaeus |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe morphological concept refers to a species as a group of organisms with shared traits. It assumes that individuals that look alike are part of the same species. This was proposed by Linnaeus. **Choice D** is correct. **Choice A** is incorrect because although the definition of the biological species concept is correct, it was not proposed by Linnaeus, it was proposed by Mayr. **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect because neither the definitions nor the proposers are correct."},{"ref":"BI1051","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1051\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the binomial naming system and its importance.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how a sample of cells taken from a foetus can be used to determine whether the foetus will be biologically male or biologically female at birth.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Outline the outcomes of the human genome project and their impacts on different fields of science. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial naming system as the system used to name species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genus name and species name     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species with the same genus share many characteristics **OR** are closely related    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ internationally agreed        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates understanding and communication between scientists     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The binomial naming system is the system which scientists use to **name species** ($\\checkmark$) and this is **internationally agreed** ($\\checkmark$), which means it’s used all over the world, which aids in the **communication** between scientists ($\\checkmark$) in different countries. The name of each species has two parts, the first highlights the **genus** to which it belongs and the second part refers to the **species** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells grown in a culture medium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells stained [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ observed under a light microscope [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photomicrograph taken during mitosis **OR** mitotic metaphase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomes arranged according to size **OR** position of centromeres [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ look at chromosome pair 23 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two large chromosomes mean foetus is XX [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one large chromosome and one small chromosome means foetus is XY [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ XX is biologically female **AND** XY is biologically male [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Once the sample of cells has been taken from the foetus they are grown in a **culture medium** ($\\checkmark$). They are then **stained** ($\\checkmark$) and observed under a **light microscope** ($\\checkmark$). A **photomicrograph** is taken ($\\checkmark$), and the image is cut up so that the **chromosomes** can be **arranged by size** and position of the **centromere** ($\\checkmark$). A foetus with **two large X** chromosomes will be **biologically female** ($\\checkmark$), and a foetus with **one large X** and **one small Y** chromosome will be **biologically male** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Human Genome Project (HGP) sequenced the entire human genome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it determined the total number of base pairs in the human genome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it estimated the number of genes in the human genome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified the loci of genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified repeat sequences     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified locations of single nucleotide polymorphisms            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified gene sequences associated with certain diseases **OR** biological processes **OR** traits    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HGP stimulated further development **OR** reduced the price of sequencing technology    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ technology developed for the HGP is now used to diagnose diseases **OR** identify micro-organisms OR DNA matching in forensics and medicine **OR** genetic modification  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contributed to the field by identifying genetic markers that affect how individuals metabolise and respond to medications **OR** contribute information for personalised medicine        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insights into evolutionary relationships **OR** genetic similarities **OR** genetic differences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identification of regulatory elements **OR** non-coding RNAs **OR** components that play essential roles in gene expression **OR** cellular function  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emphasised importance of consideration of ethical **OR** legal **OR** social implications of genomic research  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Human Genome Project (HGP) **sequenced** the entire human genome ($\\checkmark$), **identified the loci** of genes ($\\checkmark$) and identified gene sequences that are connected to some **diseases** ($\\checkmark$) such as breast cancer. Locations of **single nucleotide polymorphisms** ($\\checkmark$) were discovered which are variations in individuals DNA base pairs. The results have contributed to the development of **personalised medicine** ($\\checkmark$) as genetic markers have been identified that affect how individuals respond to medications. The technology developed for the HGP has led to further advancements in technology, **reducing the cost** of genetic sequencing ($\\checkmark$).  The HGP also emphasised how important it is to consider the **ethical implications** ($\\checkmark$) of genomic research and the importance of informed consent."},{"ref":"BI1220","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1220\n\nWhich statement is **not** true about the genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The human genome is organised into chromosomes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each liver cell contains a full copy of the genome.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms of the same species share most of their genome.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the information carried by the genome is expressed in each cell.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNot all genes are expressed in each cell. Only the necessary genes are expressed based on the specialised function of the cell. **Choice D** is **not** a true statement, and so is the correct answer.\n\nThe genome is all the genetic information of an organism, including the genes and non-coding DNA. In humans, the genome is organised into linear chromosomes in the nucleus, and circular chromosomes in the mitochondria. **Choice A** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer. Organisms of the same species have most of their genome in common; however, variations give rise to some diversity between individuals. **Choice C** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer. Each cell in a multicellular organism typically contains a full copy of the genome, including liver cells, an exception is red blood cells as they do not have a nucleus. **Choice B** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer."},{"ref":"BI1227","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1227\n\nThe scientific name for the little spotted kiwi is *Apteryx owenii*. Which of the species below is most closely related to the little spotted kiwi?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1227.png\" alt=\"BI1227\">\n[Source: Adapted from Lapsley Miller, J. (2023, August 28). *Little Spotted Kiwi*. Wikipedia. Retrieved September 13, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_spotted_kiwi.  Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Apteryx australis*\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Little spotted pardalote\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Owenii apteryx*\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Neognathae owenii*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSpecies names are given using the binomial system, where the genus comes first, and the species follows. Species with the same genus have similar traits and are closely related;  **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect as it is not a scientific name, so conveys no information about the relatedness of species. **Choice C** is incorrect as it is just a reversal of the scientific name of the little spotted kiwi, and is in a different genus. **Choice D**  is incorrect as it is not in the same genus as the little spotted kiwi."},{"ref":"BI1384","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1384\n\nThe following table shows the approximate number of protein-encoding genes found in various species. What can be concluded from these findings?\n\n| Species | Approximate number of protein-encoding genes|\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly)| 14,000 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 51,000 |\n| *Gallus gallus* (chicken) |20,000-23,000 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (human) | 20,000-25,000 |\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The number of genes is not a good indicator of organism complexity. |\n    | II. | Chickens may have more genes than humans. |\n    | III. | The number of base pairs in the rice genome is greater than in the other species. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of genes found in a species does not serve as a good indicator of complexity (**Statement I**). If it did, the data here would suggest that chickens and humans are equally complex, and that rice is far more complex than either of them. Due to the overlapping ranges in the number of genes, it is entirely possible for chickens to have a greater number of genes than humans (**Statement II**). It is impossible to determine the size of the genome (measured in base pairs) of a species based on the number of genes it has. In reality, rice has far fewer base pairs than chickens or humans despite having approximately twice as many genes (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect as they all contain **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1395","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1395\n\nHow are the chromosomes in a karyogram ordered?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ By analogous pairs\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ By number of genes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ By size\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ By position of the centromeres","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the chromosomes in a karyogram are ordered in homologous pairs from largest to smallest.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chromosomes are grouped in homologous pairs (one from each parent), not analogous pairs. **Choice B** is incorrect because the number of genes on each chromosome is highly variable, and genes do not have a consistent length. Although the chromosomes are aligned with their centromeres in a karyogram, the question only asks about the order of the chromosomes, which is not dependent on centromere position; thus, **Choice D** is also incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1479","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1479\n\nWhat makes up the genome of all eukaryotes but not prokaryotes?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ Chromosomes and mitochondrial DNA\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Chloroplast DNA\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ Plasmids and ribosomal DNA\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe genome is the whole of the genetic information of an organism. In eukaryotes, chromosomes and mitochondrial DNA (**Statement I**) make up the genome. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nChloroplast DNA is not found in all eukaryotes (**Statement II**). It is primarily found in plant cells but not animal cells; **Statement II** is incorrect. Plasmids are found in prokaryotes but not eukaryotes, and ribosomes do not contain DNA, **Statement III**  is incorrect. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they all contain **Statements II** and/or **III**."},{"ref":"BI1570","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1570\n\nThe table shows the number of chromosomes found in the gametes of various species.\n\n| Species | Haploid chromosome number (n) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Parascaris equorum* (horse threadworm)| 2 |\n| *Vicia faba* (field bean) | 6 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 12 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (human) | 23 |\n| *Pan troglodytes* (chimpanzee) | 24 |\n| *Canis familiaris* (dog) | 39 |\n\nWhat can be concluded from the data in the table?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Diploid cells usually have an even number of chromosomes. |\n    | II. | Species with more chromosomes have a larger genome size. |\n    | III. | Humans are most closely related to *Pan troglodytes*. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because diploid cells have an even number of chromosomes (**Statement I**) unless affected by errors such as non-disjunction. This can be obtained by doubling the haploid number in the table and recognising that an odd number of diploid chromosomes cannot be evenly distributed to form gametes.\n\n**Statement II** is false because the number of chromosomes an organism has is not an accurate reflection of its genome size. *Oryza sativa*, for example, has a much larger genome than any of the other species in the table despite having fewer chromosomes. **Statement III** is false because although humans are closely related to chimpanzees, this cannot be concluded from the data in the table alone. *Stipa spartea* (porcupine grass), for example, has the same number of chromosomes as humans but is not closely related. **Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they contain one or more false statements."},{"ref":"BI1652","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1652\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe karyogram is from a rat (*Rattus rattus*) cell without any chromosomal abnormalities. Biological sex is determined in the same way in rats and humans.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1652.png\" alt=\"BI1652\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hamanaka, S., Yamaguchi, T., Kobayashi, T., Kato-Itoh, M., Yamazaki, S., Sato, H., Umino, A., Wakiyama, Y., Arai, M., Sanbo, M., Hirabayashi, M., & Nakauchi, H. (2011, July 15). *File: Karyogram of normal rat.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 11, 2024 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Karyogram_of_normal_rat.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. State, with a reason, the stage of mitosis at which chromosomes are photographed to make a karyogram.                     [1 mark]\n\n2. Deduce, with a reason, the biological sex of the rat.    [1 mark]\n\n3. State how the chromosomes have been arranged to create the karyogram.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Using the karyogram, compare and contrast between the chromosomes of rats and humans.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase **BECAUSE** the chromosomes are visible **OR** condensed **OR** aligned on the equator of the cell so that it is easier to sort them    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The chromosomes are in **metaphase** because the chromosomes are **visible** and aligned on the **equator** of the cell, which makes it **easier to sort** them ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male **BECAUSE** it has a Y chromosome **OR** has both X and Y chromosomes        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The rat is **male** because it has a  **Y** chromosome ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by decreasing length    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using banding patterns **OR** centromere positions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in pairs **OR** homologous pairs **OR** one chromosome from each parent forms a pair    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The chromosomes have been arranged by **decreasing length** ($\\checkmark$) and using their **banding patterns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two**  of the following: \\\n    *Answer must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to gain full marks*\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have pairs of chromosomes **OR** homologous chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have specific banding patterns **OR** genes on the chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a pair of sex chromosomes **OR** X and Y chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rats have fewer chromosomes **OR** autosomes than humans **OR** humans have more chromosomes **OR** autosomes than rats **OR** rats have 21 pairs of chromosomes **AND** humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes **OR** rats have 42 chromosomes **AND** humans have 46  chromosomes **OR**  rats have 40 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes **AND** humans have 44 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes **OR**  rats have 20 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes **AND** humans have 22 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both rats and humans have **homologous chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$), however, rats have **fewer chromosomes** (42) than humans (46) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2321","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2321\n\nWhole genome sequencing has been used to sequence the genomes of many species. Which of the following are potential future uses of whole genome sequencing?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Identifying disease-causing alleles.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sequencing the genomes of bacteria and fungi.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Advancing personalised medicine.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Identifying evolutionary relationships between species.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because personalised medicine involves using an individual's entire genetic profile to predict their response to specific drugs and tailor treatments accordingly. While the technology exists, its integration into routine clinical practice for most patients is considered a primary future goal of genomics.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the identification of disease-causing alleles (such as the BRCA1 gene or those for cystic fibrosis) is a well-established current use of genome sequencing.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the sequencing of prokaryotes (bacteria) and fungi was among the earliest successes of the Human Genome Project era and is currently performed routinely in pathology and research labs.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because comparing whole genomes to construct phylogenetic trees and determine evolutionary relationships is a standard contemporary method in modern cladistics."},{"ref":"BI2648","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2648\n\nWhich statement(s) below are challenges associated with the biological species concept (BSC)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The BSC assumes reproductive isolation, but some species are reproductively isolated only in certain environments. |\n    | II. | Fossil species cannot be assessed using the BSC due to the lack of observable mating behaviour. |\n    | III. | The BSC relies on morphological features, which can vary widely within a species. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because reproductive isolation may be context-dependent (e.g. due to geography or behaviour), making it unclear whether two populations are separate species under BSC.\n\n**Statement II** is true because the BSC depends on observing whether organisms can interbreed. This is impossible with fossils, which are studied based on morphology and location, not reproductive behaviour.\n\n**Statement III** is false because the BSC specifically does not define species based on morphology; that is the morphological species concept. BSC focuses instead on reproductive capability, so this is not a valid criticism of BSC.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it includes only the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is missing the true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2649","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2649\n\nWhich of the following statements explain why classifying diverging populations as species can be arbitrary?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Traits may diverge at different rates in different populations |\n    | II. | Some populations may remain reproductively compatible despite divergence |\n    | III. | Morphological changes always occur before genetic differences appear |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because, during speciation, some traits (e.g., behaviour, physiology, morphology) can evolve faster than others. This means two populations might look different but still interbreed, or might appear similar but already be reproductively isolated. Because divergence doesn’t follow a fixed pattern, deciding when to define them as separate species becomes subjective.\n\n**Statement II** is true because Populations might develop noticeable differences in traits (such as colouration or mating calls), but still produce fertile offspring when they interbreed. According to the biological species concept, they’re technically the same species, but these differences can confuse classification.\n\n**Statement III** is false because often, the opposite is true. Genetic differences (mutations, allele frequency changes) may accumulate before any visible morphological change occurs. For example, two populations may look identical but carry distinct genetic markers and be reproductively isolated.\n\nSaying morphological change “always” comes first is incorrect and overly simplistic.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it includes only the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2801","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2801\n\nWhat determines the overall size of a eukaryotic genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of genes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of chromosomes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The amount of protein\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The total amount of DNA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of genes contributes to the overall size of the genome, but it is not the sole determinant. Eukaryotic genomes contain a significant amount of noncoding DNA.  The amount of this non-coding DNA is a major contributing factor to the overall size of a eukaryotic genome.  **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nChromosomes vary greatly in the number of genes and noncoding DNA they contain. Simply knowing the number of chromosomes in a genome does little to describe the size of the genome.  **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because a genome is a measure of only the amount of DNA and does not consider protein.  \\\n**Choice D** is the correct choice because it is the total amount of DNA in a genome that determines the size of that genome, regardless of the function of the DNA."},{"ref":"BI2802","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2802\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe table shows genome sizes for several different species.\n\n|Organism | Genome Size (base pairs) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Escherichia coli* (Bacterium) | 5 million |\n| *Saccharomyces cerevisiae* (Yeast) | 12 million |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (Fruit Fly) | 140 million |\n| *Xenopus laevis* (Frog) | ???? |\n| *Homo sapiens* | 3 billion |\n\n1. Describe the challenges of predicting the size of the genome of *Xenopus laevis* based on the information provided in the table.     [2 marks]\n\nSingle-cell genome sequencing allows researchers to analyse the genetic material of individual cells. Cancer is characterised by significant genomic instability, leading to massive variations in genome size when compared to noncancerous cells.\n\n2. Explain how single-cell genome sequencing can be more useful than traditional genome sequencing for developing effective cancer treatments.    [3 marks]\n\nThe diagram below depicts nanopore DNA sequencing technology.  This portable technology involves passing a single piece of DNA through a tiny pore in a membrane in an electrical field in a manner such that the current can reveal the DNA sequence.  This allows for the analysis of environmental DNA (eDNA), which consists of DNA fragments shed by organisms into their surroundings (e.g., skin cells, waste).  It can also give almost immediate results for shorter fragments, and sequence entire genomes in as little as 3 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/2802a.jpg\" alt=\"2802a\">\n[Source: Adapted from *DNA sequencing method* [Infographic], by National Institute of Standards and Technology, 2012 Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 5 May 5 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:DNA_Sequencing_Method_ (8051510835).jpg&oldid=958458868.Public Domain]\n\n3. Discuss **one** potential benefit and one potential risk associated with the widespread use of portable DNA sequencing technologies.     [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest ways whole-genome sequencing can contribute to our understanding of evolutionary relationships between species, and also to personalised medicine.     [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following (award zero marks for a numerical prediction):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome size does not increase with complexity in a predictable manner     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH MEANS** it is not possible to predict a genome size based on the information in the table       [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The genome size of Xenopus laevis **cannot be predicted** ($\\checkmark$) from the table because there is **no correlation** between organism **complexity and genome size** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ traditional whole-genome sequencing provides an average genomic profile of the whole organism **OR** not just the tumour     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-cell sequencing can show differences between the typical genome of that organism and the tumor     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-cell sequencing can show differences within the tumour itself     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heterogeneity within the tumour can provide clinicians **OR** researchers with clues as to the best treatment options      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ comparison of single-cell genome sequencing of tumour with originating tissue can help clinicians predict which treatment option will best allow healthy tissue to survive     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-cell sequencing facilitates the development of more personalised and effective therapies     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Traditional whole-genome** sequencing provides an overall genomic view of a **whole organism** ($\\checkmark$), giving us an average of the genetic information across all cells. **Single-cell sequencing** offers a more **detailed** perspective by revealing **differences** ($\\checkmark$) between the typical genome of that organism and the tumour cells.  Heterogeneity within the tumour can provide clinicians and researchers with crucial clues for determining the **most effective treatment options** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** benefit and **one** risk)*:\n\n    ***benefits***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enables rapid identification of pathogens in remote or resource-limited areas     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ faster response time for public health interventions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tracking of endangered **OR** invasive **OR** elusive species by eDNA without the need for lab facilities     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identification of species present in an ecosystem based on eDNA without the need for lab facilities  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic analysis at the point of care **OR** on-site for rapid treatment response     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individual empowerment **OR** accountability over their own health     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased speed of identification of criminal suspects **OR** disaster victims     [1 mark]\n\n    ***risks***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ privacy concerns due to easy access to genetic data     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ misuse of genetic information **OR** genetic discrimination      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ misinterpretation of results by non-experts may lead to incorrect medical or legal decisions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potential for higher error rate  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potential for bioterrorism   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ technological advancements moving faster than legal regulatory framework leads to misuse and abuse [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One potential benefit of portable DNA sequencing is that it can provide **faster results** ($\\checkmark$) because the sequencing can be done on-site rather than sent to a lab, which can take days.  One possible risk is that DNA samples can be collected from the environment **without the consent of the people**, raising important **ethical concerns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following, at least one must be presented for each application\n\n    ***evolutionary relationships***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for comparison of whole genomes across species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identifies conserved regions and genes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates the study of evolutionary genetic changes such as gene duplication, loss, chromosomal alterations and horizontal gene transfer    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ generates data for analysing genome patterns of evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extensive genetic data enables the creation of detailed and accurate phylogenetic trees    [1 mark]\n\n    ***personalised medicine***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identifies genetic predispositions to disease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ predict individual responses to drugs and medicines    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ characterise the genetic make-up of tumours to guide cancer treatment    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Whole-genome sequencing can contribute to the study of evolutionary relationships by identifying differences in **genetic patterns of evolution**, such as **gene duplication or loss** and **chromosomal duplication or loss** ($\\checkmark$).  It can also **identify conserved genes** or regulatory sequences between species ($\\checkmark$).  Whole-genome sequencing can contribute to personalised medicine through the **identification of genetic diseases** ($\\checkmark$) and predispositions to disease, as well as probable individual physiological **responses to treatment** options ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Evolution & Speciation","questions":[{"ref":"BI0226","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0226\n\nWhich statement best defines evolution?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Humans breed organisms to produce desired traits\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Two populations become reproductively isolated\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A change in the heritable characteristics of a population over generations\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Individuals change their characteristics during their lifetime in response to the environment","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because evolution is defined as a change in heritable characteristics (genetic traits) within a population over generations. The key ideas are population-level change and inheritance across generations.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes selective breeding (artificial selection), where humans choose which organisms reproduce to increase desired traits. It can cause evolutionary change, but it is not the definition of evolution.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because reproductive isolation (no interbreeding or gene flow) can be a mechanism leading to speciation, but isolation itself is not the definition of evolution. Evolution is about changes in heritable traits in a population.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because this describes acquired characteristics or acclimatisation (changes during an individual’s lifetime). These changes are generally not inherited genetically, so they do not define evolution."},{"ref":"BI0227","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0227\n\nThe forelimbs of a human, bird, and whale have the same basic bone pattern but different functions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0227ii.png\" alt=\"BI0227ii\">\n\nWhat does this provide evidence for?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The differences are caused by acquired characteristics that are passed on\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The limbs formed due to selective breeding by humans\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The limbs are analogous, showing convergent evolution from unrelated ancestors\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The limbs are homologous, suggesting a common ancestor and divergent evolution","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA shared underlying structure with different functions is evidence of homologous structures. Homology supports common ancestry, and the differences in function are explained by divergent evolution (adaptation to different environments). **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this is a Lamarck-style idea, which says that acquired characteristics are passed on. Evolutionary change occurs through inheritance of alleles and changes in allele frequencies, not traits acquired during an individual’s lifetime being inherited.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because selective breeding requires humans intentionally choosing parents to reproduce. The limb similarities across humans, bats, and whales are not explained by human-directed breeding; they are found in natural lineages over long timescales.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because analogous structures have similar function but different underlying structure and do not come from a common ancestor (e.g., bird wing vs insect wing). In this case, the underlying bone plan is very similar, so they are not analogous."},{"ref":"BI0228","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0228\n\nWhich of the following is/are example(s) of selective breeding?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ A dog is trained to sit on command\n- II. $\\hspace{0.7em}$ Farmers repeatedly breed cattle with the highest milk yield over many generations\n\n- III. $\\hspace{0.5em}$A bacterial population becomes antibiotic-resistant after exposure to antibiotics\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true. Training a dog changes behaviour through learning. It does not involve choosing parents to reproduce, and the learned behaviour is not a genetic trait passed on through reproduction.\n**Statement II** is true. This is classic artificial selection, where humans choose which cows reproduce based on a desired inherited trait (high milk yield). Over many generations, the alleles linked to that trait become more common.\n**Statement III** is not true. Antibiotics act as an environmental selection pressure. Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce more successfully, so resistance increases in the population. This is natural selection, not humans selecting parents to breed for a desired trait.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it includes the only true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more of the untrue **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI0229","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0229\n\nWhich of the following best describes an analogous structure?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A structure in two different species that serves a similar function and was inherited from a common ancestor.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    A structure in two different species that serves a similar function but evolved independently, without a common ancestor.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    A structure found in all members of a species that performs the same function.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    A structure found in all members of a species but serves different functions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnalogous structures are traits found in different species that perform similar functions but do not originate from a common ancestor. Instead, they arise through convergent evolution, where species adapt to similar environments or ecological roles independently. **Choice B** is correct as it describes analogous structures, which evolve due to similar selective pressures rather than shared ancestry.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it describes homologous structures, which share a common evolutionary origin but may have different functions (e.g., a whale's flipper and a human's arm).\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because this refers to universal structures within a species, which is unrelated to analogous structures.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because this describes structural variation within a species, not similarities between different species."},{"ref":"BI0230","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0230\n\nBased on the structure of each forelimb shown below, which one is likely to be analogous?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_A.png\" alt=\"BI0230_A\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_B.png\" alt=\"BI0230_B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_C.png\" alt=\"BI0230_C\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_D.png\" alt=\"BI0230_D\">|\n\n[Adapted from: Source Владислав Петрович , В. (2011). *File:homology vertebrates-en.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 8, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homology_vertebrates-en.svg copyright free \\\nGraham, V. B. (2016, May 31). *File:analogous & homologous structures.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 8, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Analogous_%26_Homologous_Structures.svg CC-BY-SA-4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn analogous structure is one that has a similar function to other structures but does not come from a common ancestor. **Choice B** is a forelimb, but does not have a structure similar to the other three limbs, which indicates that it does not come from a common ancestor, so is likely to be analogous.\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they are all homologous to each other i.e. they share a common ancestor, evidenced by the similarities in their structures."},{"ref":"BI0232","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0232\n\nWhich of the following is **not** an example of speciation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Evolution of different beak shapes in finches (*C. olivacea*)\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Breeding varieties of green apples (*M. domestica*) with desirable characteristics\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Squirrels on the North and South rims of the grand canyon, who cannot interbreed\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Buffalo grass (*B. dactyloides*) is able to grow in polluted soil but other species of grass cannot","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it refers to selective breeding, not speciation. **Choices A**, **C** and **D** are all examples of speciation."},{"ref":"BI0233","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0233\n\nWhich option lists examples of evidence that can be used to support the theory of evolution?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sudden appearance of complex organisms, fossil record, homologous structures\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Fossil record, homologous structures, size of organisms\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Homologous structures, speciation, fossil record\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Speciation, the complexity of organisms, increasing size of organisms","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe fossil record provides evidence of evolution, as the sequence in which fossils appear matches the sequence that they would be expected to evolve. Homologous structures provide evidence of evolution as they provide evidence that organisms shared a common ancestor. Speciation provides evidence of evolution as when populations become reproductively isolated, then natural selection can act differently on those populations, resulting in the formation of new species. **Choice C** is correct. **Choices A** and **D**  are incorrect as the organisms becoming more complex does not necessarily correlate with evolution. **Choice B** is incorrect, as the increasing size of organisms does not necessarily correlate with evolution."},{"ref":"BI0235","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0235\n\nHow does gradual divergence provide evidence for evolution?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is variation between different species across geographical areas.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is no variation between different species across geographical areas.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is variation between populations of the same species across geographical areas.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is no variation between populations of the same species across geographical areas.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGradual divergence describes gradual changes in allele frequencies and phenotypes across a species’ geographical range, so neighbouring populations are similar but become increasingly different with distance while still belonging to the same species. Therefore,  **Choice C** is correct: there is variation within a species across geographical areas.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because “no variation” does not support gradual divergence.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because gradual divergence refers to differences within a species, not variation between different species."},{"ref":"BI0236","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0236\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram represents the evolution of features in the forefoot of horses from the oldest ancestor (a) to modern day horses (e).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0236.png\" alt=\"BI0236\">\n[Source: Heard, J.M. (2015, August 8). *Image from page 65 of \"breeding, training, management and diseases of the horse and other domestic animals*\" (1893). Flickr. Retrieved June 7, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20385840566/. CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Distinguish between the earliest and most recent forefoot. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain the mechanism through which the horse forefoot has changed over time. [4 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest what would happen if ancestral horses were brought back and introduced into the wild today. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n2. State the definition of evolution. [1 mark]\n\n3. Different horse breeds exist in varying geographical locations. Suggest **one** possible way to show that these horses living in different geographical locations were from the same species. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot is longer **THAN** the older forefoot (or vice versa) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot is more narrow **THAN** the older forefoot (or vice versa) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot has **FEWER** toes **OR** only one toe **COMPARED** to the older forefoot **OR** more recent foreleg has **FEWER** individual **OR** fused bones **COMPARED** to the older forelegs [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The more recent forefoot is **longer than the earlier forefoot** ($\\checkmark$) and has fewer **toes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **four** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ heritable variation in toe number OR forefoot length existed in the population    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutation produced individuals with fewer toes OR more weight on the central toe OR longer forefoot                                    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental selection pressure meant this phenotype gave a fitness advantage OR better survival OR greater reproductive success                        [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with the advantageous trait survived and reproduced more than others    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ trait was inherited by offspring                            [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ over many generations, the frequency of the advantageous allele increased in the population                                        [1 mark]\n\n        [4 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Random mutations** and recombination produced individuals with **variation** in forefoot structure, including **reduced toe number**  and a **longer central toe** ($\\checkmark$). In **changing environments**, individuals with these traits had a **fitness advantage** ($\\checkmark$), and their **offspring inherited the alleles** ($\\checkmark$). Over **many generations, allele frequencies shifted** ($\\checkmark$), and the reduced-toe forefoot became common.\n\n    3. **one** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ wouldn\\'t survive **OR** would die **BECAUSE** they are less adapted to the environment **OR** would be outcompeted **OR** would be selected against **OR** other reasonable justification [1 mark]\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Ancestral horses **would not survive**  ($\\checkmark$) in the wild because they are not as well **adapted to the environment** ($\\checkmark$) as modern horses.\n\n2. $\\ce{ -> }$ heritable characteristics of a species change over time [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Evolution is when the **heritable characteristics** ($\\checkmark$) of a species change over time.\n\n3. any **two** of the following (*must be from the same pair of statements to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cross-breed the horses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if produce fertile offspring they are same species [1 mark]\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analyse horses' DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ look for very similar base sequences **OR** same barcode [1 mark]\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compare chromosome number **OR** karyotypes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ same chromosome number supports being the same species [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Cross-breed** the **horses** from different locations ($\\checkmark$). If they **produce fertile offspring** then they are of the same species ($\\checkmark$), ."},{"ref":"BI0813","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0813\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA study has been conducted on the effect of hormonal contraception pollution on the mating calls of the adult male African clawed frog (*Xenopus laevis*). The spectrogram shows the frequency and pitch of clicks (mating call) of control *X.laevis* and those exposed to oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813ai.png\" alt=\"BI0813ai\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813aii.png\" alt=\"BI0813aii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Distinguish between the clicks in the spectrogram between the control *X. laevis* and the *X. laevis* treated with oestrogen in 1 second.            [2 marks]\n\nThe graph shows the average duration of the *X. laevis* mating calls when exposed to the hormone oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813b.png\" alt=\"BI0813b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n2. Describe the effect of oestrogen on the duration of the mating call shown in this study.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why it is important to have the *X.laevis* control group.        [1 mark]\n\n4. Identify the independent variable in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n5.\n    1. Predict the relative reproductive success of *X.laevis* exposed to 29.6 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen.            [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the possible consequences of oestrogen exposure on *X. laevis*.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control *X.laevis* have a more regular click pattern **WHEREAS** *X.laevis* treated with oestrogen have an irregular click pattern        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control *X.laevis* have a lower pitch of clicks **OR** a pitch of approximately 2.5 kHz **WHEREAS** *X.laevis* have a higher pitch of clicks **OR** a pitch of approximately 3 kHz    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the *X.laevis*  exposed to oestrogen have longer clicks **COMPARED** to the control *X.laevis* **OR** the control *X.laevis* has more frequent clicks **OR** faster clicks than the *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813ci.png\" alt=\"BI0813ci\">\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813cii.png\" alt=\"BI0813cii\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n    The control *X.laevis* have a more **regular click pattern compared** to the *X.laevis* treated with oestrogen, which have an **irregular click pattern** ($\\checkmark$). The control *X.laevis* have a **lower pitch** of clicks at approximately **2.5 kHz**, whereas the *X.laevis* that were exposed to oestrogen have a **higher pitch** of approximately **3 kHz** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 29.6 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen **OR** the highest concentration of oestrogen has the shortest mean mating call duration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lowest level of oestrogen OR 0.296 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen has the longest mean mating call duration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the middle concentration of oestrogen OR at ngL:sup[-1], the mean mating call duration was similar to the control OR only 1s longer than the control        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as oestrogen levels increase the mean duration of the mating call decreases            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **longest** mean mating **call** of 33s was seen at the **lowest concentration of oestrogen** (0.296 ngL:sup[-1]) ($\\checkmark$).  *X.laevis* exposed to an **oestrogen concentration of 2.96 ngL:sup[-1]** had a mean mating call **duration similar** to the **control** ($\\checkmark$), just 1s longer.  However, at the **highest concentration of oestrogen** (29.6 ngL:sup[-1]), *X.laevis* had a much **lower** mean mating call **duration**, 4 seconds less than the control ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that any effect being seen on the mating call duration **OR** frequency of clicks **OR** pitch of clicks is only due to the effect of hormones **OR** oestrogen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a baseline comparison            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for establishing causality            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to minimise the impact of confounding variables    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    It is important to have a control group because it helps establish that **any observed changes in mating call duration are due to oestrogen** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of oestrogen that *X.laevis* is exposed to [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The independent variable in this study is the **concentration of oestrogen** ($\\checkmark$) that the *X.laevis* is exposed to.\n\n5.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lower reproductive success than the control **OR** reduced successful matings    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen may have **lower** reproductive success than the control ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n    2. Any **three** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen have a higher pitch of clicks **OR** longer clicks **OR** a shorter mating call duration, so may not be recognised by female *X.laevis*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting in reduced mating opportunities and so reduced reproductive success    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *X.laevis*  exposed to oestrogen may have shorter life spans and die without passing their genes onto the next generation reducing population size and gene pool        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this can result in the population being more vulnerable to disease **OR** environmental changes **OR** limit the population’s adaptive potential [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if the altered mating call resembles calls of a different species of frog **OR** attracts females from another species, this can result in hybridisation of the species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ altered calls may disrupt the social dynamics **OR** may reduce ability of male to establish dominance over competitors **OR** defend territory [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exposure to oestrogen in  *X.laevis* frogs could result in **reduced reproductive success due to reduced mating opportunities** ($\\checkmark$) as a result of females **not recognising the altered mating call** ($\\checkmark$). The population size and gene pool may also decrease if oestrogen pollution **reduces the lifespan of the frogs** ($\\checkmark$). Altered calls may also reduce the ability of the males to defend their territories against competitors and so may **disrupt the social dynamics** of the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0855","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0855\n\nWhich statement best illustrates the process of evolution?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A horse that increases its maximum running speed has faster offspring.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A random mutation gives resistance to a disease and spreads in the population.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations occur as needed to increase fitness in individuals and populations.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The most complex organisms are the most evolved.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because evolution is the change in heritable characteristics of a population, and random mutations alter heritable traits.  In this case the mutation confers resistance to a disease, making these individuals more likely to survive and pass on the mutation to their offspring.  Over time resistance to the disease becomes more prevalent in the population.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because traits acquired through an organism's lifetime cannot be passed on to the next generation.  **Choice C** is incorrect because mutations are random and do not occur because an individual or population requires a different trait.  **Choice D** is incorrect because complexity is not a measure of evolutionary progress, as evolution refers to change over time."},{"ref":"BI0856","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0856\n\nWhich of the following provides evidence for evolution through selective breeding?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Phenotypic variation in the domesticated dog (*Canis familiaris*) has increased significantly as a result of humans selecting desirable traits. |\n    | II. | Crop yield in domesticated crop plants is significantly greater than their wild relatives. |\n    | III. | *Homo sapiens* in many parts of the world have alleles that provide resistance to diseases endemic to their region. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because **Statements I** and **II** are correct.  **Statement I** supports the theory of evolution because an increase in phenotypes is a component of the process of evolution.  **Statement II** supports the theory of evolution because it exemplifies that humans, selecting for desirable traits, can cause directional selection in crop plants.\n\nAlthough **Statement III** does support the theory of evolution, it does not do so as an example of selective breeding.  Humans have not been selectively bred.  **Choices B, C** and **D** do not exclusively include **Statements I and II**, so they are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0857","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0857\n\nShown in diagrams A and B are two pairs of similar structures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0857.png\" alt=\"BI0857\">\n\nWhich of the following statements correctly explain the structural similarities shown in diagrams A and B?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Structural similarities in diagram A are primarily due to random mutations, not common ancestry. |\n | II. | Structural similarities in diagram B are due to the fact they both perform similar functions. |\n | III. | Diagram A depicts analogous structures, and diagram B depicts homologous structures. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because only **statement II** correctly explains the structural similarities.\n\nThe bird and insect wings are similar due to convergent evolution. **Statement III** is incorrect because diagram A depicts homologous structures, and diagram B depicts analogous structures. **Statement I** is incorrect because humans and bats evolved from a common ancestor, and random mutations slowly gave rise to differences in the pentadactyl limb between different species. **Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect because they include either **statements I** or **III**, or both."},{"ref":"BI0858","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0858\n\nWhat sequence of events usually occur during the process of speciation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reproductive isolation → one species becomes two species → random mutations\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Random mutations → reproductive isolation → one species becomes another species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Geographic isolation → reproductive isolation → one species becomes another species\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Geographic isolation → reproductive isolation → one species becomes two species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because geographic isolation leads to a buildup of different mutations between two populations, leading to reproductive isolation and the production of two species from one.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as reproductive isolation cannot occur prior to a buildup of random mutations.  **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect because speciation involves one species becoming two, not one."},{"ref":"BI0859","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0859\n\nIt is thought that the Congo River divided a population of the common ancestor of bonobos (*Pan paniscus*) and chimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*), resulting in geographic isolation. The south side of the river has more stable food availability, while the north side experiences more frequent food shortages.\n\nWhich correctly pairs theoretical selection pressures and consequent adaptations of the two populations?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0859.png\" alt=\"BI0859\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because a scarcity of food (alongside competition with gorillas) led to increased aggression in the ancestral population of chimpanzees. Plentiful food on the south side of the river (as well as an absence of gorillas) led to increased cooperation in the ancestral population of bonobos.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because there was and is no difference in solar radiation on either side of the Congo River. **Choice C** is incorrect because the selection pressures and adaptations are reversed in terms of where they existed (south side vs. north side).  **Choice D** is incorrect because food scarcity led to more aggression, and plentiful food led to more cooperation."},{"ref":"BI0860","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0860\n\nThe base sequence between two species was found to be 78% the same, while the order of amino acids found in the same cell types between two species was 82% the same.  What may be the reason for this?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is degenerate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations occur more frequently in DNA that codes for proteins.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Techniques used to analyse base sequences of DNA are less reliable.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is universal.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because what is meant by “the genetic code is degenerate” is multiple codons sometimes code for the same amino acid, which means that sometimes a mutation will change the genetic code, but the same amino acid is still coded for.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as mutations generally occur more frequently in DNA that does not code for proteins. **Choice C** is incorrect as techniques used to analyse the base sequence of DNA are more reliable than those used to determine the order of amino acids.  **Choice D** is incorrect as the universality of the genetic code refers to the fact that all organisms use the same genetic code."},{"ref":"BI0861","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0861\n\nThe overall structure of the flipper of a whale, in terms of bones and muscles, is similar to the overall structure of the limb of a horse.  What are these structures referred to as?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ancestral structures\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Convergent structures\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Analogous structures\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Homologous structures","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because homologous structures refer to structures inherited from a common ancestor which are similar but often used for different functions.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as ancestral structures refer to structures present in an ancestor species, not structures found in present day species.  **Choice B** is incorrect as convergent structures are not a term used in biology.  Convergent evolution, on the other hand, leads to analogous structures. **Choice C** is incorrect as analogous structures refer to structures used for the same function, which are significantly different when closely inspected.  Analogous structures also have different evolutionary origins."},{"ref":"BI0862","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0862\n\nWhich of the following is the strongest evidence supporting the hypothesis that two populations are two different species?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Allele frequencies differ between the two populations.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The two populations are reproductively isolated.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The DNA sequence of some genes was found to be different.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The two populations are geographically isolated.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because two reproductively isolated populations are a key component of the definition of a species.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because allele frequencies often differ between different populations of the same species. **Choice C** is incorrect because varying DNA sequences in genes confirms the existence of different alleles, not the existence of two species.  **Choice D** is incorrect because even when two populations are geographically isolated, they may still be the same species."},{"ref":"BI1154","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1154\n\nWhich pair of structures would be considered analogous?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1154.png\" alt=\"BI1154\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnalogous structures refer to structures found in different species which serve a similar function, but are not the result of common ancestry. Sharks are cartilaginous fish, and their fins are modified pectoral fins made of cartilage. Whales are mammals, and their fins are modified forelimbs, which are homologous to the forelimbs of land-dwelling mammals. Whale and shark fins serve the same purpose, are similar in structure, and are the product of convergent evolution.\n\nThe structures in **Choices A** and **B** share similar functions, but they also share a common ancestry, making them homologous, not analogous. **Choice C** cannot be correct as they share a common ancestry but are quite different in terms of structure and function, also making them homologous."},{"ref":"BI1232","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1232\n\nWhich of the following statement concerning analogous structures is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | They are formed through convergent evolution |\n    | II. | They perform the same or similar functions in different organisms |\n    | III. | They evolve from a common ancestor |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnalogous structures refer to structures found in different species which serve a similar function (**Statement II**) but are not the result of common ancestry, they are formed through convergent evolution (**Statement I**), which is when different organisms evolve similar structures but have not arisen from a common ancestor. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nAnalogous structures do not evolve from a common ancestor (**Statement III**); **Choices C** and **D**  are incorrect. **Choice A**  is incorrect because it only involves **statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1291","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1291\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe pale and dark forms of the peppered moth (Biston betularia) are a classic example of natural selection. The graph shows the changes in the population of pale (mostly white) and dark (mostly black) peppered moths (*Biston betularia*) during and post the industrial revolution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291.png\" alt=\"BI1291\">\n\nThe image shows the phenotype of pale and dark moths on white tree bark.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291b.png\" alt=\"BI1291b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Bedin, T. (2020, May 26). *Tilpasning og evolusjon - natural science (PB) - NDLA. ndla.no*. Retrieved October 02, 2023, from https://ndla.no/en/subject:1:bb834c76-d1e4-46c4-8c0a-8f978bd2c956/topic:6:f604b380-5cdb-4227-885e-bc9b57cb3237/topic:3:aa019f6a-a569-4491-b0d2-c8d2df21733f/resource:09fc6c1a-eaa1-4901-b2ae-ee5e7ce1900c. Copyright free]\n\n1. Distinguish between the changes in the numbers of pale and dark moths shown in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline how the trends in the graph can be explained by natural selection. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a technique a group of students could use to compare the population sizes of peppered moths between a deciduous (broadleaved) and a coniferous (evergreen) wood. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in dark-coloured moths between 1850 and 1900. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a line on the graph, predicting how the number of pale moths would have changed, if the industrial revolution did not happen. [1 mark]\n\n5. In addition to collecting data such as that shown in the graph, scientists rely on various forms of evidence to explore the concept of evolution. State two other forms of evidence that supports the theory of evolution. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1800 and 1850 **OR** around the middle of the Industrial Revolution, the number of pale moths remained high **OR** between 95 and 100% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths remained low **OR** between 0 and 5% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1850, the number of pale moths began to rapidly decrease **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths rapidly increased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ around 1870 **OR** just before 1875, the number of dark moths surpasses the number of pale moths [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1900, the number of pale moths was low **OR** less than 5% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths was high **OR** around 95% [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Between **1800 and 1850**, the number of **pale** moths was between **95 and 100%**, whereas the number of **dark** moths was between **0 and 5%** ($\\checkmark$). However, **from 1850** the number of **pale** moths **decreased** rapidly, whereas the number of **dark** moths **increased** rapidly ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection explains how individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive to reproduce [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ before the Industrial Revolution, lichens **OR** mosses **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork were white, so the pale moths were able to camouflage from predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rates of pollution **OR** soot during the Industrial Revolution caused white lichen and mosses on lichen **OR** moss **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork to turn black [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pale moths were more visible to predators, so their numbers decreased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dark moths were able to camouflage from predators, so were better able to survive and breed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the dark moths gained a selective advantage over the pale moths **OR** were selected for **OR** are under directional selection **OR** pale moths faced intense selection pressure due to predation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Before the Industrial Revolution, **lichen and mosses** on trees were **white**, so the pale moths were able to **camouflage from predators** ($\\checkmark$). However, **post-Industrial Revolution**, there were increased rates of **pollution** which caused trees to turn **black** ($\\checkmark$). This meant that the dark moths were able to **camouflage** from predators, so were better able to **survive and breed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ capture-mark-release-recapture [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One technique that students could use is the **mark-release-recapture** of **peppered** moths ($\\checkmark$) in coniferous and deciduous woods.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1800 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291c.png\" alt=\"BI1291c\">\n\n Percentage increase = ((final value - initial value) ÷ initial value) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = ((95 - 5) ÷ 5 ) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = **1800%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line should continue the same trend from before 1850 to 1900 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291d.png\" alt=\"BI1291d\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequencing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **fossil record** ($\\checkmark$) and **DNA sequencing** ($\\checkmark$) provide evidence for the theory of evolution."},{"ref":"BI1316","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1316\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline three sources which provide evidence for evolution.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how both bats and butterflies have wings.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the process of speciation with at least one named example.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Evidence for evolution can come from **DNA sequences** ($\\checkmark$), **amino acid sequences** ($\\checkmark$), or **homologous structures** ($\\checkmark$) such as the pentadactyl limb.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bat wings and butterfly wings  do not share a common ancestor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bats share common ancestry with other mammals, whereas butterflies share common ancestry with other insects **OR** bats are mammals **OR** Mammalia, whereas butterflies are insects **OR** Arthropoda     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings evolved through convergent evolution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution is the process where unrelated organisms evolve similar characteristics **OR** traits to perform similar functions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings in bats and butterflies are an example of analogous structures         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures share a similar function but are not the result of a common ancestor                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings evolved in response to similar environmental pressures **OR** ecological niches **OR** as a result of natural selection favouring flight     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings in bats are modified forelimbs **OR** are made of skin and bone **WHEREAS** butterfly wings are not **OR** are made of chitin         [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Bats and butterflies do not share a recent **common ancestor** ($\\checkmark$), but both evolved wings due to **convergent evolution** ($\\checkmark$). Convergent evolution is the process by which unrelated organisms can **evolve similar traits** due to similar necessities, such as the need to fly ($\\checkmark$). This means that wings in bats and butterflies are an example of **analogous structures** ($\\checkmark$) because they share a **similar function** but do not result from a common ancestor ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ speciation is the process by which a population is split and evolves differently over time to form distinct species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity exists within a population **OR** genetic variation exists within a population      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ members of a population become reproductively isolated                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sympatric speciation occurs when new species evolve within the same geographic area       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopatric speciation occurs when new species form in separate geographic areas due to geographical isolation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation can be due to geographical **OR** physical **OR** behavioural **OR** temporal barriers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each population is exposed to different environmental conditions **OR** selective pressures [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each population evolves differently over time **OR** different adaptations evolve over time **OR** mutations affect each group differently **OR** differential selection **OR** genetic drift affects populations differently    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these differences act as barriers to gene flow     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, the groups evolve differently and can no longer interbreed, so new species have been formed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bonobos and chimpanzees **OR** any other suitable example of speciation     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Speciation is the process by which a population becomes split, and each group evolves differently over time, leading to the formation of **new species** ($\\checkmark$). This is possible because **variation** exists within a population ($\\checkmark$). When members of a population become **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$) by a geographical barrier like a river ($\\checkmark$), both groups are exposed to different environmental conditions or **selective pressures** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, the two groups evolve differently as different mutations affect both groups, this is referred to as **differential selection** ($\\checkmark$). These differences between the populations act as **barriers to gene flow** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, the groups evolve so differently that they can no longer **interbreed** and are two distinct species ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1377","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1377\n\nWhich characteristic(s) is/are observed in both natural selection and selective breeding?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Favourable traits are selected|\n    | II. | Increase in variation |\n    | III. | Change is driven by the environment |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct because in both selective breeding and natural selection, favourable characteristics are “selected” by either the environment (natural selection) or humans (selective breeding). **Statement II** is incorrect because natural selection and selective breeding don’t increase variation, it influences the proportion of existing variants. **Statement III** is incorrect because selective breeding is not driven by the environment, it is driven by humans. **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statement II** and/or **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1577","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1577\n\nWhich of the following pairs of limbs are homologous?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577A.png\" alt=\"BI1577A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577B.png\" alt=\"BI1577B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577C.png\" alt=\"BI1577C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577D.png\" alt=\"BI1577D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the horse and sea lion limbs have the same bones in the same order. This is the result of common ancestry between the two species, making these structures homologous despite their different functions.\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **C** are incorrect because these are examples of analogous structures as a result of convergent evolution. Analogous structures share a common function but do not share a common ancestry. A further clue is that horse and sea lion are both mammals, whereas the other pairs of animals belong to different taxonomic groups (**Choice A** = mammal, insect; **Choice B** = mammal, crustacean; **Choice C**= mollusc, echinoderm)."},{"ref":"BI2803","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2803\n\nWhich statement is true for both Lamarckism and epigenetic inheritance?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both involve changes in the DNA sequence that are inherited and lead to evolution.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both involve inherited changes caused by the environment, but the effect of epigenetics may only last a few generations, so it is not a type of evolution.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both operate by the use and disuse of traits, leading to evolutionary change.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only epigenetics leads to changes in the DNA sequence and, therefore, evolution.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because both Lamarckism and epigenetic inheritance propose that environmental factors can cause inherited changes, while Lamarckism suggested these changes were permanent and led to evolution through traits acquired during an organism's lifetime. Epigenetic changes involve reversible chemical modifications to DNA or histone proteins that affect gene expression without altering the DNA sequence. These epigenetic changes can be passed to offspring, but often only persist for a few generations and typically do not lead to long-term evolutionary change.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because neither Lamarckism nor epigenetic inheritance involves changes to the DNA sequence itself. Epigenetics modifies gene expression through chemical tags, while Lamarckism assumed physical or behavioural changes were inherited.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because only Lamarckism proposed that the use or disuse of traits directly causes evolutionary change. Epigenetic inheritance does not involve the use or disuse of structures but rather chemical changes to gene regulation influenced by environmental factors. Furthermore, epigenetics does not lead to evolution.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because epigenetic inheritance does not involve changes to the DNA sequence itself and therefore does not cause long-term evolutionary change."},{"ref":"BI2804","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2804\n\nThe table below shows the percentage of the amino acid sequences of cytochrome c shared between a human and four other species.\n\n| Species Pair | Cytochrome c sequence shared (%) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Human–chimpanzee | 99 |\n| Human–mouse | 87 |\n| Human–chicken | 75 |\n| Human–zebrafish | 50 |\n\nWhich conclusion is best supported by these data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A human and a chimpanzee shared a common ancestor more recently than a human and a mouse.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cytochrome c has evolved faster in the chicken lineage than in the mouse lineage.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The human–zebrafish divergence occurred most recently.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The molecular clock runs at different rates in mammals and fish.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because there is a higher percentage similarity for the cytochrome c amino acid sequence between the human and chimpanzee (99%) compared to the human and mouse (87%). This indicates less time for mutations to accumulate, meaning humans and chimpanzees shared a common ancestor more recently than humans and mice.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although the cytochrome c percentage similarity is lower in chickens (75%) than in mice (87%), this alone does not confirm that cytochrome c evolved faster in the chicken lineage. To determine evolutionary rates, both sequence similarity and known divergence times are needed.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the lowest sequence similarity (50%) between human and zebrafish suggests the longest time since divergence, not the most recent.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because differences in sequence similarity alone cannot demonstrate variation in molecular clock rates. The molecular clock is a concept based on the idea that mutations accumulate at a relatively constant rate over time in most species. To conclude rate differences, both the time since divergence and the number of accrued changes must both be known."},{"ref":"BI2805","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2805\n\nWhich breeding practice most directly illustrates artificial selection of a polygenic trait?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Allowing the organisms with traits that fit the environment best to pass them on to the next generation.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Breeding plants which are susceptible to a specific bacteria with those that contain the resistance gene.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing only the tallest wheat plants each generation for an increase in yield.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Introducing wild genes to increase genetic diversity in a breed.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because artificial selection on a polygenic trait involves humans selecting individuals with desirable values for a trait that shows continuous variation, such as plant height, which is typically controlled by multiple genes. By repeatedly crossing the tallest wheat plants, breeders shift the population’s average height upwards over successive generations.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it describes natural selection, where environmental pressures—not human choice—determine which traits are passed on. In this scenario, humans are letting the environment dictate which individuals breed more successfully, so it is not an example of artificial selection.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because crossing a disease-resistant plant with those that are susceptible to the disease is an example of artificial selection, but not on a polygenic trait, as it only involves one gene.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because introducing wild genes to increase genetic diversity adds variation to the gene pool, but does not involve selecting for a specific trait."},{"ref":"BI2806","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2806\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nChimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*) and bonobos (*Pan paniscus*) diverged from a common ancestor around 1.25 million years ago. This event is thought to have been influenced by the formation of the Congo River.\n\nThe diagram shows the ranges of chimpanzees and bonobos.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI2806.png\" alt=\"BI2806\">\n[Source: Adapted from Guy, Cp. (2016, May 24). *File: Bonobo vs chimp.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 12 , 2024 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Bonobo_vs_chimp.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Chimpanzees and bonobos are two different species that share a recent common ancestor. State **three** similarities or differences between them that would result from this shared ancestry and subsequent divergence.    [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Name and explain which type of speciation is likely to have occurred between chimpanzees and bonobos. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Describe the role of differential selection in the speciation event between chimpanzees and bonobos.                [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. Explain how the process of speciation affects biodiversity.                 [1 mark]\n\n    2. Suggest why gradual evolutionary change within a single species is not considered speciation in the absence of reproductive isolation.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following *(at least **one** from each section)*:\n\n    ***Similarities:***\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ similar DNA base sequences **OR** similar mitochondrial DNA sequences             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ similar amino acid sequences **OR** cytochrome c **OR** named example         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ similar features **OR** morphology **OR** homologous structures **OR** named example     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences:***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in some gene sequences **OR** proteins due to gene mutations since divergence  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in specific behaviours **OR** social structures      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in morphology due to adaptations since divergence          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unable to breed and produce fertile offspring         [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Chimpanzees and bonobos have **similar DNA base sequences** ($\\checkmark$) and **amino acid sequences** in **proteins** ($\\checkmark$) because they share a recent common ancestor. However, they are now **unable to breed and produce fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$), which is why they are not the same species.\n\n2.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ allopatric speciation          [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ a geographical barrier **OR** a river separates the two populations, preventing gene flow between them         [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        It is likely to be **allopatric** speciation ($\\checkmark$) because the **Congo River separates** the two populations, **preventing gene flow** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ different environmental conditions **OR** selection pressures in the separated populations favoured different phenotypes         [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection acted differently on each population, increasing the frequency of different alleles in each group             [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, genetic differences accumulated        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ this led to reproductive isolation as the populations became too genetically distinct to interbreed     [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        DIfferential selection helped lead to speciation because there were **different environmental conditions** ($\\checkmark$) in the different places, This meant **different alleles** were selected and **increased in frequency** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    1.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ speciation increases biodiversity **BECAUSE** it raises the total number of species on Earth    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Speciation leads to two species where there was previously one, so it will **increase** the total **number of species**, which **increases biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation is needed to prevent interbreeding **OR** to separate gene pools    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ without this, individuals can still mate and produce fertile offspring **OR** alleles are shared within the population                    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ gradual changes within a species without reproductive isolation represent variation within a species **NOT** the formation of a new species        [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Reproductive isolation is needed to **prevent interbreeding** ($\\checkmark$). If individuals can still interbreed, they can **share alleles across the gene pool** ($\\checkmark$). This means there is no accumulation of differences, which leads to speciation."},{"ref":"BI2807","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2807\n\nWhich of the following statements accurately describes characteristics of homologous structures?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Homologous structures are defined as functionally similar anatomical features that arise through divergent evolution. |\n    | II. | Although bats and dolphins exhibit distinct external morphology, their forelimbs evolved from a common ancestor. |\n    | III. | Bird wings and insect wings originate from the same embryonic tissues and are therefore homologous. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhile homologous structures do arise via divergent evolution, they are not defined by functional similarity. In fact, their function may differ considerably, for example, the forelimbs of bats for flight versus the flippers of dolphins for swimming. Homology is established based on shared structural origin, not shared function; **Statement I** is false.\\\nHomologous structures are anatomical features found in different species that share a common evolutionary origin, even if they have diverged in form or function. Both bats and dolphins have a pentadactyl limb structure in their forelimbs, despite a differing external appearance and function. This reflects divergent evolution from a shared ancestor; **Statement II** is true.\\\nBat wings and insect wings are analogous structures. Although they perform a similar function (flight), they evolved independently in different evolutionary lineages and do not share embryological origin. Bat wings derive from the vertebrate limb, while insect wings arise from outgrowths of the arthropod exoskeleton. **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice B** is the correct answer because it only includes the one true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **III**."}]},{"name":"Nucleic Acids","questions":[{"ref":"BI0038","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0038\n\nThe diagram shows the structure of a DNA fragment.\n\nWhich letter points to the bonds that easily break and reform?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0038.png\" alt=\"BI0038\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from User: Madprime. (2010, March). *File: DNA chemical structure jp.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 15, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_chemical_structure_jp.svg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe bonds between the nitrogenous bases holding the two strands together are hydrogen bonds. Hydrogen bonds typically break and reform easily; therefore  **Choice B** is the correct answer to the question.\n\nThe nitrogenous bases consist of single and double covalent bonds (**Choice A**) between nitrogen and carbon atoms. Deoxyribose sugars have single covalent bonds (**Choice C**) between the carbon atoms forming the pentose sugar. There are covalent bonds between the phosphate and sugar and between the phosphate and base (**Choice D**). Covalent bonds do not typically break and reform easily."},{"ref":"BI0039","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0039\n\nThe diagram below shows transcription.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0039.png\" alt=\"BI0039\">\n\nWhat do structures X and Y have in common?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both are made up of phosphate groups, ribose sugar and nitrogenous bases.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both contain hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both contain sugar-phosphate bonds.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both have six-carbon monosaccharides in their structure.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStructure X is DNA, and structure Y is RNA. Both structures are made up of phosphate groups, sugars and nitrogenous bases. Both structures contain sugar-phosphate bonds, a type of covalent bond, between phosphate groups and sugars. **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\nThe sugar in DNA is deoxyribose and the sugar in RNA is ribose (**Choice A**). The sugar molecule in both DNA and RNA is a pentose sugar which means it has five carbon atoms, not six (**Choice D**).\n\nRNA does not contain thymine (**Choice B**) and it is single-stranded."},{"ref":"BI0043","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0043\n\nWhich statements are characteristics of a DNA molecule?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The two strands are identical. |\n  | II. | The two strands are antiparallel to one another. |\n  | III. | The sugar-phosphate backbone is held together by covalent bonds. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA DNA molecule is made up of two antiparallel strands of nucleotides. Antiparallel means that both strands are geometrically parallel but oriented in opposite directions (**Option II**). One of the strands is in the direction of 5' to 3', whereas the other strand is in the direction of 3' to 5'. The backbones, made of alternating sugar and phosphate groups, are held together by covalent sugar-phosphate bonds (**Option III**). Both of these strands are joined together by the hydrogen bonds between the complementary base pairs.\n\nDNA strands are not identical (**Option I**) because identical would mean that they have the same nucleotide arrangement, instead they are complementary to each other."},{"ref":"BI0045","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0045\n\nWhat distinguishes uracil from thymine?\n\n| | Uracil | Thymine |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Joined to a pentose sugar | Joined to a hexose sugar |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Found in RNA | Found in DNA |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Two-ring structure  | Single-ring structure |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Complementary to cytosine | Complementary to adenine |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUracil and thymine are nitrogenous bases found in two types of nucleic acids: DNA and RNA.\n\nUracil is found in RNA, whereas thymine is found in DNA. (**Choice B**)\n\nIn RNA, Uracil is not complementary to cystine, it is complementary with adenine, and thymine is also complementary to adenine\n (**Choice D**).\n\nIn both DNA and RNA, these nitrogenous bases are joined to a five-carbon sugar, also called a pentose sugar which has a ring structure, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nBoth uracil and thymine are pyrimidines which are single-ring structures (**Choice C**). Adenine and guanine are purines which consist of two rings."},{"ref":"BI0047","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0047\n\nWhich of the following processes is most directly involved in the formation of nucleic acids such as DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Catabolism\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNucleotides are linked together by condensation reactions to form molecules of DNA (**Choice B**). The formation of DNA requires the removal of water for the joining of smaller molecules to form a larger molecule.\n\nThe joining of nucleotides together can also be classified as an anabolic reaction but not as a catabolic reaction (**Choice C**). Catabolism is the breaking down of a large molecule into a smaller molecule. An example of catabolism is hydrolysis which is a reaction that breaks down a large molecule into a smaller molecule by adding water (**Choice A**).\n\nTranslation is a process which follows the transcription of DNA (**Choice D**). It is where mRNA is used as a template by ribosomes to synthesize polypeptides."},{"ref":"BI0049","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0049\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe image below shows a short fragment of the DNA molecule.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0049.png\" alt=\"BI0049\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from User: Madprime. (2010, March). *File: DNA chemical structure jp.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 15, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_chemical_structure_jp.svg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Define the term ‘antiparallel’ in the context of a DNA molecule. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structures of DNA and RNA.  [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two DNA strands run in opposite directions **OR** one strand is oriented 5' to 3', while the other is 3' to 5' [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n   Antiparallel is the arrangement of the **two strands** in the **double helix**, where they run in **opposite directions**, with **one strand oriented 5' to 3'** and the **other 3' to 5'**  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    *At least **one** statement for compare and **one** statement for contrast*\n\n    **compare**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are nucleic acids **OR**  composed of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain adenine **OR** A **AND** cytosine **OR** C **AND** guanine **OR** G [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a sugar-phosphate backbone [1 mark]\n\n    **contrast**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is double-stranded **WHEREAS** RNA is single-stranded [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA contains thymine **OR** T  **WHEREAS** RNA has uracil **OR** U [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA has deoxyribose **WHEREAS** RNA contains ribose [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ structure of DNA is more stable **THAN** RNA [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Both** DNA and RNA are **nucleic acids** composed of **nucleotides**  ($\\checkmark$) and they both have a **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$). They also contain the nitrogenous bases **adenine** (A), **cytosine** (C), and **guanine** (G) ($\\checkmark$), however, **DNA** contains **thymine** (T), while **RNA** has **uracil** (U) ($\\checkmark$). **DNA** is **double-stranded**, whereas **RNA** is **single-stranded**  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0321","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0321\n\nThe graph below shows the results of an experiment conducted by a team of researchers investigating the relationship between the composition and melting point of different DNA samples. At the melting point, the DNA double helix separates into two individual strands.\n\nTwo DNA samples were compared, one consisting of only PQ pairs and another one consisting of only RS pairs. Letters P, Q, R and S represent different nitrogenous bases found in the DNA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0321.png\" alt=\"BI0321\">\n\nBased on the results presented in the graph, and your knowledge of nucleotide base pairing, deduce which nitrogenous bases correspond to the letters P, Q, R and S.\n\n| | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ P $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ Q $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ R $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ S $\\hspace{2.5em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Cytosine | Guanine | Thymine | Adenine |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Adenine | Cytosine | Guanine | Thymine |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Guanine | Adenine | Thymine | Cytosine |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Thymine | Adenine | Cytosine | Guanine |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nAdenine pairs up with thymine and cytosine pairs up with guanine. Hence, PQ or RS would either be adenine with thymine or cytosine with guanine (**Choices A** and **D**), but definitely not **Choices B** or **C**.\n\nCytosine and guanine pairing has three hydrogen bonds, whereas the adenine and thymine pairing has only two hydrogen bonds. At $95^oC$, there is a smaller percentage of RS-rich DNA melted content compared to PQ-rich DNA.\n\nThis shows that RS would be cytosine and guanine pairing (**Choice D**) and PQ is adenine and thymine. Because the extra hydrogen bond between C and G makes it more temperature resistant compared to A and T."},{"ref":"BI0541","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0541\n\nA strand of nucleotides reads CCUAGAGCUUACCG. Which type of pentose sugar could be found in the nucleotides in the natural composition of this strand?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deoxyribose only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribose only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both deoxyribose and ribose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neither deoxyribose nor ribose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe nucleotide strand CCUAGAGCUUACCG contains the bases cytosine (C), uracil (U), adenine (A), and guanine (G). While C, A, and G are found in both DNA and RNA, the base uracil is only found in RNA. So, the strand is an RNA strand.\n\nRNA contains the pentose sugar ribose. Therefore, ribose is found in the nucleotides in the strand.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because deoxyribose is only found in DNA, not RNA. The nucleotide strand cannot be DNA because it contains uracil in place of thymine (T).\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the strand is RNA which contains ribose."},{"ref":"BI0841","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0841\n\nHow does the genetic code provide evidence of universal common ancestry?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code of mitochondria is identical to the genetic code of the nucleus in eukaryotes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each amino acid is coded for by only one codon.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations cannot cause changes in the sequence of bases in the genome.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is the same across all three domains of life, with just a few exceptions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe genetic code is largely the same in all known organisms. There are a few exceptions such as mitochondrial DNA which can vary slightly in codon assignments for specific amino acids, and some bacterial species. Therefore **Choice D** is correct .\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the genetic code used in mitochondria is similar to the genetic code used in the nucleus of eukaryotic cells but not entirely the same. **Choice B** is incorrect because the genetic code is degenerate. There are 64 possible codons in the genetic code, but only 20 amino acids. As a result, some amino acids are coded for by more than one codon, providing redundancy and flexibility in protein synthesis. **Choice C** is incorrect because mutations are changes in the genetic code. As a result, mutations play a significant role in generating genetic diversity and are a driving force in the process of evolution."},{"ref":"BI1047","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms.\n\n1. Draw and label a diagram of a nucleotide. $^*$ [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the variation in base sequences leads to the ability of DNA to have an enormous capacity for storing genetic information. [5 marks]\n\n3. DNA is a polymer of nucleotides. Describe the structure of DNA. [7 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group drawn as a circle in correct place. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ribose sugar drawn as a pentagon in correct place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base drawn as a rectangle in correct place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all three parts labelled correctly [1 mark]\n\n *Bonds and names of nitrogenous bases do not need to be labelled*\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1047.png\" alt=\"BI1047\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference given to gain full marks*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is stored in the sequence of nitrogenous bases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three bases **OR** a codon, codes for one amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 64 codons but only 20 amino acids **OR** degenerate code [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for each extra nucleotide along a DNA strand, the number of possible combinations of bases increases exponentially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations introduce new genetic information [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ certain sequences are conserved across species because they encode vital information [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA molecules can be hundreds of millions of base pairs long **OR** DNA molecules are very long [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-coding regions have regulatory functions, contributing to the complexity of gene expression and overall genetic information [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The genetic code resides within the **sequences** of nitrogenous **bases** ($\\checkmark$) along a DNA molecule. Amino acids are coded for by **codons** ($\\checkmark$), and the code is **degenerate** ($\\checkmark$), in other words, some amino acids are coded for by more than one codon. The longer the DNA molecule, the greater the number of possible combinations of bases along the DNA strand, in fact there is an **exponential increase** in possibilities ($\\checkmark$) for each single nucleotide added to the polymer. **Mutations** also add to the variety ($\\checkmark$) of the genetic code.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following: \\\n*(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is a double helix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar OR deoxyribose **OR** pentose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T **AND** C pairs up with G [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a polymer of nucleotides. Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n **One end of the phosphate group is bonded to the 5’ carbon of the sugar** ($\\checkmark$) through a condensation reaction. On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide through a condensation reaction. Both bonds are **covalent bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the sugar-phosphate backbone. The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$)also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A pairs up with T, and C always pairs up with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1166","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1166\n\nWhich statement is correct concerning the structure of DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A phosphate within a nucleotide is covalently bonded to a nitrogenous base\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA molecules are made up of two parallel strands running in the same 3'-5' direction, linked by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bonding between adjacent phosphates and deoxyribose sugars forms the backbone of DNA\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adenine is complementary to uracil, and cytosine is complementary to guanine","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCovalent bonding between adjacent phosphates and deoxyribose sugars forms the backbone of DNA; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nWithin nucleotides, the phosphate is covalently bonded to the deoxyribose sugar and the sugar is attached to the base; **Choice A**  is incorrect. DNA molecules are made up of two antiparallel strands (they are parallel but run in opposite directions, one runs 3’-5’ and the other 5’-3’), linked by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases; **Choice B**  is incorrect. In DNA, adenine is complementary to thymine; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1221","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1221\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms. ATP is a type of nucleotide.\n\n1. Distinguish between ATP and the monomers of DNA in humans. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of the different types of RNA involved in translation.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the structure of the DNA double helix.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP contains three phosphate groups **WHEREAS** each DNA nucleotide contains only one phosphate group         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP has adenine for a nitrogenous base **WHEREAS** a DNA nucleotide can contain adenine, cytosine, guanine or thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a component of ATP is ribose **WHEREAS** DNA nucleotides have deoxyribose instead        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP stores energy **OR** provides energy, **WHEREAS** DNA carries **OR** transmits genetic information                          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can perform its function as a single monomer **WHEREAS** DNA can only perform its function, **OR** carry genetic material as a polymer            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is found throughout the cell **OR** primarily in the cytoplasm **OR** mitochondria **WHEREAS** DNA is found primarily in the nucleus **OR** only found in the nucleus AND mitochondria            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both ATP and DNA molecules are nucleic acids which contain a phosphate group, a sugar and a base. The structural difference between a molecule of ATP and a molecule of DNA is that one ATP molecule contains **three phosphate groups** whereas one DNA nucleotide contains **one phosphate group** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the nitrogenous base in ATP is **adenine** whereas there are four possible nitrogenous bases in one nucleotide of DNA which are **adenine, cytosine, guanine and thymine** ($\\checkmark$). The functional difference is that ATP **stores energy**, whereas DNA **carries genetic material** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (there must be at least one statement for each of mRNA, tRNA and rRNA to achieve maximum marks):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA serves as a template for protein synthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ codons of mRNA  specify positioning of  amino acids along polypeptide **OR** codons guide the assembly of amino acids into a polypeptide         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA molecules carry amino acids to mRNA **OR** ribosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodons on tRNA complementary to codons on mRNA allow amino acids to be positioned correctly along the polypeptide **OR** anticodons bind to codons to bring corresponding amino acid to the ribosome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA provides the ribosome's structural framework **OR** forms ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA catalyses the formation of peptide bonds between adjacent amino acids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA facilitates binding of mRNA and tRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA orchestrate protein synthesis during translation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Together, mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA** orchestrate the process of **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$) during translation. **mRNA carries** the genetic **information** from **DNA** to the **ribosome**  ($\\checkmark$). **Codons** on the mRNA **specify the positioning** of **amino acids** along the **polypeptide** chain  ($\\checkmark$). Each **tRNA** molecule has a specific **anticodon** sequence that is **complementary** to a **codon** on the mRNA  ($\\checkmark$). This base-pairing specificity allows tRNA to bring the **corresponding amino acid** to the **correct position** to form the polypeptide  ($\\checkmark$). The rRNA **catalyses** the formation of **peptide bonds** between adjacent amino acids  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following: \\\n    *(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand of DNA is a polymer of nucleotides         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand has a sugar-phosphate backbone            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ four nitrogenous bases A **OR** adenine **AND** T **OR** thymine **AND** C **OR** cytosine **AND** G **OR** guanine              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose                           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide)                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T AND C pairs up with G        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a **polymer of nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) . Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    One end of the **phosphate group** is bonded to the **5’ carbon** of the **deoxyribose sugar** ($\\checkmark$). On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide. Both bonds are covalent **sugar-phosphate bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$).  The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$) also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n    **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A** pairs up **with T**, and **C** always pairs up **with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1382","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1382\n\nWhich set of bonds found in nucleic acids are correctly identified in the table below?\n\n| | Bonds between phosphates and pentose sugars | Bonds between nucleotide bases |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ |Covalent | Hydrogen |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Covalent |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Covalent | Covalent |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Hydrogen |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the nucleic acid backbone is held together by covalently bonded sugars and phosphates, while the nucleotide bases are held together by hydrogen bonds.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the types of bonds are reversed. **Choice C** is incorrect because the bonds between nucleotides are hydrogen bonds, not covalent bonds. **Choice D** is incorrect because the bonds between phosphates and pentose sugars are covalent, not hydrogen bonds."},{"ref":"BI1394","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1394\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct structure of a DNA nucleotide?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/1394ii.png\" alt=\"1394ii\">\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it shows the nitrogenous base bonded to the 1’ carbon of the pentose sugar and the phosphate bonded to the 5’ carbon. Even though the structure is inverted the bonding remains accurate, as this is how it would appear on the antiparallel DNA strand.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it shows the phosphate bonded to the oxygen on the pentose sugar, which is not possible. **Choice C** is incorrect as it shows incorrect bonding for the nucleotide base, which is attached to the 2’ carbon. The phosphate is shown bonded to the 3’ end of the sugar, which would be part of the next nucleotide subunit. **Choice D** is incorrect as it shows incorrect bonding for the nucleotide base, again connected to the 2’ carbon."},{"ref":"BI1477","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1477\n\nWhich diagram shows the structure of RNA?\n\n| $\\text{A}$. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1477%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1477 fig1\"> | $\\text{B}$. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1477%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1477 fig2\"> |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| C. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1477%20fig3.png\" alt=\"BI1477 fig3\"> | D. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1477%20fig4.png\" alt=\"BI1477 fig4\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nRNA is a single strand of nucleotides (Uracil, Guanine, Adenine and Cytosine), bonded together by a phosphodiester backbone. Each nucleotide consists of a phosphate group (represented by a circle), joined to a ribose sugar (represented by a pentagon) joined to a base (represented by a rectangle). Each nucleotide is joined to another by a covalent bond between the ribose sugar of one nucleotide and the phosphate of the next. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as RNA is single stranded, not double stranded. **Choice C** is incorrect as it shows a hexose sugar rather than a pentose sugar. **Choice D** is incorrect as the pentose sugars and phosphate groups are in the wrong positions."},{"ref":"BI1565","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1565\n\nWhich of the following represents the correct structure of RNA?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565A.png\" alt=\"BI1565A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565B.png\" alt=\"BI1565B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565C.png\" alt=\"BI1565C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565D.png\" alt=\"BI1565D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because RNA is a single-stranded nucleic acid that contains adenine (A), cytosine (C), uracil (U), and guanine (G) nucleotide bases, while DNA is a double-stranded nucleic acid containing thymine (T) instead of uracil. The nucleotide bases are covalently bonded to a pentose sugar, which is covalently bonded to a phosphate at the 5’ and 3’ ends.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as this is the structure of a DNA molecule, including a thymine base. **Choice B** is incorrect because RNA does not contain thymine. **Choice D** is incorrect because the diagram shows a double-stranded molecule similar to DNA, but the strands are incorrectly depicted as parallel instead of the correct anti-parallel orientation."},{"ref":"BI1849","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1849\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nBelow is a diagram showing a DNA and RNA molecule, both of which are made of nucleotides.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/1849.png\" alt=\"1849\">\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram of a nucleotide.$^*$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the structural difference between the sugars found in DNA and RNA nucleotides.\n [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline how an RNA molecule is formed through the joining of nucleotide monomers. [2 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pentose sugar **OR** ribose **OR** deoxyribose drawn in the centre and correctly labelled [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group drawn attached to C5 of pentose sugar and labelled (C5 does not need to be labelled) [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base drawn correctly on C1 of the pentose sugar and labelled (C1 does not need to be labelled) [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1849MSii.png\" alt=\"BI1849MSii\">\n\n2. *answer can be as annotated diagram*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deoxyribose **OR** DNA sugar has a hydrogen atom **OR** H attached to C2 **WHEREAS** ribose **OR** RNA sugar has a hydroxyl group **OR** OH attached to C2 [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1849MS2i.png\" alt=\"BI1849MS2i\">\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ joined together through condensation reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sugar-phosphate bond **OR** covalent bond forms between the nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bond forms between phosphate group of one nucleotide **AND** the 3' hydroxyl group **OR** C3 of the sugar **OR** ribose in next nucleotide [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n RNA is formed through **condensation reactions** ($\\checkmark$) where **sugar-phosphate bonds** join the **phosphate group** of **one nucleotide** to the **3' hydroxyl** group of the sugar in **another nucleotide** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1850","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1850\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/1850i.png\" alt=\"1850i\">\n\n1. Describe the role of DNA in storing genetic information. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the importance of the antiparallel arrangement of DNA strands in the structure of DNA. [3 marks]\n\n3. Outline the importance of the diversity of DNA base sequences in the process of evolution. [3 marks]\n\n4. Discuss the importance of the conservation of the genetic code in understanding universal common ancestry. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is stored in the sequence of nucleotide bases **OR** adenine, thymine, cytosine, guanine within the DNA molecule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ groups of three nucleotides **OR** bases form codons, which correspond to specific amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of codons determines the primary structure of proteins, which controls the organism’s traits and functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ double-helix structure ensures stability of genetic information and allows for accurate replication during cell division [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is degenerate **OR** multiple codons can code for same amino acid providing some protection against mutations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes can be turned on or off **OR** are used when needed **OR** can be expressed and repressed [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA stores genetic information in the form of **codons**, which are **sequences** of **three nucleotide bases** ($\\checkmark$) that **code** for specific **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). These codons **determine** the **structure of proteins**, which are essential for the **organism’s traits** and **functions** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two DNA strands run in opposite directions **OR** one strand runs 5' to 3', while the other runs 3' to 5' [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the complementary base pairs **OR** adenine-thymine AND guanine-cytosine to align properly and form hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding stabilises the double helix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antiparallel strands allow for the twisting of the DNA molecule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures that the DNA molecule is stable and can store genetic information effectively [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The antiparallel arrangement means that **one DNA strand** runs **5' to 3'**, while the **other runs 3' to 5'** ($\\checkmark$). This allows the **complementary base pairs** to **align** correctly and form **hydrogen bonds** between adenine-thymine and guanine-cytosine ($\\checkmark$). These **hydrogen bonds** help **stabilise** the **double helix** and allow the **DNA to twist** into its characteristic structure ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to genetic variation between individuals within a population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides the basis for natural selection, as certain traits may offer a selective advantage in specific environments [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing their favourable alleles **OR** genes to the next generation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, advantageous traits become more common, driving evolution and the development of new species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows populations to adapt to changing environments, contributing to species' survival [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The diversity of DNA base sequences leads to **genetic variation** between **individuals** in a **population** ($\\checkmark$). This variation provides the basis for **natural selection**, where **certain traits** offer a selective **advantage** in **specific environments** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, **individuals** with **advantageous traits** are **more likely** to **survive, reproduce**, and **pass on** their **favourable genes** ($\\checkmark$), driving the process of evolution and the development of new species.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is nearly universal across all living organisms **OR** there are only a few minor variations in the genetic code observed in certain species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in nearly all organisms the same triplet codons specify the same amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ universality of the genetic code implies a shared ancestral origin for all organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ used in phylogenetic analysis to reconstruct evolutionary relationships, providing strong evidence for universal common ancestry [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conservation of the genetic code helps maintain the integrity of proteins **OR** reduces risk of mutations that could disrupt essential cellular functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ despite evolutionary changes in species, the genetic code remains largely unchanged over time [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The conservation of the genetic code across all organisms suggests that all life shares a common ancestor, as the **same triplet codons** specify the **same amino acids** in **all species** ($\\checkmark$). This universality indicates that the **genetic code** has been **passed down** through **generations** with **little change** ($\\checkmark$), providing evidence for universal common ancestry."},{"ref":"BI2319","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2319\n\nWhich statement explains why DNA is evidence of common ancestry?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All living organisms have an identical DNA base sequence.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All living organisms contain DNA.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Closely related species contain the same alleles.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ More recently evolved species have more similar DNA.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the presence of DNA in all organisms reflects a universal lineage, providing evidence of common ancestry. All organisms use the same four nucleotides (adenine, thymine, cytosine, and guanine) and the same genetic code to translate DNA into proteins, which supports the idea of a shared evolutionary origin.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while all organisms share common sequences in their DNA, their base sequences are not identical. Differences in DNA sequences accumulate over time, and species with more recent common ancestors have more similar DNA.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because closely related species do not necessarily contain the same alleles. They share homologous genes and may have similar alleles, but their alleles often differ due to mutation and divergence after their lineages split. Also, this does not directly explain why DNA is evidence of common ancestry—rather, it describes a consequence of common ancestry.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because evolution does not progress in a linear ladder from “older” to “more recently evolved” species. All extant species are equally evolved. DNA similarity reflects how recently two species share a common ancestor, not how “recently evolved” they are. The wording is conceptually flawed, so it cannot explain why DNA provides evidence of common ancestry."},{"ref":"BI2645","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2645\n\nRefer to the diagram showing a section of a DNA strand.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/Picture1.png\" alt=\"Picture1\">\n\nWhich carbon on the deoxyribose sugar is bonded to the phosphate group of the adjacent nucleotide, in the sugar-phosphate bond?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 1'\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 2'\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 3'\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 4'","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/ANS.png\" alt=\"ANS\">\n\nThe psugar-phosphate bond forms between the 3' carbon of the sugar in one nucleotide (which has an –OH group), and the 5' phosphate group of the next nucleotide. So, the 3' carbon is directly involved in forming the bond that links nucleotides within a single strand of DNA or RNA, making **Choice C** correct.\n\nThe 1' carbon of the sugar is the attachment point for the nitrogenous base, not for the phosphate group; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nIn deoxyribose, the 2' carbon lacks an –OH group (it has just –H), distinguishing DNA from RNA. It is not involved in bonding to phosphate, so **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nThe 4' carbon connects to the 3' carbon and the 5' carbon within the sugar ring, but does not form bonds with the phosphate group; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2646","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2646\n\nWhich of the following best supports the idea that DNA is the universal genetic material in living organisms?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms have the same number of chromosomes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms translate proteins using ribosomes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms use DNA to pass on genetic information\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms reproduce sexually","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the use of DNA is universal across all living organisms.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chromosome number varies widely across different species.\n\n**Choice B** is true, but it is not a statement specific to DNA being the genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because not all organisms reproduce sexually."},{"ref":"BI2647","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2647\n\nWhich of the following is the correct pairing of a nucleotide component and its description?\n\n| | Component | Description |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sugar | Varies between RNA and DNA to form genetic code |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Base | Provides structure to the backbone |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Phosphate | Forms bonds with the base and sugar |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Base | Varies along nucleic acid to form genetic code|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the sequence of nitrogenous bases is what encodes genetic information in DNA and RNA. This is the only row with a fully correct and precise description.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, while the sugar differs between DNA (deoxyribose) and RNA (ribose), it does not form the genetic code. The code is carried in the base sequence, not the sugar.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because Bases are not part of the backbone (which is made of sugar–phosphate units). Bases project from the backbone and form the coding region, not the structure.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because phosphate forms bonds with sugars, not with bases. Bases attach to the sugar (at carbon 1'), not to phosphate."},{"ref":"BI2666","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2666\n\nWhat characteristic of DNA allows it to serve as a template for both DNA replication and mRNA synthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code in DNA is universal across all organisms\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding enables the correct alignment of deoxyribonucleotides and ribonucleotides in newly synthesised strands\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA’s double helix structure means one strand can be replicated at the same time as the other is being transcribed\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The strong sugar-phosphate backbone","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct. During both replication and transcription, nucleotides are aligned along the DNA template strand according to base-pairing rules. Hydrogen bonds form between the nitrogenous bases of the template DNA strand and the incoming deoxyribonucleotides (during replication) or ribonucleotides (in transcription).\\\nThe genetic code being universal means that the same codons code for the same amino acids in all organisms. However, this relates to protein synthesis, not to how DNA acts as a template for replication or transcription; **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because replication and transcription can’t occur on opposite strands simultaneously. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because while the sugar-phosphate backbone is a feature of both DNA and RNA strands that provides structural stability, it does not explain how DNA can interact with both DNA and RNA nucleotides."},{"ref":"BI2667","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2667\n\nWhich of the following molecules are classified as nucleic acids?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | ATP |\n    | II. | rRNA |\n    | III. | DNA |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false. ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a nucleotide, not a nucleic acid. It is a single molecule, not a long chain (polymer) of nucleotides, which is what defines nucleic acids like DNA and RNA. \\\n**Statement II** is true. Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) is a type of RNA, which is one of the two major types of nucleic acids (the other being DNA). It is a polymer of ribonucleotides, synthesised during transcription, and plays a role in protein synthesis. \\\n**Statement III** is true. DNA is a polymer of deoxyribonucleotides. It serves as the hereditary material in most organisms.\\\nNote that the correct answer includes the two molecules whose names end in ‘NA’, which stands for nucleic acid - DNA and RNA.\\\n**Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer as it only includes the two true **Statements II** and **III**.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2842","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2842\n\nWhich of the following features of DNA most directly underpins its virtually limitless capacity to encode genetic information?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The use of four chemically distinct nitrogenous bases arranged in long chains.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Semiconservative replication via complementary base pairing, which preserves sequence diversity.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The covalent sugar-phosphate backbone’s antiparallel orientation, which fixes strand directionality.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Coiling of DNA into chromosomes, which aids efficient storage.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the linear sequence of four different nucleotides (A, T, C and G) joined by sugar-phosphate bonds can form an almost infinite number of unique combinations, providing DNA with its enormous information-storage capacity. The human genome is about 3,000,000,000 nucleotides long, so there would be** $\\mathbf{4^{3,000,000,000}}$ theoretical combinations of nucleotides.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because complementary base pairing ensures faithful replication, but does not itself generate new sequences.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the antiparallel backbone ensures the strands are arranged in opposite directions, but does not contribute to the sequence diversity.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because coiling allows DNA to be compacted and stored efficiently within the nucleus, but it does not directly contribute to the *capacity* to encode genetic information. While necessary for packaging long DNA molecules, coiling does not influence the number of unique sequences that DNA can carry or its ability to store diverse instructions."},{"ref":"BI2851","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2851\n\nThe table shows the base composition of three DNA samples from different organisms:\n\n| Sample | %A | %T | %G | %C |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| X | 24 | 24 | 26 | 26 |\n| Y | 21 | 21 | 49 | 49 |\n| Z | 20 | 30 | 20 | 30 |\n\nWhich sample(s) most likely represent double-stranded DNA molecules?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ X only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ X and Y only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Y and Z only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ X, Y and Z","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTo identify double-stranded DNA, two rules must be satisfied:\n\n-   1\\. Base pairing rule: %A = %T and %G = %C\n\n-   2\\. Total base composition must add up to 100%\n\nSample **X** has equal proportions of adenine and thymine (24% each) and equal proportions of guanine and cytosine (26% each). The total adds up to 100%, which satisfies all criteria for double-stranded DNA. This sample is therefore most likely double-stranded.\n\nSample **Y** also has equal proportions of A and T (21%) and G and C (49%), but the total adds up to 140%, which is biologically impossible. This does not follow the base composition rule for double-stranded DNA.\n\nSample **Z** has mismatched base pairs: A $\\not=$ T and G $\\not=$ C. Although the total adds up to 100%, the base-pairing rules are not followed. This suggests that Z is likely single-stranded DNA.\n\n**Choice A** is therefore the correct answer because only sample **X** likely represents a double-stranded DNA molecule.\\\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more samples that are not double-stranded."},{"ref":"BI3204","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3204\n\nA single, linear RNA polymer contains 300 nucleotide monomers.\n\nHow many condensation reactions were required to form the polymer, and how many water molecules were released?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ 300 condensation reactions; 300 water molecules\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ 300 condensation reactions; 600 water molecules\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ 150 condensation reactions; 150 water molecules\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ 299 condensation reactions; 299 water molecules","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nJoining *n* monomers into a single linear polymer requires *n−1* bonds to be formed. Therefore, joining 300 nucleotides requires 299 condensation reactions. In the simplified condensation model, each reaction releases one water molecule, so 299 water molecules are released. **Choice D** is correct because 299 links are required, resulting in 299 condensation reactions and 299 water molecules.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the number of links formed is one fewer than the number of monomers.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because each condensation reaction releases one water molecule in this model, not two.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because there is no reason to divide the number of nucleotides by two."},{"ref":"BI3205","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3205\n\nDNA stores genetic information using a 4-symbol system, because each position in the sequence can contain one of four bases: A, T, C, or G.\n\nThe graph compares the number of possible sequences in a 2-symbol system and a 4-symbol system at sequence lengths of 2, 5 and 10 symbols. The graph uses a logarithmic scale on the y-axis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI3205.png\" alt=\"BI3205\">\n\nWhat does the graph suggest about the capacity of DNA for storing information?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ A two-symbol system stores more information because fewer symbols are used.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ The two systems have the same information capacity because both use a finite number of symbols.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Using four symbols rather than two symbols produces more possible sequences of the same length, giving DNA a greater capacity for storing information.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Using four symbols produces only four times as many possible sequences as using two symbols, regardless of sequence length.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor a sequence of length *n*, a 2-symbol system can produce $2^n$ possible sequences, whereas a 4-symbol system can produce $4^n$. At a sequence length of 10, these systems produce 1,024 and 1,048,576 possible sequences, respectively. Therefore, the 4-symbol DNA system produces 1,024 times as many sequences of this length, demonstrating how four bases arranged in sequences give DNA an enormous capacity for storing information. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because using fewer possible symbols at each position produces fewer, not more, possible sequences.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the number of symbols available at each position affects the number of possible sequences.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the difference increases with sequence length; the 4-symbol system produces 1,024 times as many possible sequences at a length of 10, not four times as many."},{"ref":"BI3206","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3206\n\nThe diagram below shows a replication fork with exposed bases on one template strand.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI3206.png\" alt=\"BI3206\">\n\nWhat is the sequence of the new DNA strand shown by the empty boxes, written from 5' to 3'?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–ATGCCT–3'\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–TACGGA–3'\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–UACGGA–3'\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–AUGCCU–3'","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe template strand runs from 3' to 5' and has the sequence 3'–TACGGA–5'. Complementary base pairing in DNA places A opposite T and G opposite C. Because the new strand is antiparallel to the template, its sequence is 5'–ATGCCT–3'. Therefore, **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it repeats the template sequence rather than providing its complementary sequence.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is not complementary to the template and contains uracil, which occurs in RNA rather than DNA.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it is a complementary RNA sequence, as shown by the presence of uracil; it is not the new DNA strand produced during DNA replication."},{"ref":"BI3207","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3207\n\nWhich of the following are differences between DNA and RNA?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | DNA contains uracil, whereas RNA contains thymine. |\n    | II. | DNA contains deoxyribose, whereas RNA contains ribose.  |\n    | III. | DNA is usually double-stranded, whereas RNA is usually single-stranded.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Statement I** is false because the bases have been reversed: DNA contains thymine, whereas RNA contains uracil.\n\n**Statement II** is true because DNA nucleotides contain deoxyribose, whereas RNA nucleotides contain ribose.\n\n**Statement III** is true because DNA is usually double-stranded, whereas RNA is usually single-stranded.\n\nTherefore, **Choice C** is correct because it includes both true **Statements II** and **III**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it omits the true **Statement III**.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI3208","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3208\n\nOne strand of a DNA molecule has the following base composition:\n\nA = 22%, T = 36%, G = 15%, C = 27%\n\nWhich principle can be used to determine the percentage of guanine in **the other strand** of the DNA molecule, and what is this percentage?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antiparallel orientation; 27%\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ Complementary base pairing; 27%\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Complementary base pairing; 15%\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antiparallel orientation; 15%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDuring complementary base pairing, guanine pairs with cytosine. Because cytosine makes up 27% of the original strand, guanine must make up 27% of the other strand. Therefore, **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it gives the correct percentage but identifies the wrong principle. Antiparallel orientation means that the two DNA strands run in opposite directions: one runs from 5′ to 3′, while the other runs from 3′ to 5′ alongside it. This describes the orientation of the sugar–phosphate backbones, not which bases pair together. Therefore, antiparallel orientation alone cannot be used to determine the percentage of guanine.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it identifies the correct principle but gives the wrong percentage. The 15% guanine in the original strand pairs with cytosine, so it determines the percentage of cytosine, not guanine, in the other strand. To determine the guanine percentage, we must use the original strand’s 27% cytosine.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because both the principle and the percentage are wrong. Antiparallel orientation describes strand direction rather than the relationship between base percentages. Complementary base pairing determines that guanine makes up 27% of the other strand, not 15%."},{"ref":"BI3209","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3209\n\nA gene encoding green fluorescent protein (GFP) from a jellyfish is inserted into a bacterium by gene transfer. The bacterium’s ribosomes successfully produce functional GFP.\n\nWhat does this demonstrate about the genetic code?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Bacteria and jellyfish have identical sets of genes, allowing the protein to be produced.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is conserved because the same codons generally specify the same amino acids in bacteria and jellyfish, supporting universal common ancestry.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ The bacterium evolved the ability to read jellyfish codons after exposure to the jellyfish gene.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code differs between bacteria and jellyfish, but their ribosomes translate codons into the same amino acids.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe bacterium’s protein-synthesis machinery can correctly translate the jellyfish gene because the same codons generally specify the same amino acids in both organisms. This conservation of the genetic code across different life forms supports the conclusion that they inherited the code from a universal common ancestor. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because bacteria and jellyfish share the same genetic code, but they do not have identical sets of genes.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the bacterium did not acquire a new ability after exposure to the gene. Its existing translation machinery could already interpret the codons.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because if the codons are translated into the same amino acids, the genetic code is conserved rather than different."},{"ref":"BI3210","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3210\n\nWhich of the following statements about nucleic acids are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The coding of genetic information depends on the sequence of bases.|\n    | II. | Adenine, cytosine and guanine occur in both DNA and RNA. |\n    | III. | Covalent bonds form between the phosphate group of one nucleotide and the pentose sugar of a neighbouring nucleotide.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Statement I** is true because genetic information is encoded by the sequence of nitrogenous bases.\n\n**Statement II** is true because adenine, cytosine and guanine occur in both DNA and RNA. DNA contains thymine as its fourth base, whereas RNA contains uracil.\n\n**Statement III** is true because covalent bonds between phosphate groups and pentose sugars in neighbouring nucleotides produce the continuous sugar–phosphate backbone of a nucleic acid strand.\n\nTherefore, **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **C** are all incorrect because they omit one or more of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI3211","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3211\n\nThe table compares characteristics of bonding in the sugar–phosphate backbone and between complementary bases in DNA.\n\n| Property | Sugar–phosphate backbone | Cross-strand base pairing |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Type of bond | **P** | **Q** |\n| Relative strength of individual bonds | Strong | **R** |\n| Role | **S** | Links complementary bases |\n\nWhat are P, Q, R and S?\n\n| | P | Q | R | S |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Covalent | Hydrogen | Weak | Forms the structural framework of each strand |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Covalent | Strong | Forms the structural framework of each strand |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Covalent | Hydrogen | Strong | Encodes genetic information |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Covalent | Weak | Encodes genetic information|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCovalent bonds link the sugars and phosphate groups of neighbouring nucleotides, forming a strong, continuous sugar-phosphate backbone. Therefore, P is covalent, and S is “forms a continuous structural backbone.”\n\nComplementary bases on opposite strands are linked by hydrogen bonds. Individual hydrogen bonds are relatively weak compared with covalent bonds, although many hydrogen bonds collectively help to stabilise the DNA molecule. Therefore, Q is hydrogen and R is weak. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it reverses the bond types: the backbone contains covalent bonds, while complementary bases are linked by hydrogen bonds.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because individual hydrogen bonds are relatively weak, and genetic information is encoded by the base sequence rather than by the sugar–phosphate backbone.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it reverses the bond types and incorrectly states that the backbone encodes genetic information."},{"ref":"BI3212","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3212\n\nA student states, “ATP molecules and nucleic acid monomers are nucleotides.”\n\nWhich response correctly evaluates this statement?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Correct because all nucleotides contain more than one phosphate group.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ Correct because both ATP and nucleic acid monomers consist of a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and one or more phosphate groups.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Incorrect because ATP contains a hexose sugar, whereas nucleic acid monomers contain pentose sugars.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Incorrect because ATP contains an amino acid rather than a nitrogenous base.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nATP consists of the nitrogenous base adenine, the pentose sugar ribose and three phosphate groups. The monomers from which DNA and RNA are formed also contain a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and phosphate. Therefore, ATP and nucleic acid monomers can both be classified as nucleotides. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because nucleotides do not all contain more than one phosphate group. A nucleotide consists of a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and one or more phosphate groups. ATP contains three phosphate groups, but the nucleotides incorporated into nucleic-acid polymers each contribute one phosphate group to the sugar–phosphate backbone.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ATP contains ribose, which is a pentose sugar rather than a hexose sugar.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because ATP contains the nitrogenous base adenine, not an amino acid."},{"ref":"BI3213","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3213\n\nThe diagram shows three domains of living organisms - bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, together with a separate group of RNA viruses.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI3213.png\" alt=\"BI3213\">\n\nWhat conclusion is supported by the diagram?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ RNA viruses should be classified as living organisms because they contain heritable genetic information.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA evolved from RNA, so all early living organisms used RNA as their genetic material.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ All living organisms use DNA as their genetic material, whereas some viruses use RNA and are not considered living.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of genetic material is sufficient for an entity to be considered living.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBacteria, archaea and eukaryotes represent the three domains of living organisms, and all use DNA as their genetic material. Some viruses, including influenza viruses and HIV, use RNA as their genetic material. However, viruses are not considered living organisms. Therefore, the occurrence of RNA genomes in some viruses does not contradict the statement that DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because containing heritable genetic information is not, by itself, sufficient for an entity to be considered living.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the diagram does not show that DNA evolved from RNA or that the earliest living organisms used RNA as their genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because viruses contain genetic material but are not considered living organisms."},{"ref":"BI3214","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3214\n\nA newly discovered entity  has RNA rather than DNA as its genetic material and can replicate only inside host cells.\n\nWhich statement best evaluates this discovery in relation to the principle that DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ It disproves the principle because any entity capable of replication is classified as a living organism.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ It disproves the principle because living organisms can use either DNA or RNA as their genetic material.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ It does not disprove the principle because the entity could be an RNA virus, which is not considered a living organism.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ It does not disprove the principle because RNA cannot function as genetic material.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe entity has characteristics consistent with an RNA virus: it contains RNA and depends on host cells for replication. Viruses are not considered living organisms, so the discovery does not contradict the principle that DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the ability to replicate inside host cells is not sufficient for an entity to be considered living.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the presence of an RNA virus does not prove that living organisms can use RNA as their genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because some viruses use RNA as their genetic material. These viruses do not contradict the principle because they are not considered living organisms—not because RNA cannot carry genetic information."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"BI0450","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0450\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe micrograph shows a part of a plant cell from the leaf of *Anemone sp.*\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/EM%20of%20chloroplast%20no%20labels.png\" alt=\"EM of chloroplast no labels\">\n[Source: and3k, & caper437. (2009, June 25). *File: chloroplast in leaf of Anemone sp Tem 85000x.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 13, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_12000x.png Copyright free]\n\n1. Label structures in the plant cell above:\n 1. using the letter R where starch can be found; [1 mark]\n\n 2. using the letter S where lipids can be found; [1 mark]\n\n 3. using the letter T where cellulose can be found. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure and functions of glycogen and starch. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled R pointing to a lighter oval [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/450ai%201.png\" alt=\"450ai 1\">\n\n 2. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled S pointing to the cell membrane **OR** membrane around chloroplast **OR** thylakoid membrane **OR** lipid droplet **OR** vacuolar membrane [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/450aii.png\" alt=\"450aii\">\n\n 3. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled T pointing to the cell wall\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/450aiii.png\" alt=\"450aiii\">\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (should include **AT LEAST ONE** for compare and **AT LEAST ONE** for contrast):\n\n *Compare:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are polysaccharides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are made up of $\\alpha$- glucose molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have 1,4 and 1,6 linkages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are energy storage molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** amylopectin in starch and glycogen are branched molecules [1 mark]\n\n *Contrast:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is more branched **IN CONTRAST TO** starch, **OR** amylose of starch is unbranched, amylopectin has few branched chains **WHEREAS** glycogen is more branched [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Starch is composed of two different polymers, amylose and amylopectin **WHEREAS** glycogen is composed of only one polymer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ starch is found in plant cells **WHEREAS** glycogen is found in animal cells [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nBoth starch and glycogen are **polysaccharides** ($\\checkmark$) made of **$\\alpha$- glucose molecules** ($\\checkmark$), which have **1,4 and 1,6 linkages** ($\\checkmark$). Starch is composed of **two different** polymers; **amylose** (unbranched and forms a helix) and **amylopectin** (branched), whereas **glycogen** is composed of only **one type of polymer** ($\\checkmark$). Both amylopectin, in starch, and glycogen are **branched** molecules ($\\checkmark$), but **glycogen is more branched than starch** ($\\checkmark$). Both are **energy storage** ($\\checkmark$)molecules but **starch** is found in **plant cells** whereas **glycogen** is found in **animal cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{For additional detail}}$: see 4:30 in Carbohydrates Key Concept Video"},{"ref":"BI0572","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0572\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. Outline the steps in calculating the magnification of a micrograph.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the steps taken to focus on a specimen using the 40x objective lens of a light microscope.      [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ measure (using a ruler) the scale bar on the drawing **OR** micrograph   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ note the stated length which the scale bar represents [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ convert the represented and real length or scale bar into the same units [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ M= I/A **OR** any correct example of the calculation     [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To calculate the magnification of a microphotograph, the first step is to measure **the scale bar using a ruler** ($\\checkmark$). The second step is **to note the length the scale bar represents** ($\\checkmark$). This gives you the actual size of the image. These two measurements should be converted to **the same units** ($\\checkmark$). The last step is to **divide the length measured by the ruler by the length represented by the scale bar** ($\\checkmark$). During this calculation, units cancel out.  Therefore, the **magnification does not have units**.\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  use the lowest power **OR** 4X objective lens first        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ place the specimen **OR** slide directly below the objective lens **OR** on the centre of the microscope stage   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  turn the coarse focus knob to focus on the specimen at 4X  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ adjust the turret **OR** change the objective lens so the medium power **OR** 10X objective lens is used     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ refocus on the specimen using a fine focus knob at 10X and 40X [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  adjust the turret **OR** change the objective lens, so the high power **OR** 40X objective lens is used [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ while fine focusing always move the lens away from the slide [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPlace the specimen **in the centre** of the microscope stage  ($\\checkmark$).  Use the **4x objective lens first**  ($\\checkmark$) and use the **coarse focus knob**  ($\\checkmark$) to focus on the specimen.  Adjust the position of the specimen so it is in the middle of the field of view and then **switch to the 10x**  ($\\checkmark$) objective lens and **refocus using the fine focus knob**, ($\\checkmark$) by moving lens **away from the specimen**  ($\\checkmark$) to prevent lens breakage.  Finally, switch to the **40x objective lens** ($\\checkmark$) and use the **fine focus knob** to refocus on the specimen."},{"ref":"BI0769","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0769\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nAn investigation was carried out into the effects of household detergents on aquatic habitats. Scientists tested the effect of different household detergents on the surface tension of water in order to better understand the impact they may have on organisms living on the surface.\n\nA paper clip was folded slightly on one side and rested on the surface of the water, maintained at a constant temperature and contained within a water tank. Drops of detergent solution were slowly added to the water tank until the paperclip sank to the bottom. The volume of detergent solution taken to sink the paperclip was recorded.\n\nDetergent solutions were made by adding 5 ml of detergent to 15 ml of water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI0769A.png\" alt=\"BI0769A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI0769B.png\" alt=\"BI0769B\">\n\n1. State the relationship between the volume of detergent solution added and the surface tension of water. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. For the **Easy Clean** detergent, calculate the mean volume of detergent needed to sink the paper clip. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Estimate the volume of **Easy Clean** detergent solution it would take to sink 5 paper clips. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State **one** variable, other than those given, which would need to be controlled in this experiment in order to achieve a valid result. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest **one** method that could be used to keep the water at a constant temperature. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Explain the importance of maintaining water at a constant temperature. [2 marks]\n\n 4. Identify **one** potential source of error in the methodology. [1 mark]\n\n4. Suggest why different numbers of drops of detergent solution were needed to sink the paperclip between each type of detergent. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface tension decreases as detergent solution volume increases [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ As the volume of detergent solution **increases** the **surface tension** of the water **decreases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. (units must be included in order to be awarded the mark)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 9.25ml [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI0769ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0769ANSA\">\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Mean = total/number of trials\n\n = (8 ml+10 ml+10 ml+9 ml) / 4\n\n = 37 ml / 4\n\n = **9.25ml** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. (units must be included in order to be awarded the mark)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 9.25ml [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If it takes 9.25 ml of detergent solution to reduce the surface tension enough to sink one paper clip, then the **same volume** would sink 5 paper clips ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of the water (in the water tank) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** mass **OR** brand **OR** material of paper clip **OR** way it is folded [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance of burette from water surface [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **size of the paper clip** ($\\checkmark$) in the experiment would need to be controlled.\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water bath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heating/cooling blocks [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A **water bath** ($\\checkmark$) could be used to maintain a constant temperature.\n\n3.\n 3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the cohesive properties of water create surface tension **OR** surface tension is due to hydrogen bonding between water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cohesive forces **OR** hydrogen bonding decrease with increasing temperature **BECAUSE** there is more kinetic energy so molecules move more and interact less **OR** less attractive forces form between them, **OR** cohesive forces **OR** hydrogen bonding increase with decreasing temperature **BECAUSE** there is less kinetic energy so molecules move less and interact more **OR** more attractive forces form between them [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It’s important to maintain a constant temperature in this experiment because surface tension is due to **cohesion** ($\\checkmark$) between water molecules which is a result of hydrogen bonding. High temperatures can break hydrogen bonds and **reduce the cohesive properties** ($\\checkmark$) of water which in turn reduces surface tension.\n\n3.\n 4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface tension broken by the droplets of detergent solution **OR** ripples created by droplets of detergent [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clips folded incorrectly **OR** differently (if different paper clips used for each trial) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clip has residual detergent between trials (if same paper clip is used for each trial) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clips are different materials **OR** brands **OR** texture (if different paper clips are used for each trial) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A source of error could be that the surface tension of the water is broken by the **droplets of detergent** ($\\checkmark$) which create ripples. This could result in the paperclip sinking.\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ detergents are made using different chemicals **OR** detergents have different starting concentrations **OR** different concentrations of chemical ingredients [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ some chemicals **OR** detergents affect the cohesion between water molecules more **AND** therefore reduce surface tension more [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ There may be a difference between results because each detergent is a different brand which may be made using **different chemicals** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the chemicals found in some detergents may have a **greater effect on cohesion** between water molecules which means they could reduce the **surface tension** more ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1049","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1049\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline one way unicellular organisms can carry out each of nutrition, movement and excretion. [3 marks]\n\n2. Draw a single mitochondrion, and annotate it to outline the role of four labelled structures. [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of E. coli and a palisade mesophyll cell. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition by diffusion through cell membrane **OR** engulfment **OR** phagocytosis **OR** endocytosis **OR** photosynthesis **OR** digestion by enzymes in vacuoles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement by cilia **OR** flagella **OR** pseudopodia [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion by simple diffusion **OR** active transport **OR** exocytosis **OR** contractile vacuoles **OR** vacuoles storing **OR** isolating waste before removal [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Unicellular organisms carry out the processes of life in varying ways. They can carry out **nutrition** by **engulfing** the nutrients ($\\checkmark$) from their surroundings, they can **move** using **flagella** ($\\checkmark$), and they can **remove wastes** from the cell by **simple diffusion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Diagram must include four of the following labels and annotations:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ outer membrane protective barrier **OR** contains transport proteins that allow specific molecules to enter the mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane **OR** cristae increase surface area for reactions **OR** contains proteins essential for reactions **OR** ATP production [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA encodes genes essential for mitochondrial functioning [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes for synthesis of proteins required by mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matrix contains enzymes for chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intermembrane space - allows build-up of protons **OR** ion gradient for ATP synthesis **OR** chemiosmosis **OR** contains enzymes for chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clear drawing with no sketchy lines and of suitable size [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI1049.png\" alt=\"BI1049\">\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (*must include at least **two** similarity and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Similarities***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have cell **OR** plasma membranes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have a cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells contain ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* cell wall is made up of peptidoglycan **WHEREAS** palisade mesophyll cell walls are made of cellulose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have a compartmentalised structure **WHEREAS** *E.coli* do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have 80S ribosomes **WHEREAS** *E.coli* have 70S [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have nuclei **WHEREAS** *E.coli* have a nucleoid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* can have plasmids **OR** circular DNA **WHEREAS** palisade mesophyll cells have linear DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* have external structures such as flagella **OR** pili **BUT** palisade mesophyll cells do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have membrane bound organelles such as golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum, chloroplasts, mitochondria **WHEREAS** *E.coli* do not [1 mark]\n [8 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli* both have **cell membranes** ($\\checkmark$) and **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). They also both can have **cell walls** ($\\checkmark$), however the cell wall in *E.coli* is composed of **peptidoglycan**, whereas the cell wall in palisade mesophyll cells is composed of **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$). Palisade mesophyll cells are full of **membrane bound** organelles such as the golgi apparatus, which gives them a **compartmentalised** structure, whereas *E.coli* **do not** have a compartmentalised structure or membrane bound organelles ($\\checkmark$). Both palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli* have **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$), but *E.coli* have **70S** ribosomes and palisade mesophyll cells have **80S** ribosomes ($\\checkmark$). DNA is also stored differently in palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli*. Palisade mesophyll cells store their DNA inside the **nucleus** and it has a **linear** structure, whereas *E.coli* have DNA floating freely in the cytoplasm as a **nucleoid** ($\\checkmark$) and can sometimes contain extra DNA in the form of a **plasmid** ($\\checkmark$). E.coli can also have external structures such as **pili** or flagella ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2798","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2798\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Electron microscopy provides images of cells with significantly higher resolution when compared to light microscopy. Evaluate the implications and limitations of relying primarily on electron micrographs to understand cell function.            [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how advances in microscopy have contributed to our understanding of cell structure and function.                                [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the evidence for and against the idea that prokaryotic cells are “simple” compared to eukaryotic cells.                             [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following, *must include at least **one** implication and **one** limitation*\n\n    ***Implications***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fine detail resolution has led to a more detailed understanding of structure-function relationships    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ discovery of new organelles and structures    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Limitations***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ requires complex sample preparation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample preparation can leave artefacts    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ samples must be fixed, dead, meaning no dynamic cellular processes can be observed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pigmentation or colour is not detected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maximum understanding comes from combining information from both light and electron microscopy    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Electron microscopy gives us very **detailed images**, which let us see how the tiny parts of a cell are **structured to fulfil** their **functions** ($\\checkmark$). It has also led to the discovery of **new things inside** cells ($\\checkmark$). Unfortunately, to **prepare specimens** for electron microscopy, they must be treated in such a way that they are **killed**, and so we **cannot observe processes** happening in real time ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light microscopy allowed for the initial visualisation of cells and basic structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ staining techniques have enhanced the visibility of cell components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fluorescence microscopy enables visualisation and localisation of specific molecules     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ live-imaging techniques allow dynamic processes to be observed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopy provides significantly higher resolution than light microscopy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transmission electron microscopy reveals details of the internal structure of organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scanning electron microscopy provides 3-D images of cell surfaces **OR** cell shape    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Light microscopy first allowed us to see **cells** and their **basic** parts ($\\checkmark$). Later, **staining** made it easier to see these parts more **clearly** ($\\checkmark$). **Fluorescence** microscopy has allowed scientists to visualise and pinpoint the **locations** of specific molecules within cells ($\\checkmark$).  **Electron** microscopy provides **far greater resolution** than light microscopy ($\\checkmark$), making it possible to see **internal structures** like mitochondria and membranes in **fine detail** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    ***In favour***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles **OR** have less compartmentalisation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have a single, circular chromosome; eukaryotic cells have multiple linear chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are generally smaller        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells reproduce by binary fission, which is less complex than mitosis/meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Against***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells efficiently carry out all life processes within a single cell, demonstrating functional sophistication        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells exhibit diverse and complex metabolic pathways        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells exhibit unique metabolic pathways, ie chemosynthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells can engage in cell signaling        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have highly specialised adaptations (ie flagellae)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have a high surface area to volume ratio, increasing the exchange rate with the surroundings         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are structurally simpler but functionally complex        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ evaluation of the application of the word “simple”        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Prokaryotes are generally **smaller** ($\\checkmark$) than most eukaryotic cells, and they reproduce by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$) instead of mitosis or meiosis. Prokaryotic cells are often called more simple than eukaryotic cells, but it is important to **distinguish between structure and function** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells may appear simple, but they still carry out all of the **functions of life** ($\\checkmark$) and have some that are unique to prokaryotes. Although prokaryotes **do not have membrane-bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$), they still have **complex metabolic** pathways like cellular respiration and even pathways eukaryotes do not have, like **chemosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotes can communicate with each other via **cell signalling** pathways like quorum sensing ($\\checkmark$) and also have highly **specialised structures** such as flagellae for motility ($\\checkmark$). The term “simple” is an oversimplification of a superficial observation."}]},{"name":"Water","questions":[{"ref":"BI0177","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0177\n\nWhich of the following statements about the dipolarity of the water molecule are true?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen attracts the shared electrons stronger than hydrogen.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Hydrogen atoms are partially negative.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The shared electrons spend more time closer to oxygen.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  II and III\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct. Oxygen has a higher electronegativity compared to hydrogen. This means that oxygen will attract shared electrons more strongly.\n\n**Statement II** is false. Since the shared electrons between oxygen and hydrogen spend more time near oxygen,  the hydrogen in the water molecule becomes partially positive.\n\n**Statement III** is true because oxygen attracts the shared electrons more strongly resulting in the shared electrons spending more time closer to the oxygen atom."},{"ref":"BI0178","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0178\n\nWhat intermolecular bond is responsible for water's cohesive, adhesive and thermal properties?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Covalent bond\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bond\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ionic bond\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metallic bond","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because water is a polar molecule (dipolar). Hydrogen bonds form due to the attraction between the partially positive (δ:sup[+]) hydrogen atom of one molecule and the partially negative (δ:sup[-]) oxygen atom of another. These intermolecular forces are responsible for:\n\n- Cohesion: Water molecules sticking to each other.\n- Adhesion: Water molecules sticking to other polar/charged surfaces.\n- Thermal Properties: High specific heat capacity and latent heat of vaporisation due to the energy required to break these bonds.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because covalent bonds are intramolecular; they hold the hydrogen and oxygen atoms together within a single water molecule, but do not hold different water molecules to one another.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because water is a molecular compound; it does not contain metals (metallic bonding) nor does it involve the complete transfer of electrons between ions (ionic bonding)."},{"ref":"BI0181","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0181\n\nWhat substances are attracted to water and form intermolecular bonds with water molecules?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Insoluble substances\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophobic substances\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophilic substances\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Non-polar substances","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHydrophilic (**Choice C**) substances dissolve in water through the formation of hydrogen bonds with water. \\\nNon-polar (**Choice D**) substances are not attracted to water molecules and do not form bonds with them. These substances are hydrophobic (**Choice B**), making them insoluble (**Choice A**) in water."},{"ref":"BI0182","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0182\n\nWhat property of water helps living organisms maintain their internal body temperature?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Thermal properties\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cohesive properties\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesive properties\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solvent properties","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater's thermal properties (**Choice A**) include a high heat of vaporisation which results in the absorption of a large amount of heat by water before changing its physical state. This means that changing the temperature of the water is slow, and this helps to maintain the internal temperature of organisms between set limits. \\\nWater has cohesive properties (**Choice B**) which means it sticks to other water molecules. \\\nWater has adhesive properties (**Choice C**) which means it adheres to charged surfaces. \\\nWater is an excellent solvent (**Choice D**) which means it dissolves a wide array of polar and ionic substances."},{"ref":"BI0183","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0183\n\nWhich of the following is not soluble in water?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Amino Acids\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium Chloride","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCholesterol (**Choice C**) is a non-polar hydrophobic molecule. Therefore, it is insoluble in water. \\\nGlucose (**Choice A**), amino acids (**Choice B**), and sodium chloride (**Choice D**) are soluble in water and are hydrophilic."},{"ref":"BI0186","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0186\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n1. Outline how hydrogen bonds form between water molecules. [2 marks]\n\n2. State one of the ways that hydrophilic substances interact with water. [1 mark]\n\n3.  State one of the ways that non-polar molecules interact with water.[1 mark]\n\n4. Distinguish between cohesion and adhesion.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds are formed between the hydrogen atom of one water molecule and the oxygen atom of another water molecule [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen atoms in water molecules are partially positive [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen atoms in water molecules are partially negative [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds are a polar attraction and electrons are not shared [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    In water molecules, hydrogen bonds are formed between the **partially positive** ($\\checkmark$) hydrogens of one water molecule and the **partially negative** ($\\checkmark$) oxygens of other water molecules.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  they are attracted to and form intermolecular bonds with water            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they form hydrogen bonds                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  they dissolve in water **OR** are soluble in water                    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Hydrophilic substances are **attracted to water**($\\checkmark$)  and form intermolecular bonds. They are said to be **soluble** ($\\checkmark$) in water.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  do not freely associate with water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  hydrophobic are repelled by water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  do not dissolve in water OR insoluble in water [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Non-polar molecules are **hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$) and **do not dissolve** ($\\checkmark$)  in water.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in cohesion, water molecules stick to each other **WHILE** in adhesion, water molecules adhere to the polar, **OR** charged, surfaces of other molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cohesion explains water’s surface tension properties  **WHILE** adhesion explains capillary action    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in plants, adhesion allows water to travel by capillary forces  **WHILE** cohesion is responsible for maintaining an uninterrupted water column [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWater molecules are polar, with one end being partially positive and the other end being partially negative. This polarity allows water to form hydrogen bonds with other molecules. When **hydrogen bonds form between two water molecules, this is cohesion**($\\checkmark$), whereas hydrogen bonds forming between water and other polar molecules is **adhesion**. An example of adhesion and cohesion working together would be in plants, where water travels up xylem vessels against gravity. This is possible because **cohesion maintains an uninterrupted water column**($\\checkmark$), and the adhesion of water to the cellulose walls of xylem vessels drives the water upwards through **capillary action**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0187","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0187\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nBelow is a picture of a water strider ($\\textit{Gerris sp.}$).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI0187i_2.2.png\" alt=\"BI0187i_2.2\">\n[Source: Schnobby. (2010). *Water Strider*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 27, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Water_strider_in_a_pond.jpg. Copyright by CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Explain why the water strider ($\\textit{Gerris sp.}$) does not sink. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe, using an example, the importance of water as a solvent in living organisms. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cohesion, **OR** cohesive properties, of water is responsible [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water molecules bind to other water molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water molecules form intermolecular hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cohesion results in high surface tension of water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  legs of the insect are hydrophobic [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Cohesion**($\\checkmark$) is the water property that is responsible for this phenomenon. Water **molecules bind with other water molecules** ($\\checkmark$) through **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$)to create **high surface tension**($\\checkmark$). The legs of the water strider are covered in a secreted wax, making them **hydrophobic**($\\checkmark$), which allows the water strider to move on top of the water.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following (maximum of 2 marks if no example is provided):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  metabolic reactions can only happen in solutions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water dissolves hydrophilic substances needed for life processes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  substances can be transported through the water-based solutions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  blood plasma dissolves and transports sodium chloride **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids **OR** any other valid example in animals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water dissolves and transports nutrients **OR** minerals needed by plants **OR** any other valid example in plants [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Water dissolves hydrophilic substances**($\\checkmark$)\nrequired for **metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$) which can only occur in solution.  Dissolved substances can react with each other and also be **transported**($\\checkmark$)to other parts of an organism. For example, water dissolves and transports glucose in the **blood plasma**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0668","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0668\n\nThe cohesive and adhesive properties of water allow xylem vessels to transport water inside plants.\n\nWhich of the following are correct descriptions of how these properties are related to water transport in plants?\n\n| | Cohesive | Adhesive |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. | Polar water molecules are attracted to the hydrophilic cell walls in xylem.\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Water molecules are polar, so are able to dissolve ions | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form oxygen bonds. | Water molecules are polar, so are able to dissolve ions |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar water molecules are attracted to the hydrophilic cell walls in xylem. | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is cohesive because it is a polar molecule. The oxygen atom in one water molecule forms a hydrogen bond with the hydrogen atom of another water molecule. In xylem vessels water is attracted to the hydrophilic regions of the cell wall and hydrogen bonds are formed.  This an accurate description of the adhesive properties of water in the context of water transport in plants (**Choice A**). These statements are reversed in **Choice D**.\n\nIons dissolve in water, but this is not related to the cohesive (**Choice B**) or adhesive (**Choice C**) properties of water."},{"ref":"BI0834","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0834\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe image shows a cross-section of the vascular tissue of a woody dicot stem, showing the annual rings of phloem and xylem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI0834.jpg\" alt=\"BI0834\">\n[Source: Adapted from: Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. (2014, February 10). File:Woody Dicot stem primary xylem in late one year  - wikimedia. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 07, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woody_Dicot_Stem_Ray_System_in_Two_Year_Tilia_%2834997659262%29.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n1. Explain how the properties of water allow it to be transported in the xylem.  [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe a physical property of water, other than those that are important for the movement of water through the plant, that makes water essential for the survival of plants.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline why plants cannot use water to transport lipids.    [1 mark]\n\n4. If the temperature drops to -1:sup[o]C for a few hours suggest a reason why the water in xylem may not freeze.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is cohesive **OR** hydrogen bonding between water molecules **OR** strong intermolecular forces so can form a continuous column of water **OR** counteract gravity     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is adhesive **OR** water molecules can adhere to other polar surfaces **OR** water adheres to walls of xylem vessels **OR** to lignin and cellulose of xylem vessels, so prevent collapse of water column **OR** separation from xylem vessel walls     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ capillary action occurs as water moves upwards against gravity in the narrow xylem vessels         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the hydrogen bonds between water molecules mean that as water evaporates  from the leaf  negative pressure **OR** tension occurs, so water moves upwards against gravity         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low viscosity reduces resistance to flow within the xylem         [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nWater can be transported efficiently through the xylem as the water molecules adhere to the inside of the xylem vessels ($\\checkmark$) and cohere to each other, therefore forming a continual column of water ($\\checkmark$). As the water evaporates from the leaves, the hydrogen bonds between the molecules mean that a negative pressure occurs at the top of this column of water, enabling the water to move upwards, against gravity ($\\checkmark$).  This is the cohesion-tension theory.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is a solvent so can dissolve nutrients which can be used by the plant **OR** provides a medium for various biochemical reactions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity prevents extreme temperature fluctuations that could damage plant tissues **OR** maintain optimal enzyme activity and metabolic processes in plants     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transparency allows light to penetrate and reach submerged plants for photosynthesis         [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water is known as a universal solvent, so nutrients can be dissolved in it and be transported to where they are needed ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lipids are hydrophobic **OR** non-polar, so cannot dissolve in water     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water cannot be used to transport lipids in plants as they are hydrophobic  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high specific heat capacity, **OR** it has to lose a lot of energy to freeze     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ due to the high solute concentration of the contents of the xylem         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in pressure in the xylem raises the freezing point of water             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity means water can release stored heat slowly, maintaining a stable temperature above freezing point                 [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water in the xylem may not freeze when the temperature drops below -1:sup[o]C, as it has a high specific heat capacity  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0835","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0835\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe image shows a ringed seal (*Pusa hispida*) that lives in the Arctic Sea.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI0835.png\" alt=\"BI0835\">\n[Source: Adapted from: Kingfisher. (2021, May 29). Pusa hispida. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved from July 07, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Pusa_hispida. Copyright free]\n\n1. Distinguish between the physical properties of air and water that allow the seal to mostly live in the sea.      [2 marks]\n\n2. Ringed seals glide into the water head first from ice shelves, rather like humans diving into a pool head first. Explain the advantage of entering the water in this way rather than ‘belly-flopping’ into the water.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high specific heat capacity **COMPARED TO** air, so its temperature remains relatively stable compared to air **OR** so water remains relatively warmer during the winter     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a higher density **COMPARED TO** air, and this supports the seal’s body weight as the water exerts buoyant forces on the seal     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a higher viscosity **COMPARED TO** air, providing the seal with more resistance to movement **OR** motion, which provides the seal with thrust and propulsion for swimming     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The ringed seal is mostly able to live in water rather than air as water has a **high specific heat capacity** compared to air, so its **temperature remains relatively stable** so that the seal does not get too cold ($\\checkmark$). Water has a **higher density** compared to air, and this **supports** the seal’s **body weight** as the water exerts **buoyant forces** on the seal ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high surface tension due to hydrogen bonds **THEREFORE**, ‘belly-flopping’ will cause pain due to the hydrogen bonds breaking forcefully **OR** gliding/diving causes hydrogen bonds between water molecules to be broken more gently **OR** entering head first reduces the body surface area that initially comes into contact with the water **THEREFORE** reducing water resistance         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gliding in is more energy-efficient for the seal as they use momentum generated from sliding on ice      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gliding is more stealthy OR quiet, which allows the seal to avoid predators     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gliding is more controlled, so the seal is at less risk of injury     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Gliding in head first’ is advantageous as water has a **high surface tension** due to the high number of hydrogen bonds between water molecules, therefore,  ‘belly-flopping’ may cause **pain** from **breaking the hydrogen bonds** forcefully ($\\checkmark$). Gliding is more **stealthy** so the seal has more chance of catching prey or **escaping predators** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0836","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0836\n\nThe black-throated loon (*Gavia arctica*) is a bird that lives near large bodies of water in cold climates.  Which of the following is a correct description of how the properties of water may make winter temperatures more moderate for the black-throated loon?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water has a relatively high specific heat capacity which buffers the environmental temperature.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ice floats on water so insulates the liquid water beneath the ice.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water has a low specific heat capacity which warms the surrounding air.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water is dense which enables the black-throated loon to float.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because water’s ability to absorb and store a significant amount of heat without undergoing large temperature changes provides thermal cushioning.  In summer water absorbs heat energy and then in winter this heat energy is lost to the surrounding air helping to create milder microclimates. This enables the black-throated loon to inhabit areas where temperature might otherwise be too extreme.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it does not explain how winter air temperatures are made more moderate, only that ice insulates water that is below it which is not where the black-throated loon lives.  **Choice C** is incorrect because water does not have a low specific heat capacity.  **Choice D** is incorrect because, although it is a true statement, it does not explain how winter temperatures are made more moderate."},{"ref":"BI1010","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1010\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a real-life example of adhesion?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Surface tension on the surface of a lake\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dew drops hanging off the tip of a blade of grass\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water coating the surface of the alveoli\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water sticking to cellulose in cell walls","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSurface tension on the surface of the lake, describes the property of cohesion, the ability of water molecules to form hydrogen bonds with other water molecules. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nAdhesion is the ability of water to form hydrogen bonds with other polar molecules. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they are all examples of water is sticking to surfaces that are not water molecules, demonstrating adhesion."},{"ref":"BI1011","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1011\n\nWhich is the correctly annotated diagram of a molecule of water?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1011iii.png\" alt=\"BI1011iii\">\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA water molecule consists of 2 hydrogens and one oxygen connected by single covalent bonds. Water molecules are polar, with a slight negative charge on the oxygen (δ-) and a slight positive charge on the hydrogens (δ+) due to unequal sharing of electrons. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as it consists of one hydrogen bonded to two oxygens. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as water molecules are not linear due to the sharing of electrons. **Choice D**  is incorrect as oxygen is not slightly positive (δ+) and hydrogen is not slightly negative (δ-)."},{"ref":"BI1012","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1012\n\nWhich term to describe a property of water is matched to its correct definition?\n\n| | Term | Definition |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Exerts buoyant forces | Much energy is required to turn water into a vapour |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High specific heat capacity | Much energy is required to raise the temperature of 1kg water by 1 °C |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High boiling point | Turns from ice into steam at 100 °C |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low viscosity | Easily allows objects to float on the surface |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater has a high specific heat capacity, meaning it can absorb or lose a lot of heat with only a small temperature change. This property helps maintain stable temperatures in organisms and aquatic environments. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nWater exerts buoyant forces on objects, which means that it allows them to float easily on its surface. Objects that are less dense than the water they displace will experience an upward force greater than their weight, causing them to float.  **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nA high boiling point means that liquid water turns into steam at 100°C; **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nLow viscosity means that water flows easily; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1014","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1014\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) about the role of water as a solvent is correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Salt can dissolve in water because it is hydrophilic|\n    | II. | Lipids cannot dissolve in water because they are non-polar |\n    | III. | Cellulose can dissolve in water, as it is able to form hydrogen bonds |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSalt, an ionic compound, dissolves readily in water because water is a polar solvent and can interact with the charged ions in salt through electrostatic attractions. Substances that have an affinity for water and can form interactions with water molecules are referred to as hydrophilic (**Statement I**). Lipids are non-polar molecules, and water is a polar solvent. Polar and non-polar substances do not readily mix because water molecules are attracted to each other through hydrogen bonding and other dipole-dipole interactions. Lipids are hydrophobic, meaning they repel water and do not dissolve well in it (**Statement II**). **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\nAlthough cellulose can form hydrogen bonds, it is insoluble (**Statement III**) as it is too large to dissolve in water; **Choices B**  and  **D**  are therefore incorrect as they include this statement. **Choice A** is incorrect as it does not include **statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1015","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1015\n\nWhich of the following statements explains how water has facilitated the evolution of life on Earth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water acts as a catalyst for chemical reactions\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water acts as a solvent allowing chemical reactions to occur faster within it\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water acts in hydrolysis reactions allowing for the synthesis of large biological molecules\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water has a high latent heat of vaporisation so is able to convert between states easily","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater's unique ability to act as a solvent is crucial for life's evolution. Many biochemical reactions that are fundamental to life occur within aqueous environments. Water's solvent properties facilitate the dissolution and interaction of molecules, enabling chemical reactions to occur more rapidly and efficiently. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\nWater acts as a reaction medium for many chemical reactions, but does not catalyse the chemical reactions that facilitate evolution, **Choice A** is incorrect. **Choice C**  is incorrect as hydrolysis reactions break down larger molecules into smaller ones. **Choice D**  is incorrect as water’s high latent heat of vaporisation means that it requires a lot of energy to convert liquid water into a gas."},{"ref":"BI1046","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1046\n\nThe micrograph below shows the longitudinal cross-section of a stem from a plant in the *Pinus* genus.  Xylem vessels are clearly visible.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1046.png\" alt=\"BI1046\">\nMagnification: 100X\n\n[Source: From Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. (2014). *Radial section: Pinus stem magnification: 100x iron-alum hematoxylin and safranin stain*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 29, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gymnosperm_Stem_Soft_Wood_in_Pinus_(36087426450).jpg.\n Copyright by CC BY CC0 1.0\n]\n\nThe xylem is composed of hollow, tube-like tissues that transport water through capillary action. What property of water explains capillary action?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Melting point\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesive properties\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific heat capacity\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Buoyant forces","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAdhesive properties (**Choice B**) refer to water’s ability to stick to charged surfaces or polar molecules. This, together with cohesion, allows water to move against gravity up the stem through capillary action.\n\nWater's melting point (**Choice A**) is the temperature at which it changes its state from solid to liquid.\n\nThe specific heat capacity (**Choice C**) of water pertains to the amount of heat energy needed to raise the temperature of a unit mass (usually one gram) of water by 1 °C.\n\nThe buoyant force of water (**Choice D**) is the upward force on an object submerged or partially immersed in water, equal to the weight of the displaced water."},{"ref":"BI1224","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1224\n\nThe image shows the vascular tissue in the leaves of a corn plant (*Zea mays*). What property of water is not demonstrated in the vascular tissue of the plant?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1224.png\" alt=\"BI1224\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College  Bioscience Image Library. (2017, October 3). *Angiosperm morphology: The closed and collateral vascular bundle in Zea*. Flickr. Retrieved September 11, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/146824358@N03/37215761790. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cohesion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Universal solvent\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ High thermal conductivity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe vascular tissue of a plant contains the xylem, which transports water from the roots to the leaves of the plant and the phloem, which transports soluble organic compounds that are made during photosynthesis. Although water has a high thermal conductivity, which means that it can transfer heat easily, this is not demonstrated by the movement of water in the xylem; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nCohesion is the ability of water molecules to stick to each other via their hydrogen bonds, this allows a continuous column of water to be transferred up the xylem; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Adhesion is the ability of water to stick to surfaces, this allows water to stick to the inner surface of the xylem, which helps to maintain the integrity of the continuous water column; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Water is a universal solvent, which means that many nutrients can be dissolved in it and be transported via the xylem and phloem to where they are needed in the plant; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1424","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1424\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe photograph below is of the sea oat (*Unola paniculata*). This plant is found on sand dunes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1424.png\" alt=\"BI1424\">\n[Source: Adapted from Daniel Di Palma (2 Feb 2017) *Miami Beach - Sand Dune Flora - Sea Oats in South Beach*. Retrieved 31 Jan 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=59792482. Copyright CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n1. Explain how the sea oat (*Unola paniculata*) is adapted to survive in its environment.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the importance of the solvent properties of water for transport in plants and humans.            [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wide shallow root system that helps anchor them securely in shifting sands **OR**  to allow rapid absorption of water from sand **OR** to stabilise sand reducing rate of erosion        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ salt tolerance to survive exposure to salt spray from the sea    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow leaves that reduce water loss through transpiration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flexible **OR** bendy stems allow sea oats to sway with the wind without breaking    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long bristle-like structures on seeds that catch the wind to aid seed dispersal    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some sea oat populations have a symbiotic relationship with nitrogen-fixing bacteria in their root nodules enabling them to survive in nutrient-poor beach soils    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nodes **OR** rhizomes allow regeneration after a fire **OR** to reproduce asexually to survive being covered by sand    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **wide, shallow root** system of sea oats allows them to **anchor themselves** in the shifting sand ($\\checkmark$) of the dunes and to **absorb water rapidly** ($\\checkmark$). Their **narrow leaves reduce water loss** through **transpiration** ($\\checkmark$) and their **flexible stems** allow them to survive the **sea winds without breaking** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following (*need **one** example for plants and **one** example for humans to gain maximum marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules are polar **OR** have an uneven distribution of charge so attract other polar molecules **OR** ions through hydrogen bonding **OR** water dissolves hydrophilic substances                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in cellular transport water allows movement of ions **OR** hydrophilic molecules **OR** polar molecules around the cell            [1 mark]\n\n    ***Plants*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem **OR** transpiration stream carries minerals **OR** minerals **OR** potassium **OR** calcium **OR** magnesium from roots [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem carries nutrients **OR** sucrose **OR** carbohydrates **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Humans*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood plasma dissolves and transports sodium chloride **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids **OR** wastes **OR** hormones **OR** oxygen **OR** carbon dioxide                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen dissolution in aqueous lining of lungs allows uptake of oxygen into blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waste products are filtered out at the kidney **OR** wastes are transported in glomerular filtrate **OR** water allows the transport of wastes through the nephron to the bladder for excretion    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Because **water molecules** are **polar, hydrophilic substances dissolve** in **aqueous solutions** ($\\checkmark$). In **plants**, water transports **minerals** up **from** the **roots**, where they have been absorbed from the soil, to where they are needed in the plant ($\\checkmark$).  In humans, water acts as the medium for **blood plasma, transporting** essential **nutrients** and **oxygen** around the body ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1537","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1537\n\nWhich of the following thermal properties of water is **incorrectly** matched?\n\n| | Property | Water |\n\n| --- | :-- | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Melting Point | 0 $\\degree$C |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Specific Heat Capacity | 2.2 J $\\text{g}^{-1}$ $\\degree \\text{C}^{-1}$ |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Latent Heat of Vaporisation | 2260 J $\\text{g}^{-1}$ |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Boiling Point | 100 $\\degree$C |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\n**Choice B** is incorrectly matched. The specific heat capacity of water is 4.2 J $\\text{g}^{-1} \\degree \\text{C}^{-1}$, not 2.2  J $\\text{g}^{-1} \\degree \\text{C}^{-1}$.\n\n**Choice A**, **C**, and **D** are correctly matched."},{"ref":"BI1758","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1758\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Outline how the properties of water contributed to the origin of life on Earth. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Draw **two** water molecules, labelling the partial charges and the bond between them.$^*$  [3 marks]\n\n3.  Explain how the solubility of different types of substances in water affects their transport in the blood, giving named examples. [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1.  any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polarity of water molecules enables formation of hydrogen bonds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding assisted formation of stable molecules **OR** assembly of complex structures **OR** lipid bilayer **OR** other suitable named structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solvent properties facilitated dissolution **OR** interaction of organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enabled formation of more complex structures **OR** protocells **OR** precursors to first living cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity prevented rapid temperature changes, enabling chemical reactions to occur    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provided medium for enzymes **OR** chemical reactions **OR** synthesis of biological molecules        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Water’s **polarity** enables the formation of **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$), which are crucial for the **stability** and **assembly** of **complex structures** such as **DNA, proteins** and **lipid bilayers** ($\\checkmark$). As a **universal solvent**, water facilitated the **dissolution** and **interaction** of **organic molecules** ($\\checkmark$), leading to the formation of **protocells** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, water’s **high specific heat capacity** provided a **stable environment, preventing** rapid **temperature changes** and allowing essential **enzymatic** controlled **reactions** to occur ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **three** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two water molecules each made up of one oxygen atom attached to two hydrogen atoms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ slight negative charge on oxygen atom and slight positive charge on both hydrogen atoms on at least one of the water molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bond drawn as dashed lines and labelled    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1758b.png\" alt=\"BI1758b\">\n\n3.  any **eight** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport of molecules in the blood depends on their solubility in water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport occurs in blood plasma and red blood cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic **OR** water-soluble substances travel freely in solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ionic compounds **OR** ions transported in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NaCl dissociates into Na+ and Cl- ions and carried in plasma **OR** NaCl is transported as free ions in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is polar, so dissolves in water and travels in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solubility of amino acids determined by side chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids are transported in an ionised state    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic **OR** water-insoluble substances can not travel in solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is non-polar **OR** has limited solubility in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ majority of oxygen binds to haemoglobin **OR** is transported by red blood cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol **OR** fats **OR** lipids **OR** triglycerides are non-polar **OR** hydrophobic and bind to proteins **OR** form lipoproteins to become soluble        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The **transport** of molecules in the blood is **determined by** their **solubility** in water ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrophilic** substances, such as **glucose** ($\\checkmark$) and **ionised amino acids**  ($\\checkmark$), **dissolve** easily **in plasma** and **travel freely** in **solution** ($\\checkmark$). **Ionic compounds** like **sodium chloride dissociate** and are carried as **free ions**, such as **Na$^+$ and Cl$^-$** ($\\checkmark$), in the **plasma** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, **non-polar substances** like **oxygen** and **lipids** are **not soluble** in water. Only a **small proportion** of **oxygen** is **dissolved** in the **plasma**; it is **primarily transported** by binding to **haemoglobin**($\\checkmark$)  in **red blood cells** ($\\checkmark$). **Lipids**, including **cholesterol** and **triglycerides**, **bind to proteins** to form **lipoproteins**, which makes them **soluble** and allows their transport in the bloodstream ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1810","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1810\n\nWhich of the following are a reason why water's properties would have influenced early cellular evolution in aquatic environments?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | High surface tension allowing formation of proto-cell membranes. |\n    | II. | Polar solvent properties enabling interaction of biomolecules. |\n    | III. | High density causing rapid sedimentation of organic material. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater's ability to dissolve biomolecules and, therefore, enabling them to interact (**Statement II**) would have been crucial to early cellular evolution. This property allows for the formation of a \"primordial soup,\" where simple organic molecules could interact, form more complex compounds, and eventually lead to the development of proto-cells. **Choice B** is the correct answer to this question.\n\nWhile surface tension is a property of water, it does not directly contribute to the formation of proto-cell membranes (**Statement I**) in the context of early cellular evolution. Proto-cell membranes are primarily formed by the self-assembly of amphipathic molecules like phospholipids, which align themselves in a bilayer in water due to their hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions.\n\nOrganic molecules needed to remain in suspension for interactions, rather than settling quickly (**Statement III**). Water's density is well-suited for suspending organic particles, allowing them to remain available for biochemical reactions, while enabling gradual sedimentation over time.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more untrue statements."},{"ref":"BI1811","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1811\n\nWhy is water considered a \"universal solvent,\" and why is this important for the origin of life?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | --: | :-- |\n    | I. | $\\hspace{1em}$ It dissolves a wider variety of substances than any other liquid. |\n    | II. | $\\hspace{1em}$ It can dissolve both polar and non-polar molecules. |\n    | III. | $\\hspace{1em}$ It enables complex biochemical reactions by facilitating the interaction of reactants. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is known as the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve a wider range of substances, than any other liquid (**Statement I**). This property is due to water's polarity and ability to form hydrogen bonds with various molecules. Water’s ability to dissolve various substances means that it can bring reactants together in a solution, enabling the complex biochemical reactions that are necessary for life (**Statement III**). **Choice C** is correct.\n\nWater is a polar solvent, which means it is very effective at dissolving other polar substances and ionic compounds. However, water is not good at dissolving non-polar molecules because there is no attraction between water molecules and non-polar molecules. **Statement II** is therefore not true.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI2318","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2318\n\nWhich substance is insoluble in water and suitable for energy storage in animals?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Starch\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycogen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is the correct answer because glycogen is the primary carbohydrate used for short- to medium-term energy storage in animals. It is insoluble in water, so it does not affect the water potential of the cytoplasm, making it ideal for storage.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because starch is the carbohydrate storage molecule in plants, not animals. While starch is insoluble and suitable for energy storage in plants, it is not used by animals.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because glucose is hydrophilic, soluble in water, and used for immediate energy, not long-term storage.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ribose is hydrophilic and is not used for energy storage. Instead, it is a component of RNA and nucleotides like ATP."},{"ref":"BI2865","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2865\n\nThe diagram below shows a water column inside a xylem vessel:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20SL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI2865%20A1.1%20core%20MCQ.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2865 A1.1 core MCQ.jpeg\">\n[Sources: Adapted from Chan, T (26 May 2025), *File: Capillary Action in Plants [image]*. Retrieved 5 June 2025 from https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Capillary_Action_in_Plants.jpg. CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following best explains how the property illustrated contributes to plant survival?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Cohesion between molecules allows tension from transpiration to be transmitted up the xylem. |\n    | II. | Cohesion enables    water to evaporate more easily. |\n    | III. | Adhesion prevents the water column from breaking. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because cohesion refers to the attraction between water molecules due to hydrogen bonding. This cohesive property allows the water column to behave as a continuous stream, enabling tension created by transpiration at the leaves to be transmitted all the way down the xylem. This is central to the cohesion-tension theory of water transport.\\\n**Statement II** is false because cohesion actually resists evaporation. The hydrogen bonds between water molecules make it harder for individual molecules to escape into the air. Evaporation is more closely influenced by temperature and humidity, not directly by cohesion.\\\n**Statement III** is true because adhesion is the attraction between water molecules and the walls of the xylem vessel. This helps to counteract gravity and supports the column, reducing the chance of it breaking, especially under tension during transpiration. So this contributes to the integrity of the water column.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore the correct answer because it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **III**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is missing the true **Statement III**.\\\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement II**."}]}]}]};
